WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.580
Andrew Huberman: [Music Playing] Welcome to the Huberman Lab

00:00:01.580 --> 00:00:05.685
Guest Series, where I and an expert
guest discuss  science and science

00:00:05.685 --> 00:00:06.925
based tools for everyday life.

00:00:08.514 --> 00:00:12.224
I'm Andrew Huberman, and I'm a Professor
of Neurobiology and Ophthalmology

00:00:12.465 --> 00:00:13.854
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:14.354 --> 00:00:17.145
Today marks the third episode
in our four episode series

00:00:17.174 --> 00:00:18.695
about mental health with Dr.

00:00:18.695 --> 00:00:19.525
Paul Conti.

00:00:19.964 --> 00:00:23.894
Today's episode deals with the topic
of healthy relationships, how to

00:00:23.894 --> 00:00:27.305
define what a healthy relationship
is, and how to achieve healthy

00:00:27.305 --> 00:00:31.034
relationships of all kinds, including
romantic relationships, interpersonal

00:00:31.034 --> 00:00:35.294
relationships at work, friendships with
family, and of course, with oneself.

00:00:35.534 --> 00:00:38.714
This episode builds on the framework
of the psychology of self and mental

00:00:38.714 --> 00:00:42.125
health that was established in the first
and second episodes of this series.

00:00:42.195 --> 00:00:45.004
However, even if you didn't listen to
the first or second episode in this

00:00:45.004 --> 00:00:48.555
series, today's episode will still
contain a lot of information and

00:00:48.555 --> 00:00:51.754
protocols that you will find valuable
for improving your relationships.

00:00:51.925 --> 00:00:55.415
That said, if you have the opportunity to
listen to the first and second episodes in

00:00:55.415 --> 00:00:59.295
this series, I think you'll find those to
be tremendously beneficial at any point.

00:00:59.405 --> 00:01:00.714
During today's episode, Dr.

00:01:00.714 --> 00:01:03.985
Conti discusses what makes for
a successful relationship of

00:01:03.985 --> 00:01:07.015
any kind, as well as tools to
improve those relationships.

00:01:07.065 --> 00:01:10.585
He discusses various types of bonds,
including healthy bonds and trauma

00:01:10.585 --> 00:01:13.885
bonds, not just in the context of
romantic relationship, but in the

00:01:13.885 --> 00:01:15.855
context of all types of relationships.

00:01:15.945 --> 00:01:18.735
We also discuss different challenges
that people face in relationships,

00:01:18.735 --> 00:01:22.705
including abusive relationships, and
we discuss the role of power dynamics,

00:01:22.744 --> 00:01:27.265
anxiety, and boundaries in relationships,
both from the perspective of unhealthy

00:01:27.265 --> 00:01:30.875
relationships, but more importantly
from the understanding and protocols

00:01:30.875 --> 00:01:32.785
to cultivate healthy relationships.

00:01:32.885 --> 00:01:36.175
While there is an abundance of opinions
and information out there on the Internet

00:01:36.185 --> 00:01:40.454
these days about relationships both
healthy and unhealthy, today's discussion

00:01:40.454 --> 00:01:44.375
approaches the topic of relationships
through an entirely different lens, which

00:01:44.375 --> 00:01:49.905
is the lens of the self in terms of one's
conscious and subconscious mind, and how

00:01:49.915 --> 00:01:54.575
multiple conscious and subconscious minds
through different individuals, interact

00:01:54.575 --> 00:01:58.217
with one another in ways that we can see
and ways that we can't see, and all of

00:01:58.217 --> 00:02:02.165
that framed within the actionable steps
that any of us can take to improve our

00:02:02.165 --> 00:02:04.225
relationship to ourself and to others.

00:02:04.585 --> 00:02:07.905
Before we begin, I'd like to emphasize
that this podcast is separate from my

00:02:07.905 --> 00:02:09.525
teaching and research roles at Stanford.

00:02:09.845 --> 00:02:13.155
It is, however, part of my desire and
effort to bring zero cost to consumer

00:02:13.155 --> 00:02:16.774
information about science and science
related tools to the general public.

00:02:17.075 --> 00:02:20.415
In keeping with that theme, I'd like to
thank the sponsors of today's podcast.

