00:04:58.055 --> 00:05:00.555
And now for my discussion
about mental health with Dr.

00:05:00.555 --> 00:05:01.504
Paul Conti.

00:05:02.074 --> 00:05:02.405
Dr.

00:05:02.405 --> 00:05:03.464
Conti, welcome back.

00:05:03.574 --> 00:05:04.074
Paul Conti: Thank you.

00:05:04.094 --> 00:05:04.944
Happy to be here.

00:05:05.795 --> 00:05:09.305
Andrew Huberman: Today we're going to
discuss relationships, and that will

00:05:09.795 --> 00:05:14.894
often focus on romantic relationships,
but also relationships between friends,

00:05:14.895 --> 00:05:19.765
between family members, and inevitably,
the relationship to self, which is what

00:05:19.835 --> 00:05:23.744
we really focused on in episodes one
and two of this series, episode one

00:05:23.745 --> 00:05:29.075
being all the things that go into a
healthy self and how to understand what

00:05:29.075 --> 00:05:33.954
is unhealthy and healthy in all of us
and make adjustments to the unhealthy

00:05:33.954 --> 00:05:39.605
aspects of our unconscious and conscious
through really specific proactive

00:05:40.255 --> 00:05:42.085
behaviors and patterns of thought.

00:05:42.085 --> 00:05:45.385
So really a roadmap to these
ideals that we call mental health

00:05:45.385 --> 00:05:46.625
and understanding of the self.

00:05:47.645 --> 00:05:51.414
Today we want to talk about
relationships from the perspective

00:05:51.414 --> 00:05:53.224
of, of course, how people relate.

00:05:53.744 --> 00:05:58.415
But I have a feeling it's going to have
something to do with, or perhaps almost

00:05:58.415 --> 00:06:02.105
everything to do with our relationship,
to understanding ourselves first.

00:06:02.425 --> 00:06:02.925
Paul Conti: Yes.

00:06:03.155 --> 00:06:05.305
Andrew Huberman: So, just to make
sure that we're all on the same page,

00:06:05.395 --> 00:06:08.854
regardless of whether or not people
have seen episodes one and two, and

00:06:08.855 --> 00:06:13.034
certainly people do not need to have
seen or listened to episodes one and two

00:06:13.355 --> 00:06:17.365
of this series in order to understand
today's discussion, could you please

00:06:17.365 --> 00:06:20.395
tell us what is a healthy person?

00:06:20.615 --> 00:06:25.015
And how can we ask ourselves the sorts
of questions that allow us to determine

00:06:25.015 --> 00:06:30.055
whether or not we are healthy and where
to look in terms of making adjustments

00:06:30.055 --> 00:06:33.005
if we want to be healthier than we
already are, which I have to believe

00:06:33.395 --> 00:06:36.955
almost everybody, if not everybody,
certainly wants to be the healthiest

00:06:36.955 --> 00:06:40.905
and best expression of themselves
so that they can do the most for

00:06:40.905 --> 00:06:42.375
themselves and for others in the world?

00:06:42.535 --> 00:06:49.025
Paul Conti: So, the linchpin of it all,
is the agency and gratitude as verbs.

00:06:49.355 --> 00:06:50.655
That's the top of the mountain.

00:06:50.965 --> 00:06:54.275
There's a lot of climbing we do
to get to the top of the mountain.

00:06:54.354 --> 00:06:58.205
Once we're over the top of the mountain,
then things are in a better place.

00:06:58.495 --> 00:07:02.030
And even though there are two words,
agency is, of course, a word, g

00:07:02.045 --> 00:07:05.934
ratitude is a word, b ut it doesn't
mean that they're separate concepts.

00:07:06.175 --> 00:07:10.565
Approaching the world through the lens
of agency and gratitude, thought of as

00:07:10.795 --> 00:07:15.715
one thing because they come together
and they come together as verbs.

00:07:16.174 --> 00:07:21.585
That's what we're aiming for, because
that's the thing that we can work towards.

00:07:21.585 --> 00:07:26.034
If you think about what comes
underneath that, it takes us back to

00:07:26.034 --> 00:07:29.534
the two pillars and the 10 cupboards.

00:07:29.534 --> 00:07:33.685
And if we're looking in there enough,
we're mInding, like what is in my

00:07:33.685 --> 00:07:36.185
unconscious mind I might not be aware of.

00:07:36.185 --> 00:07:39.489
Let me generate some curiosity
about my defense mechanisms and

00:07:39.715 --> 00:07:42.814
my character structure and think
about what's salient inside of me.

00:07:42.885 --> 00:07:46.455
If we're doing all of that, then
what we're doing, we're building

00:07:46.465 --> 00:07:48.304
empowerment, we're building humility.

00:07:48.545 --> 00:07:52.815
And then ultimately, the expression
of all of that is the agency and

00:07:52.815 --> 00:07:56.485
gratitude, and it's something
that we can't have enough of.

00:07:56.494 --> 00:07:58.514
Like I say, what's the
best amount of that?

00:07:58.735 --> 00:08:00.705
The most that's possible, right?

00:08:00.905 --> 00:08:04.684
Because we're going to engage in
the world in the healthiest way.

00:08:04.694 --> 00:08:08.715
Because agency and gratitude doesn't mean
like, we're happy about everything, right?

00:08:08.715 --> 00:08:12.435
If there's something negative
and we can change that, then

00:08:12.535 --> 00:08:13.795
we don't feel happy about that.

00:08:14.575 --> 00:08:18.675
The sense of gratitude in me
makes me more likely to feel

00:08:18.675 --> 00:08:20.135
that I can change that, right?

00:08:20.135 --> 00:08:22.795
Or to take the chance of
trying to change that.

00:08:22.795 --> 00:08:26.065
And then the agency part of that
concept can come more to the

00:08:26.065 --> 00:08:27.615
fore and I can make the change.

00:08:27.775 --> 00:08:32.705
So it's not about bliss, it's not
about forgiving self and others

00:08:32.715 --> 00:08:34.945
for all sorts of things and not
working to make things better.

00:08:35.335 --> 00:08:39.434
It's about being in the world and
being as aware as I can possibly

00:08:39.434 --> 00:08:42.784
be, including being aware that
there are things I'm not aware of.

00:08:43.334 --> 00:08:48.704
So sort of having a healthy respect and an
orientation to the world that values truth

00:08:48.704 --> 00:08:50.984
and values understanding and exploration.

00:08:51.244 --> 00:08:54.584
If we do all of that, then we
reach the top of the mountain

00:08:54.604 --> 00:08:56.134
through the agency and gratitude.

00:08:56.435 --> 00:09:02.135
And then what builds upon that or
what comes from that is the peace,

00:09:02.145 --> 00:09:05.865
the contentment, the delight, the
generative drive being strengthened

00:09:05.865 --> 00:09:08.144
like that all comes together.

00:09:08.145 --> 00:09:10.974
And then the aggressive, or we
say aggressive because that's

00:09:10.974 --> 00:09:13.364
the traditional term, b ut the
aggressive or the assertive.

00:09:13.714 --> 00:09:17.824
The assertiveness or assertion drive,
however, whatever word we want to put

00:09:17.824 --> 00:09:23.445
to that, that drive exists in us in a
way that subserves the generative drive.

00:09:23.685 --> 00:09:25.785
The same thing with the
pleasure drive in us.

00:09:25.785 --> 00:09:29.365
It exists in us, but it's
subserving the generative drive.

00:09:29.365 --> 00:09:32.775
And then all those good things come
together and they come together

00:09:32.775 --> 00:09:36.935
to help us be as healthy as we can
and to stay healthy, including when

00:09:36.954 --> 00:09:42.064
tribulation or difficulties come our
way, so that we stay as best we can

00:09:42.265 --> 00:09:45.134
in the agency and gratitude as verbs.

00:09:45.135 --> 00:09:47.994
And again, there's nothing
theoretical about this.

00:09:48.214 --> 00:09:53.785
It's a way to live, and there are a lot
of people who live some of their lives,

00:09:53.785 --> 00:09:55.775
or parts of their lives through that.

00:09:55.775 --> 00:10:00.064
And we can aspire to it and we can work
towards it, and if we're the best that

00:10:00.064 --> 00:10:04.645
we can be, then we're going to be in
our relationships the best we can be.

00:10:04.645 --> 00:10:07.384
Think about it, like, you and I
have a relationship, we know each

00:10:07.385 --> 00:10:08.604
other, w e're working together.

00:10:08.775 --> 00:10:12.495
If I can bring my best self to
you, to thinking about you, to

00:10:12.495 --> 00:10:16.325
understanding you, if I can bring
the agency and the gratitude,

00:10:16.614 --> 00:10:18.694
then I'm going to do right by you.

00:10:18.694 --> 00:10:21.545
I'm going to mentalize this
idea of thinking about what's

00:10:21.545 --> 00:10:22.614
going on inside of you.

00:10:22.805 --> 00:10:24.514
I'm going to bring the
best o f all of that.

00:10:24.805 --> 00:10:28.384
And then, because we have a
relationship, t here's also an "us."

00:10:28.655 --> 00:10:32.154
There's a me, there's a you, there's
something that happens between us.

00:10:32.165 --> 00:10:34.384
That's the relationship, that's the "us."

00:10:34.614 --> 00:10:39.095
And I will also bring my best self
to that thing that is no longer just

00:10:39.095 --> 00:10:41.765
me or just you, but that is an us.

00:10:41.795 --> 00:10:44.474
And that applies to all relationships.

00:10:44.755 --> 00:10:50.694
You can apply that to seeking health in
every single relationship in our lives.

00:10:51.604 --> 00:10:53.604
Andrew Huberman: What you're saying
really speaks to the importance of

00:10:53.614 --> 00:11:00.144
people taking a real look at themselves,
which is not necessarily an inventory

00:11:00.145 --> 00:11:04.874
of self in the typical sense that we're
used to hearing it, but rather through

00:11:04.874 --> 00:11:09.205
this lens or map of the self that was
spelled out in episodes one and two.

00:11:09.235 --> 00:11:13.525
And again, for those that are
just joining the series now, the

00:11:13.564 --> 00:11:17.674
map, as we're referring to it,
is available as a downloadable

00:11:17.674 --> 00:11:19.374
PDF in the show note captions.

00:11:19.675 --> 00:11:21.745
If people want to get that,
it's completely zero cost.

00:11:21.745 --> 00:11:24.144
You can just go there and access
it, or just view it on a screen,

00:11:24.145 --> 00:11:25.824
print it out, whatever you like.

00:11:26.474 --> 00:11:31.085
You certainly don't need to do that,
because here again, we're talking about

00:11:31.085 --> 00:11:36.655
the core elements of that map, which,
if I understand correctly, arrive

00:11:36.664 --> 00:11:41.705
eventually at a set of verb states that
we're calling agency and gratitude.

00:11:41.854 --> 00:11:45.314
Those are not separate, as you
pointed out, they work together.

00:11:45.964 --> 00:11:48.705
Paul Conti: You described it as
on top of the geyser, r ight?

00:11:48.854 --> 00:11:52.591
And I really like that there's a
lot of things going on, but then

00:11:52.784 --> 00:11:55.385
it all uplifts to something, right?

00:11:55.385 --> 00:11:59.254
And if we're doing it in the right way,
in the diligent way, then i t is like

00:11:59.255 --> 00:12:03.505
a geyser, a nd what it's lifting up on
top of it is the agency and gratitude.

00:12:03.505 --> 00:12:05.685
I love that you put those
words to i t, and I think it

00:12:05.685 --> 00:12:07.774
captures it really profoundly.

00:12:08.545 --> 00:12:11.604
Andrew Huberman: Well, the stuff that
geysers up perhaps deserves a bit of

00:12:11.604 --> 00:12:14.104
our attention just for a few moments.

00:12:15.425 --> 00:12:20.535
You describe these two pillars
that I described as geysering up

00:12:20.675 --> 00:12:25.034
into agency and gratitude in the
best circumstances, but in the best

00:12:25.035 --> 00:12:29.034
circumstances, we want to remind
ourselves and everybody, are attainable

00:12:29.295 --> 00:12:31.285
by looking at what's in those pillars.

00:12:32.295 --> 00:12:34.194
Those two pillars are
the structure of self.

00:12:34.194 --> 00:12:36.655
So really understanding something
about the structure of self.

00:12:36.964 --> 00:12:41.064
So that includes some understanding of
the unconscious mind, some understanding

00:12:41.064 --> 00:12:46.174
of the conscious mind, defense
mechanisms, character structure and self.

00:12:46.275 --> 00:12:50.944
That was all reviewed and described
in episodes one and two, as well

00:12:50.944 --> 00:12:53.954
as the functions of self, which
are more of the verb states, the

00:12:53.954 --> 00:12:56.194
expressions of the structure of self.

00:12:57.045 --> 00:13:01.384
Self-awareness being the first of
those defense mechanisms in action.

00:13:01.494 --> 00:13:06.574
And here I'd be remiss if I didn't tell
myself and everybody else again that

00:13:06.574 --> 00:13:08.525
defense mechanisms are not always bad.

00:13:08.624 --> 00:13:09.925
There can be healthy defense mechanisms.

00:13:09.925 --> 00:13:10.454
Paul Conti: Absolutely.

00:13:10.635 --> 00:13:12.894
Andrew Huberman: They can
protect us in very valuable ways.

00:13:13.444 --> 00:13:17.375
And then this notion of salience,
what we pay attention to, what

00:13:17.375 --> 00:13:20.244
sorts of scripts are going on in
our head about ourselves and others,

00:13:20.244 --> 00:13:21.384
what are we paying attention to?

00:13:21.395 --> 00:13:24.854
How are we interpreting those in our
head and to others, and then most

00:13:24.854 --> 00:13:26.305
importantly, perhaps to ourselves?

00:13:26.645 --> 00:13:29.915
And then our actual behaviors, like what
we are doing from the time we wake up

00:13:29.915 --> 00:13:33.925
until the time we go to sleep at night,
and then our strivings, our sense of

00:13:34.084 --> 00:13:37.274
hopefulness, or perhaps lack of strivings.

00:13:37.584 --> 00:13:43.014
And there I am reminded also
that nobody does all of these

00:13:43.015 --> 00:13:44.664
things perfectly all the time.

00:13:44.664 --> 00:13:49.755
We all have elements within us that
perhaps are not serving to geyser up

00:13:49.755 --> 00:13:54.405
into agency and gratitude as well as
they could, but that all of us have the

00:13:54.405 --> 00:14:00.014
capacity to look at these two pillars
and these 10 things that were just listed

00:14:00.015 --> 00:14:03.344
off, as you describe them, as cupboards
that we look into and examine and think

00:14:03.474 --> 00:14:08.415
about, and that if we can do that with a
skilled clinician like yourself or another

00:14:08.415 --> 00:14:10.385
psychiatrist or psychologist, terrific.

00:14:10.645 --> 00:14:14.124
But even if we don't have access
to that, we can examine within

00:14:14.124 --> 00:14:20.135
us what is and is not serving to
geyser up into agency and gratitude.

00:14:20.494 --> 00:14:25.525
Paul Conti: We can bring any issue
of self to those two pillars and

00:14:25.525 --> 00:14:26.874
the 10 cupboards within them.

00:14:27.185 --> 00:14:28.165
Any issue of self.

00:14:28.185 --> 00:14:28.525
Why?

00:14:28.525 --> 00:14:34.295
Because that's what it is, in
the s ense that it is the "us."

00:14:34.324 --> 00:14:35.314
What's inside of me.

00:14:35.314 --> 00:14:38.116
I have an unconscious mind, I have a
conscious mind, I have a defensive s

00:14:38.116 --> 00:14:42.324
tructure, I have a character structure,
I h ave a self, that's what it is.

00:14:42.324 --> 00:14:46.024
And all the functions that you
mentioned, they're all going on

00:14:46.334 --> 00:14:49.074
within that structure of self.

00:14:49.074 --> 00:14:50.454
They're manifesting themselves.

00:14:50.834 --> 00:14:56.534
That's what it is w hen we're being,
in the active way of the verb being.

00:14:56.704 --> 00:15:00.264
That's what it is, which is why
w e can bring to it any issue

00:15:00.264 --> 00:15:03.935
of self, even though, of course,
there's tremendous complexity there.

00:15:04.374 --> 00:15:08.045
The million things that go on in a
second underneath the surface in the

00:15:08.045 --> 00:15:11.285
unconscious mind and our defensive
structures and how to see them.

00:15:11.555 --> 00:15:12.794
But we're human beings.

00:15:12.795 --> 00:15:14.055
We're complicated, right?

00:15:14.255 --> 00:15:18.334
That's okay, because we have methods
of understanding, we have methods

00:15:18.334 --> 00:15:23.055
of inquiry, and we can use those
methods to make things better.

00:15:23.895 --> 00:15:26.285
And that's how we make the
geyser as strong as it can be.

00:15:26.285 --> 00:15:31.194
Where maybe some of the water is
running countercurrent in the...

00:15:31.194 --> 00:15:33.625
okay, well, let's try and have
things go all in the same direction.

00:15:33.625 --> 00:15:40.275
But we don't need things to be perfect
for the geyser to spring up and the agency

00:15:40.275 --> 00:15:43.115
and gratitude to then be uplifted upon it.

00:15:44.704 --> 00:15:47.634
Andrew Huberman: So everybody has
one of these maps, as I understand

00:15:47.635 --> 00:15:52.285
it, and therefore anytime we're
talking about relationships, romantic

00:15:52.344 --> 00:15:56.285
relationships or otherwise, we're
talking about the intersection or

00:15:56.285 --> 00:16:00.224
overlap of those maps in some way,
maybe even the synergy and the outgrowth

00:16:00.224 --> 00:16:02.185
of those maps becomes its own map.

00:16:02.214 --> 00:16:03.735
I think we'll get to
this a little bit later.

00:16:03.784 --> 00:16:04.314
Paul Conti: Yes.

00:16:05.484 --> 00:16:09.404
Andrew Huberman: When I and most
people hear the word relationships, in

00:16:09.404 --> 00:16:13.895
particular romantic relationships, I
make a couple of automatic assumptions.

00:16:14.214 --> 00:16:17.845
First of all, I have to assume
that people are either in a

00:16:17.845 --> 00:16:19.765
relationship or out of a relationship.

00:16:20.444 --> 00:16:23.884
There's probably a third category out
there of plurals and other things,

00:16:23.885 --> 00:16:25.314
but we'll keep it relatively simple.

00:16:26.734 --> 00:16:32.595
And most people, I assume, when they
search for or enter, or entered a

00:16:32.595 --> 00:16:37.775
relationship, they thought about whether
or not they had resonance with the

00:16:37.775 --> 00:16:43.545
person, whether or not there was an
intellectual or mutual interest resonance

00:16:43.545 --> 00:16:50.245
or a physical resonance, maybe something
about family history, common goals, etc.

00:16:51.474 --> 00:16:53.564
That's typically what people think about.

00:16:53.864 --> 00:16:58.515
And then perhaps if people will have a bit
more of a psychological understanding, or

00:16:59.255 --> 00:17:03.634
they're reading some pop psychology books
these days, some of which are pretty good,

00:17:04.434 --> 00:17:08.185
they might understand something about
themselves or the other person being a

00:17:08.185 --> 00:17:09.875
bit more anxious or a bit more relaxed.

00:17:09.875 --> 00:17:12.224
So you'll hear things like
anxious, attached, secure,

00:17:12.225 --> 00:17:14.015
attached, things of that sort.

00:17:14.465 --> 00:17:18.915
If we could just step back from all
of that for a moment and examine it

00:17:18.915 --> 00:17:23.025
through the lens of the maps as you're
describing them, which exist in all of us.

00:17:23.025 --> 00:17:26.944
And that really are the map to
being the best version of ourselves.

00:17:28.885 --> 00:17:33.735
If we look at relationships in terms
of lists of where people went to

00:17:33.735 --> 00:17:36.724
school, if they went to school, are
their parents married or divorced?

00:17:37.025 --> 00:17:41.335
Do they have trauma that they're aware of
or not aware of these kinds of things, I

00:17:41.335 --> 00:17:46.135
guess we could call those, and here I'm
borrowing language that you used earlier

00:17:46.355 --> 00:17:48.525
off-camera, so I want to acknowledge that.

00:17:49.415 --> 00:17:53.074
Points of compatibility,
I think that seems like a

00:17:53.075 --> 00:17:54.725
reasonable place to start, right?

00:17:55.255 --> 00:17:56.635
Do people want the same thing?

00:17:56.635 --> 00:18:00.804
So if you could, could you talk a
little bit about points of compatibility

00:18:00.804 --> 00:18:05.285
in a relationship and how those
show up, for better or for worse?

00:18:05.585 --> 00:18:09.135
When you encounter people in the clinical
setting, when you see somebody who's in

00:18:09.135 --> 00:18:13.045
a relationship that's really working for
them and is healthy versus if they're in a

00:18:13.045 --> 00:18:17.345
relationship where it's really unhealthy,
presumably there's some knowledge about

00:18:17.345 --> 00:18:20.495
points of compatibility, but I'm guessing
that's not always intuitive, right?

00:18:22.155 --> 00:18:26.135
Paul Conti: I think the place we
start there is acknowledging what

00:18:26.135 --> 00:18:28.535
we can know and what we can't know.

00:18:29.345 --> 00:18:35.055
So this idea that there are levels o
f emergence where things at a lower

00:18:35.055 --> 00:18:39.525
level come together and create something
that's new, we see this throughout

00:18:39.535 --> 00:18:42.865
science, from subatomic particles
all the way through to culture.

00:18:43.325 --> 00:18:47.845
So someone could know theoretically,
lots and lots and lots about you

00:18:48.135 --> 00:18:54.065
and lots and lots and lots about me,
but they don't know about us, right?

00:18:54.065 --> 00:18:55.494
They don't know how we interact.

00:18:55.525 --> 00:18:58.005
They don't know if we have shared
interests, what we talk about.

00:18:58.825 --> 00:18:59.605
They don't know that.

00:18:59.615 --> 00:19:00.605
No one knows that.

00:19:00.805 --> 00:19:05.434
We don't know that about
the combination of people.

00:19:05.435 --> 00:19:10.444
We can't know it in advance, but
we often believe that we can, which

00:19:10.454 --> 00:19:15.405
then leads to a lot of false metrics
of trying to figure things out.

00:19:15.415 --> 00:19:19.445
So when we're looking for compatibility,
the thought will be just very basic,

00:19:19.455 --> 00:19:23.365
tangible things, like in romance,
for example, if there's someone who

00:19:23.445 --> 00:19:26.615
absolutely wants to have a family
and there's someone who absolutely

00:19:26.645 --> 00:19:30.025
does not want to have a family, okay,
that would be a reason for those two

00:19:30.025 --> 00:19:31.855
people to not choose one another.

00:19:33.245 --> 00:19:36.455
So there are these factors, but
the factors are all, in a sense,

00:19:36.455 --> 00:19:38.275
very evident and very concrete.

00:19:41.045 --> 00:19:44.275
If we go beyond that, we say,
okay, we can see the obvious.

00:19:44.985 --> 00:19:49.605
Then what we're looking for is really
a compatibility of generative drives.

00:19:50.435 --> 00:19:53.845
And then that tells you, can
these people then get along?

00:19:54.295 --> 00:19:57.044
Maybe one of them is from one s ide of
the world and the other i s from the

00:19:57.045 --> 00:20:00.745
other side of the world, or one's an
accountant and the other is a musician.

00:20:00.745 --> 00:20:06.675
And it doesn't mean that they're built not
to get along or that if they're doing the

00:20:06.675 --> 00:20:11.655
same thing they're built to get along, or
if they went to college in the same place,

00:20:11.655 --> 00:20:17.865
or they went to college versus not going
to college, there's so much there that we

00:20:17.925 --> 00:20:22.684
try to build a story on, and then what we
do is we miss the forest for the trees.

00:20:23.264 --> 00:20:26.395
And the trees, I think, are
the factors that don't matter.

00:20:26.405 --> 00:20:28.425
So again, let's think about
the factors that do matter.

00:20:28.485 --> 00:20:30.485
Person wants to have a family,
the other person doesn't.

00:20:30.875 --> 00:20:31.735
That's relevant.

00:20:32.095 --> 00:20:36.595
But the trees that mislead us might be,
do they have the same level of education?

00:20:37.395 --> 00:20:39.325
Do they have the same
family structure growing up?

00:20:39.364 --> 00:20:40.665
Are parents still together?

00:20:41.274 --> 00:20:42.065
What do they enjoy?

00:20:42.065 --> 00:20:45.105
Are they creative or
scientific or whatever?

00:20:45.105 --> 00:20:46.365
Or creatively scientific.

00:20:46.365 --> 00:20:49.865
We could look at all of that,
but I think then we're making a

00:20:49.865 --> 00:20:51.625
bunch of trees that mislead us.

00:20:51.855 --> 00:20:57.335
If you say, look, do these people come
at the world through how much agency

00:20:57.335 --> 00:20:59.755
and gratitude is there guiding them?

00:20:59.755 --> 00:21:03.415
How high is the top of that geyser?
How high is it uplifting that?

00:21:03.415 --> 00:21:05.155
How strong is the generative drive?

00:21:05.385 --> 00:21:08.855
If we match people upon that,
then we see, oh, those people got

00:21:08.855 --> 00:21:10.034
along and those people didn't.

00:21:10.034 --> 00:21:13.965
And then there are all the other factors,
like pheromones, a nd things that we can't

00:21:13.965 --> 00:21:16.375
understand or reflexive first impressions.

00:21:16.375 --> 00:21:21.295
We could let things develop, honoring
the truth of what we can't know.

00:21:21.295 --> 00:21:24.465
That when you put two people
together, you get something that's

00:21:24.465 --> 00:21:27.975
different from the sum of both people.

00:21:28.425 --> 00:21:33.535
It's not an overlap of maps, i t's a
new map, a nd the map is informed by

00:21:33.545 --> 00:21:35.905
things that are on each individual's map.

00:21:36.184 --> 00:21:44.345
But what we're looking for are maps that
don't have very significant differences

00:21:44.414 --> 00:21:46.984
around just clear, concrete things.

00:21:47.635 --> 00:21:53.024
But once we get beyond that, the maps can
synergize in all sorts of beautiful and

00:21:53.075 --> 00:21:59.845
unpredictable ways, and if both are coming
from the perspective of a generative

00:21:59.855 --> 00:22:02.595
drive that is at the forefront, great.

00:22:02.605 --> 00:22:04.985
You put those maps together
and see what happens.

00:22:04.985 --> 00:22:06.015
Maybe nothing happens.

00:22:06.065 --> 00:22:06.365
Okay.

00:22:06.365 --> 00:22:08.005
Those people don't go
out on a second date.

00:22:08.505 --> 00:22:11.335
Maybe something happens, but it
doesn't develop in certain ways.

00:22:11.335 --> 00:22:13.085
Okay, those people dated
and stopped dating.

00:22:13.085 --> 00:22:14.715
Like, this happens all the time, r ight?

00:22:14.895 --> 00:22:19.795
But we set the odds in our favor that
the generative drive in each that is

00:22:19.835 --> 00:22:24.125
at the forefront means that their maps
can synergize in ways that can then be

00:22:24.125 --> 00:22:29.824
beautiful and ways that can maybe bring
to both people that which they want.

00:22:29.825 --> 00:22:35.385
And I think there's a simplicity
to that, that I think if we honor

00:22:35.615 --> 00:22:37.525
it can be very, very helpful.

00:22:37.525 --> 00:22:41.065
Whether it's romance, it's friendships,
of looking at what the truth of it

00:22:41.065 --> 00:22:45.874
is instead of the factors that we try
and gain a false sense of security

00:22:45.895 --> 00:22:48.284
if we're using them to select upon.

00:22:48.684 --> 00:22:52.275
Andrew Huberman: You mentioned several
times the generative drive, and I

00:22:52.465 --> 00:22:56.035
definitely would like to learn more
about that and spend some time there

00:22:56.035 --> 00:22:57.774
in the context of relationships.

00:22:58.414 --> 00:23:01.755
But just to drill a little bit
further into what we've all heard

00:23:01.755 --> 00:23:06.474
so often, and you touched upon a
few of these themes around points of

00:23:06.475 --> 00:23:13.775
compatibility, but also where sometimes
we can respond to the wrong things

00:23:13.905 --> 00:23:15.334
when thinking about compatibility.

00:23:15.355 --> 00:23:18.924
Again, we're framing this mainly
in the context of hypothetical

00:23:18.925 --> 00:23:22.585
romantic relationships, but certainly
it pertains to other sorts of

00:23:22.585 --> 00:23:29.397
relationships, things like, is one
person educated with an advanced

00:23:29.397 --> 00:23:30.764
degree, and is the other person also?

00:23:31.165 --> 00:23:34.735
We tend to assume that people
who are, are somehow a better

00:23:34.735 --> 00:23:35.915
match than people who aren't.

00:23:35.915 --> 00:23:39.585
That's sort of an implicit assumption
that's often made, not always.

00:23:39.914 --> 00:23:45.535
Or that if two people really love music,
that they will enjoy music together,

00:23:45.605 --> 00:23:49.515
and therefore the sum of the whole
is greater than either of its parts.

00:23:49.995 --> 00:23:52.585
Paul Conti: And I think those
things are utterly irrelevant.

00:23:52.655 --> 00:23:55.024
Andrew Huberman: Interesting, and I want
to hear more about this because I think

00:23:55.034 --> 00:23:59.695
that if you think about, which I'm sure
you don't, but, like dating apps, for

00:23:59.695 --> 00:24:05.495
instance, like what's listed out there
or first or second dates, which consists

00:24:05.515 --> 00:24:08.655
of learning a little bit about somebody
and what they're doing and maybe even a

00:24:08.655 --> 00:24:15.275
little bit about their history and maybe
an activity and certainly an intentional

00:24:15.275 --> 00:24:18.414
awareness to how the other person is
behaving in the context of different

00:24:18.415 --> 00:24:22.805
things, like a waiter or a waitress and
how they're treating people, and you.

00:24:22.805 --> 00:24:24.825
And then you hear about things like...

00:24:24.825 --> 00:24:27.995
this is not something I'm familiar with...

00:24:28.125 --> 00:24:30.774
oh, right, the love languages.

00:24:30.795 --> 00:24:32.685
People are like, oh,
what's their love language?

00:24:33.135 --> 00:24:40.355
It's like gifts or, like acts of physical
touch or acts of service or something.

00:24:40.355 --> 00:24:45.615
I met someone recently, and she told me
my love language is all of them, which I

00:24:45.615 --> 00:24:47.225
think is the most honest answer, right?

00:24:47.365 --> 00:24:50.055
Because, sure, there's probably some
weighting around what people value

00:24:50.055 --> 00:24:53.385
more or less, and in the absence
of the things they value the most,

00:24:53.645 --> 00:24:57.315
it would probably feel a bit like
deprivation in any relationship.

00:24:57.325 --> 00:25:00.345
But as I'm describing all
this, I'm realizing more and

00:25:00.345 --> 00:25:03.295
more, yes, all of that matters.

00:25:03.465 --> 00:25:06.284
Anxious, attached, secure,
attached, love languages, etc.

00:25:06.815 --> 00:25:09.334
But it really doesn't get
to the heart of the matter.

00:25:09.985 --> 00:25:13.825
It really doesn't get to this, as
you're describing it, this generative

00:25:13.905 --> 00:25:18.145
drive in individuals, and whether
or not those match up well along

00:25:18.145 --> 00:25:21.385
the points of compatibility with
the other person's generative drive.

00:25:21.565 --> 00:25:25.435
And I haven't run a controlled study
for this, but the best evidence I have

00:25:25.435 --> 00:25:30.145
is that there, fortunately, are many
people out there who are in happy,

00:25:30.145 --> 00:25:35.095
healthy relationships, but there
are many, many people who are not,

00:25:35.575 --> 00:25:38.345
either because they can't find them,
or they're in them and they're not

00:25:38.345 --> 00:25:41.205
healthy, or they're not happy, etc.

00:25:41.284 --> 00:25:45.555
So if you could elaborate a bit
more on the generative drive, again

00:25:45.555 --> 00:25:47.445
reminding us what the nature of that is.

00:25:47.445 --> 00:25:50.515
We covered this a bit in episodes
one and two, but what the nature

00:25:50.515 --> 00:25:55.595
of that is and some different ways
that that's expressed and how that

00:25:56.515 --> 00:25:59.595
shows up in healthy relationship.

00:25:59.795 --> 00:26:05.204
Paul Conti: Yeah, I think we really
disprove the idea that some of

00:26:05.874 --> 00:26:09.575
these factors that I think are
superficial in the context o f whether

00:26:09.575 --> 00:26:10.745
people are going to be compatible.

00:26:10.745 --> 00:26:12.995
They're not superficial things,
but they're not germane.

00:26:13.005 --> 00:26:18.415
So let's say you think about music,
and say two people are contemplating

00:26:18.415 --> 00:26:22.454
romance, and they both really like music.

00:26:23.034 --> 00:26:25.035
W ell, that could go very, very well.

00:26:25.045 --> 00:26:27.885
Let's say they both have
a strong generative drive.

00:26:28.545 --> 00:26:33.074
Those pillars are pretty healthy,
and the geyser of the agency and

00:26:33.075 --> 00:26:37.145
gratitude is riding upon it, and
they can find peace in themselves.

00:26:38.065 --> 00:26:41.955
They're strongly generative, then
they could become interested in

00:26:42.284 --> 00:26:43.785
the music the other person likes.

00:26:43.785 --> 00:26:46.545
There's not going to
be a complete overlap.

00:26:46.955 --> 00:26:49.365
Even if people generally
like the same thing.

00:26:49.735 --> 00:26:53.055
But let's say they like different things,
like just liking music, or even liking

00:26:53.055 --> 00:26:55.435
the same music, there's still difference.

00:26:55.905 --> 00:27:01.145
So the person has to go beyond themselves,
and say, okay, I'm interested in what you

00:27:01.155 --> 00:27:02.965
think, even about the music we both like.

00:27:03.155 --> 00:27:04.315
What factors do you like?

00:27:04.585 --> 00:27:08.365
There are places of learning
and of growth for both.

00:27:08.615 --> 00:27:11.875
And you could see how both liking music,
whether it's the same or different

00:27:11.875 --> 00:27:13.645
music, that sounds great, right?

00:27:13.645 --> 00:27:16.404
But you could also see how
that could not be great.

00:27:17.295 --> 00:27:21.025
If the generative drive is too low
and the aggression drive is too

00:27:21.025 --> 00:27:25.675
high, then I'm going to think, I like
music, my music is better than yours.

00:27:27.265 --> 00:27:32.265
People then start, they'll fragment,
even though they have the same interest.

00:27:32.265 --> 00:27:35.164
Or if the pleasure drive is too high,
then they think, I want to listen to

00:27:35.165 --> 00:27:39.665
my music, not yours, I'm familiar with
it, instead of, hey, I'm interested in

00:27:39.665 --> 00:27:44.505
that music because you're interested
in it, and I'm interested in you.

00:27:45.095 --> 00:27:46.695
Which leads us to the second part.

00:27:46.695 --> 00:27:50.465
Let's say you have someone who really has
no interest in music and someone who does.

00:27:50.784 --> 00:27:55.254
Well, there can be an openness of saying,
if I'm interested in this person and

00:27:55.254 --> 00:27:59.845
this person is really interested in
music, I have some interest in it, right?

00:27:59.845 --> 00:28:01.015
I want to learn something about it.

00:28:01.015 --> 00:28:04.194
I want to experience some
of it with the other person.

00:28:04.194 --> 00:28:06.655
And let's say that person then
finds, hey, that's not my thing.

00:28:06.655 --> 00:28:09.075
It's not the thing we connect
on, then, why would that be

00:28:09.075 --> 00:28:09.905
the end o f the world, right?

00:28:09.905 --> 00:28:12.755
P lenty of people, one person goes
to concerts, the other doesn't.

00:28:12.765 --> 00:28:18.155
So we try to find these points of
commonality, because I think we

00:28:18.155 --> 00:28:20.815
get over reductionist, and then
we think, oh, here's a bunch of

00:28:20.815 --> 00:28:22.625
factors that we will identify.

00:28:22.965 --> 00:28:28.774
And what they do is they obscure us
from the primary factor, which is the

00:28:29.264 --> 00:28:33.395
generative drive, which, of course, will
then induce open mindedness mentalization.

00:28:33.395 --> 00:28:37.955
If I don't like music and you do, instead
of saying, what do you like music for?

00:28:39.045 --> 00:28:40.595
I need a friendship with
somebody who likes music.

00:28:40.595 --> 00:28:43.315
You think, hey, that's interesting.

00:28:43.414 --> 00:28:46.365
If I'm interested in you as a friend
and I have respect for you and what you

00:28:46.374 --> 00:28:50.105
think, then why would I not have some
interest in what you're interested in?

00:28:51.095 --> 00:28:56.715
So this idea of compatibility revolves
around the health in each person.

00:28:56.744 --> 00:29:02.094
It doesn't revolve around factors
that are anything but the concrete

00:29:02.095 --> 00:29:05.505
logistical factors that would
just keep two people apart.

00:29:06.335 --> 00:29:09.665
Andrew Huberman: I love the idea that
healthy relationships center around the

00:29:09.665 --> 00:29:14.425
factors that really matter within the
self, in particular the generative drive.

00:29:15.124 --> 00:29:20.545
And I love the example of someone being
able to be curious about somebody's

00:29:21.185 --> 00:29:23.774
interests, about their partner's
interests, even though they might

00:29:23.774 --> 00:29:29.615
not share those from the standpoint
of agency and gratitude, because the

00:29:29.615 --> 00:29:31.395
agency component there is really key.

00:29:31.395 --> 00:29:34.705
I think that a lot of people feel
as if they aren't really good at

00:29:34.705 --> 00:29:39.495
something or really knowledgeable about
something, then it's not for them.

00:29:40.405 --> 00:29:43.514
Which is the opposite of
having agency and gratitude.

00:29:43.515 --> 00:29:46.425
Because gratitude is
closely tied to humility.

00:29:47.185 --> 00:29:48.365
How could we know everything?

00:29:48.375 --> 00:29:50.375
It can be good, certain
things, and not others.

00:29:50.555 --> 00:29:56.234
So the way you describe it includes
aspects of openness, of humility, but

00:29:56.235 --> 00:29:57.954
also the agency side, the empowerment.

00:29:57.975 --> 00:30:01.045
Like, if I'm going to learn
more, then perhaps we could

00:30:01.045 --> 00:30:02.375
enjoy more of that together.

00:30:02.955 --> 00:30:03.855
Perhaps not, right?

00:30:04.015 --> 00:30:09.765
I think this is what I'm sensing, and I'm
also sensing that the words like-minded,

00:30:10.045 --> 00:30:16.154
like-minded people has so much more to do
with their generative drives and how those

00:30:16.165 --> 00:30:21.655
match up as opposed to the activities
that they prefer engaging in and the sorts

00:30:21.655 --> 00:30:27.435
of foods they like or the movies they
like, which makes sense at some level.

00:30:27.435 --> 00:30:32.075
But I think it is still counterintuitive
for a lot of us who just kind of

00:30:32.225 --> 00:30:36.484
reflexively think, oh, they like the same
things, or maybe even they met at work

00:30:36.505 --> 00:30:38.445
because they like the same type of work.

00:30:39.245 --> 00:30:40.895
They like to live in a certain
area of the country, and

00:30:40.895 --> 00:30:42.415
therefore, by proximity, they met.

00:30:43.275 --> 00:30:47.254
And this raises all sorts of
interesting ideas, perhaps, about

00:30:47.845 --> 00:30:49.505
the statistics of what we see.

00:30:49.905 --> 00:30:55.374
Like two musicians together, we
therefore assume that musicians belong

00:30:55.415 --> 00:30:57.355
together or two scientists together.

00:30:57.425 --> 00:31:01.155
I know loads of scientist-scientist
couples or scientist-physician

00:31:01.155 --> 00:31:05.194
couples, but of course, the numbers
are skewed because they were working

00:31:05.195 --> 00:31:08.034
in environments where it increased
the probability they would interact.

00:31:08.454 --> 00:31:11.844
So this is actually a vote for online
dating in some sense, because it breaks

00:31:11.844 --> 00:31:15.525
through all those, sometimes even
geographical barriers, but certainly

00:31:15.525 --> 00:31:17.935
the traditional barriers of culture.

00:31:18.255 --> 00:31:18.755
Paul Conti: Right.

00:31:19.095 --> 00:31:22.245
And if you think about it, we're
talking about relationships, so

00:31:22.555 --> 00:31:26.105
I would like my relationships to
last as long as possible, right?

00:31:26.664 --> 00:31:30.495
That means now we're talking about
my lifespan and my health span.

00:31:30.495 --> 00:31:33.485
That then becomes part of that discussion.

00:31:33.485 --> 00:31:36.185
And of course, we're very interested
in lifespan and health span.

00:31:36.455 --> 00:31:41.265
And we see people do much, much
better when they're interconnected

00:31:41.275 --> 00:31:44.444
in the world around them, when
they're still learning new things.

00:31:44.664 --> 00:31:49.655
We know that's true, that people often
will tail, they sort of trail off of what

00:31:49.655 --> 00:31:53.145
they're learning, whether it's music,
it's literature, it's the world around

00:31:53.145 --> 00:31:55.785
us relatively early in our lifespan.

00:31:56.175 --> 00:32:01.535
But the person who's interconnected
learning new things has a much

00:32:01.535 --> 00:32:05.614
greater probability of living
longer and living healthier.

00:32:05.745 --> 00:32:06.245
Right?

00:32:06.315 --> 00:32:07.675
So think, what's that about?

00:32:07.675 --> 00:32:09.105
It's about a generative drive.

00:32:09.665 --> 00:32:12.855
It's about, you know what, I've learned
a lot of things over the course of my

00:32:12.855 --> 00:32:17.335
life, and I'm 80 years old now, and
great, that there's more to learn, right?

00:32:17.335 --> 00:32:18.705
And that's what you see.

00:32:18.705 --> 00:32:22.844
It makes me think of a woman who's
around 90 years old in my practice.

00:32:23.194 --> 00:32:27.475
She's always learning new things, she's
super interested in things and I'm always

00:32:27.485 --> 00:32:30.565
struck by how she seems so much younger.

00:32:31.145 --> 00:32:34.525
And there, that's not just a selection
bias, like, oh, I just happen to

00:32:34.565 --> 00:32:39.855
see that in that person, she has the
factors that predispose to aging in

00:32:39.855 --> 00:32:41.994
the way that's healthiest and happiest.

00:32:42.255 --> 00:32:46.635
So it really comes down to, the root
of all of it, in ourselves, in our

00:32:46.635 --> 00:32:50.995
relationships, in the quality of them
over time and how long we get to have them

00:32:51.295 --> 00:32:54.194
really arises from a generative drive.

00:32:54.225 --> 00:32:59.404
And that's the thing that makes us
then undefended, and lets us find

00:32:59.434 --> 00:33:04.185
interest in things about other people
that are different from the things in

00:33:04.215 --> 00:33:06.815
us, which also comes about naturally.

00:33:06.825 --> 00:33:09.429
I think it's interesting that we
have this sort of bias, like I said,

00:33:09.429 --> 00:33:10.865
that musicians belong together.

00:33:11.244 --> 00:33:11.624
Well, why?

00:33:11.624 --> 00:33:13.475
They're familiar with
the same things, right.

00:33:13.585 --> 00:33:16.075
I guess they have the same interest,
same thing with scientists.

00:33:16.635 --> 00:33:20.015
But then a lot of people
like different foods, right?

00:33:20.115 --> 00:33:21.605
I love different ethnic foods.

00:33:21.725 --> 00:33:23.835
Why?
Because it's different, right?

00:33:24.045 --> 00:33:25.795
It's an appreciation of difference.

00:33:25.795 --> 00:33:28.655
I don't want to eat just like I
did growing up and many, many,

00:33:28.655 --> 00:33:29.675
many people are like that.

00:33:30.145 --> 00:33:32.535
That's an appreciation of
difference, of diversity.

00:33:32.755 --> 00:33:36.464
So sometimes we'll kind of harness that
and we'll look at it and we'll say, oh,

00:33:36.645 --> 00:33:40.385
that's present in us, but for whatever
reason we become very reductionist

00:33:41.075 --> 00:33:45.825
about relationships and now we're
trying to match based upon sameness,

00:33:47.635 --> 00:33:50.834
and sameness is not the point of it.

00:33:50.834 --> 00:33:53.544
I mean there are even thoughts
about people then in some way

00:33:53.545 --> 00:33:56.245
seeking difference, and maybe
pheromones are telling us that.

00:33:56.365 --> 00:34:00.395
I actually know very little about
that, but I certainly know that

00:34:00.515 --> 00:34:05.415
striving for sameness doesn't make
good things happen in relationships.

00:34:05.595 --> 00:34:09.505
I've been doing this for over 20
years and I don't see that the

00:34:09.514 --> 00:34:13.764
alleged factors of sameness matter
again unless they're so concrete.

00:34:13.764 --> 00:34:17.145
Like if this person is absolutely going to
live in North America and that person is

00:34:17.145 --> 00:34:20.975
absolutely going to live in South America,
let's not potentially put them together.

00:34:21.195 --> 00:34:25.735
But once we get away from almost that
level of concreteness, let's look

00:34:25.735 --> 00:34:30.264
for something different, and then
like everything else, it simplifies.

00:34:31.175 --> 00:34:35.625
The higher we go up the ladder,
the more simple things get.

00:34:35.635 --> 00:34:38.264
If you're looking at the pillars,
structure of self, function of

00:34:38.264 --> 00:34:41.855
self, you're creating the agency and
gratitude, then what are you looking

00:34:41.855 --> 00:34:43.205
for when you're looking for a partner...

00:34:43.835 --> 00:34:45.225
a match in generative drive.

00:34:46.325 --> 00:34:49.264
I want mine to be strong and I see
that it's strong in this person.

00:34:49.525 --> 00:34:53.055
They create in a way, you know what,
I do science, they grow a garden.

00:34:53.755 --> 00:34:54.935
We're generative together.

00:34:55.215 --> 00:34:57.935
Like that could be super compatible
because we're looking at the

00:34:57.935 --> 00:34:59.494
one factor that really matters.

