00:38:20.135 --> 00:38:23.565
episodes is certainly not necessary to
digest what we're talking about today.

00:38:24.305 --> 00:38:26.605
Could you go a little bit deeper
into the generative drive,

00:38:26.855 --> 00:38:29.265
flesh out for us what that is?

00:38:29.995 --> 00:38:33.065
I think generative, I think
generator, I think energy.

00:38:34.055 --> 00:38:36.455
I hear aggression or aggressive drive.

00:38:36.455 --> 00:38:43.265
I think, as I think most people do,
friction, maybe even conflict, maybe

00:38:43.265 --> 00:38:45.655
physical conflict, maybe verbal conflict.

00:38:46.035 --> 00:38:50.085
But I know it's some of that
perhaps, but a whole lot more.

00:38:50.595 --> 00:38:52.155
And then pleasure drive and pleasure.

00:38:52.615 --> 00:38:55.484
Some people think bliss,
some people think delight.

00:38:56.295 --> 00:38:58.895
Some people probably have all
sorts of specific things they

00:38:58.895 --> 00:39:00.125
think of with respect to pleasure.

00:39:00.125 --> 00:39:02.525
So if you could just flesh those out
for us a little bit more, because

00:39:02.725 --> 00:39:07.285
I think those are going to serve as
cornerstones of today's discussion.

00:39:07.815 --> 00:39:08.315
Paul Conti: Sure.

00:39:08.545 --> 00:39:14.125
So drives, in this case the generative
drive, sort of exist within us.

00:39:14.175 --> 00:39:17.395
And what they do is
they're defining potential.

00:39:17.405 --> 00:39:21.218
So right now, one could argue, if
w e just pause for a couple of s

00:39:21.218 --> 00:39:23.775
econds, we're not doing anything
generative in those seconds.

00:39:24.325 --> 00:39:27.715
But then we resume doing something
that we believe is generative.

00:39:28.445 --> 00:39:32.435
So at the time we're pausing, that's
when we can sort of look at that as a

00:39:32.435 --> 00:39:40.255
drive within us to make new learning, to
understand things we didn't understand

00:39:40.285 --> 00:39:42.525
before, to spread a sense of goodness.

00:39:42.845 --> 00:39:47.214
So it resides in us in a way
that is determined by a whole

00:39:47.215 --> 00:39:48.345
bunch of different factors.

00:39:48.784 --> 00:39:51.425
Like everything else that's
determined within us, there's a

00:39:51.425 --> 00:39:53.395
nature and a nurture component.

00:39:53.405 --> 00:39:56.385
So some aspects of it are what are
the genetics that came down to us?

00:39:56.625 --> 00:39:58.275
What are formative experiences?

00:39:58.545 --> 00:40:00.945
But we can go in and alter that.

00:40:00.965 --> 00:40:06.004
So if we see the drive as a set of
potentials, like in that moment, we pause.

00:40:06.265 --> 00:40:11.174
There's a set of potentials within us,
like potential of where we can take our

00:40:11.174 --> 00:40:16.385
thoughts, our actions, our reflections,
our decisions, that's the drive within us.

00:40:16.675 --> 00:40:23.155
And it exists within us in a
way that we could localize now.

00:40:23.155 --> 00:40:26.435
Like, if we think enough about, okay,
how much of a generative drive is there

00:40:26.445 --> 00:40:32.795
in a person, how much is that person
looking to make a better life for

00:40:32.795 --> 00:40:34.605
themselves or a better world around them?

00:40:34.605 --> 00:40:37.325
And how do they feel about themselves
and their ability to do that?

00:40:37.325 --> 00:40:38.495
How generative are they?

00:40:38.675 --> 00:40:41.345
Or how shut down or
demoralized or envious?

00:40:41.525 --> 00:40:43.994
So we can, in a sense, localize that.

00:40:44.395 --> 00:40:49.105
And again, the localization has these
genetic and nurture components to it,

00:40:49.305 --> 00:40:51.754
but we can then go and influence that.

00:40:51.785 --> 00:40:53.525
And that's the importance.

00:40:53.535 --> 00:40:58.234
If the drive is a set of potentials,
a set of possibilities, then that

00:40:58.235 --> 00:41:03.745
drive is one and the same pervaded
with, however we want to describe

00:41:03.755 --> 00:41:07.555
that, with the agency and gratitude,
because the agency and gratitude is

00:41:07.555 --> 00:41:08.985
sort of the operative form of that.

00:41:08.985 --> 00:41:09.884
That's the verb.

00:41:10.455 --> 00:41:12.895
It's the drive, actually
driving something.

00:41:13.475 --> 00:41:17.095
So if you have a strong generative
drive, then agency and gratitude

00:41:17.095 --> 00:41:18.285
are leading the decisions.

00:41:18.285 --> 00:41:23.065
The reflections are coming through
that, the aggression within us,

00:41:23.065 --> 00:41:28.535
or again, that's the historical
word, but so we can say aggression,

00:41:28.545 --> 00:41:31.675
assertion, proactiveness, right?

00:41:31.985 --> 00:41:36.004
All of this inside of us and our
drive for pleasure, for gratification,

00:41:36.475 --> 00:41:40.885
that all is, then it's in us, but
it's subserving the generative drive,

00:41:40.885 --> 00:41:44.815
meaning the generative drive, that
set of potentials and possibilities

00:41:45.155 --> 00:41:50.745
is dominant, which of course makes
sense with agency and gratitude, those

00:41:50.795 --> 00:41:54.375
active verbs being what is most active.

00:41:54.375 --> 00:42:00.075
And then we're also going to feel at
times, and hopefully at a lot of times,

00:42:00.895 --> 00:42:05.695
that set of peacefulness, that sense of
contentment, the sense of delight, what

00:42:05.695 --> 00:42:09.094
you're describing when you're doing the
podcast and you're in it and all these

00:42:09.105 --> 00:42:10.815
good things are happening to you, right?

00:42:10.815 --> 00:42:13.065
There's a strong generative drive in you.

00:42:13.065 --> 00:42:15.955
That set of potentials are
being actualized through the

00:42:15.955 --> 00:42:18.435
verbs of agency and gratitude.

00:42:18.435 --> 00:42:21.805
Like you're doing it all right then,
and as you're doing it, you have

00:42:21.855 --> 00:42:25.755
a sense of peace and contentment
as you're enacting all of it.

00:42:25.975 --> 00:42:27.745
And then that makes you healthier.

00:42:27.755 --> 00:42:29.424
It reinforces the generative drive.

00:42:29.425 --> 00:42:32.934
It protects you against the next
sling or arrow of outrageous

00:42:32.935 --> 00:42:34.315
fortune that will come your way.

00:42:35.045 --> 00:42:38.964
You become more self knowledgeable,
more, you become stronger.

00:42:39.905 --> 00:42:43.155
And ultimately, that's
what we're looking for.

00:42:43.155 --> 00:42:45.034
That's the state of goodness.

00:42:45.035 --> 00:42:47.814
And I believe that if you
look h istorically, what

00:42:47.815 --> 00:42:48.825
is it that we're seeking?

00:42:48.825 --> 00:42:53.675
We can put so many words to it, and
we're choosing to put these words to it.

00:42:53.675 --> 00:42:56.585
I mean, I think that this is the
structure, because I think the

00:42:56.585 --> 00:43:00.085
science, the history, the clinical
experience, the phenomenology,

00:43:00.085 --> 00:43:01.725
I think it all tells us this.

00:43:02.025 --> 00:43:06.184
We can put different words to it, but what
we're looking at when we're looking at

00:43:06.204 --> 00:43:10.985
truth and how to get to the "happiness"
that people have sought through the

00:43:10.985 --> 00:43:13.194
ages, I think we know enough now.

00:43:13.195 --> 00:43:17.805
We've learned enough that we can say,
oh, this is what it looks like, and

00:43:17.805 --> 00:43:23.340
it fits the arbiter of all truth,
which is, as you get further u p

00:43:23.425 --> 00:43:25.675
the hierarchy, it gets simpler.

00:43:25.704 --> 00:43:27.745
That's true about humans.

00:43:27.745 --> 00:43:31.924
What's going on in my unconscious
is very, very complicated.

00:43:32.505 --> 00:43:36.375
But if that's summing to, as we
get higher up to, I'm approaching

00:43:36.375 --> 00:43:41.445
the world with a lot of agency and
gratitude, that's a lot simpler.

00:43:41.695 --> 00:43:47.464
And that's what can be common among
us, which is why if my pillars are very

00:43:47.465 --> 00:43:51.985
different from your pillars and what
we've struggled against, each is very

00:43:51.985 --> 00:43:56.895
different, and what we may still have to
work against is very different, well, we

00:43:56.895 --> 00:44:01.135
can be extremely compatible as friends,
because are we working on ourselves?

00:44:01.155 --> 00:44:05.305
Are we fostering the generative drive so
we're coming at the world in that way?

00:44:05.305 --> 00:44:10.215
And agency and gratitude, that's the
similarity between us that matters.

00:44:10.465 --> 00:44:14.544
And ultimately, we're looking at the
potential that every moment we're building

00:44:14.555 --> 00:44:19.665
the potential in us for what comes at the
next moment, which is why we're describing

00:44:20.005 --> 00:44:24.685
the overlap, the point of commonality
that really matters, that matters.

00:44:24.685 --> 00:44:25.725
We're putting a label to it.

00:44:25.725 --> 00:44:29.288
We're saying it's the generative drive,
but i t is also that, okay, we both c ome

00:44:29.355 --> 00:44:31.785
at the world through agency and gratitude.

00:44:32.005 --> 00:44:36.625
If two people are assessing compatibility,
but if we're looking at something to nest

00:44:36.625 --> 00:44:42.304
that under, and what seems most logical is
the set of potentials within us that we're

00:44:42.315 --> 00:44:47.325
building and altering each moment, and
then as I alter it in this moment, it's

00:44:47.335 --> 00:44:49.795
altered and then affects my next moment.

00:44:50.275 --> 00:44:54.225
If I do something that's just kind of
thoughtless to somebody because I'm

00:44:54.225 --> 00:44:57.074
in a bad mood, then what am I doing?

00:44:57.074 --> 00:44:58.475
I'm projecting out my aggression.

00:44:58.475 --> 00:45:00.474
I'm doing something that
basically makes me less than.

00:45:01.055 --> 00:45:03.395
And then I'm going to feel
less in the next moment.

00:45:03.595 --> 00:45:06.735
Whether that moment is about me or
is about someone else, the drives

00:45:06.985 --> 00:45:12.715
are the potentials in us, but we are
actively working on them, determining

00:45:12.715 --> 00:45:15.735
them, changing them each moment.

00:45:16.975 --> 00:45:22.245
Andrew Huberman: I'm well on board the
idea that the typical pairings, let's

00:45:22.245 --> 00:45:26.635
call them the idiosyncratic pairings of
musician with musician, in many cases, but

00:45:27.055 --> 00:45:32.055
sometimes very verbal person with quieter
person, introvert with extrovert, or

00:45:32.055 --> 00:45:39.055
introvert with introvert all of that takes
backseat or perhaps even back backseat.

00:45:39.505 --> 00:45:42.705
Perhaps it even is out of the
vehicle compared to the critical

00:45:42.705 --> 00:45:45.055
importance of generative drive.

00:45:45.345 --> 00:45:45.845
Paul Conti: Right.

00:45:46.885 --> 00:45:51.015
Andrew Huberman: When we think about
generative drives in individuals, you

00:45:51.015 --> 00:45:54.865
beautifully described what a generative
drive is and how it shows up in

00:45:54.865 --> 00:46:00.995
individuals when thinking about generative
drives in romantic relationships, however,

00:46:01.875 --> 00:46:07.465
because generative drive can be expressed
to varying degrees, does one often see

00:46:07.795 --> 00:46:14.274
that, or do you think that, a matching
of sort of levels of generative drives

00:46:14.695 --> 00:46:17.215
is what fosters the best relationships?

00:46:17.215 --> 00:46:20.865
In other words, let's say somebody
has a pretty high pleasure drive,

00:46:22.025 --> 00:46:27.045
but not a very strong, aggressive,
also called, proactiveness drive.

00:46:27.045 --> 00:46:33.835
And so maybe they watch a lot of
Netflix, like a lot of Netflix, but

00:46:33.835 --> 00:46:36.605
they're not even the sort of person
that's really excited about the shows

00:46:36.605 --> 00:46:38.895
and telling you about them, because
there's a version of watching a lot

00:46:38.895 --> 00:46:42.884
of Netflix where the person is really
interested in learning and in knowing.

00:46:42.885 --> 00:46:46.184
Maybe even they're thinking about
writing something, you know,

00:46:46.194 --> 00:46:48.975
some poetry or book, or they're
bringing some of that to their life.

00:46:49.575 --> 00:46:53.525
I mention it that way because
watching Netflix isn't bad, per se.

00:46:54.025 --> 00:46:55.075
It's not anti-generative.

00:46:55.075 --> 00:46:57.084
Paul Conti: Is it an
escape, or is it generative?

00:46:57.084 --> 00:46:59.375
Is a person thinking about what they're
going to do, they're going to write

00:46:59.375 --> 00:47:03.305
that book, or are they just trying
to numb out, and at the end of five

00:47:03.305 --> 00:47:06.405
hours in front of the television, they
don't know what those five hours were?

00:47:06.915 --> 00:47:12.215
There's no intrinsic value judgment
based upon a lot of things that

00:47:12.215 --> 00:47:15.274
we make value judgments about.

00:47:15.845 --> 00:47:17.565
You have to look at, who is the person?

00:47:17.595 --> 00:47:18.565
What is the context?

00:47:18.874 --> 00:47:20.634
Are they being generative or not?

00:47:20.855 --> 00:47:21.115
Andrew Huberman: Yeah.

00:47:21.115 --> 00:47:23.955
Some of the movies and shows that
I've watched in relationships became

00:47:24.194 --> 00:47:28.975
the points of connection around, at
least to me, tremendously interesting

00:47:28.975 --> 00:47:33.104
discussions on hikes that we took
the next day and reflections on our

00:47:33.105 --> 00:47:35.035
own relationship and work and life.

00:47:35.035 --> 00:47:39.444
And so I'm so glad that you point out that
there's nothing intrinsically valuable

00:47:39.445 --> 00:47:45.495
or invaluable about something like
Netflix, or even intrinsically passive

00:47:45.995 --> 00:47:49.575
about doing something like sitting around
or even reading books, for that matter.

00:47:49.605 --> 00:47:53.084
There's a passive version of reading
great books also, people forget that.

00:47:53.305 --> 00:47:55.385
It's hard to read a great
book and not learn something.

00:47:55.425 --> 00:47:56.825
I guess that's why they're
called great books.

00:47:56.825 --> 00:47:59.434
But there is a version of
that, and I know many people.

00:47:59.434 --> 00:48:00.725
Paul Conti: People want
to possess the book.

00:48:01.155 --> 00:48:03.505
I read the book and I'm going t o check
it off and I don't know what was in it.

00:48:03.505 --> 00:48:04.525
Like people do that.

00:48:04.835 --> 00:48:05.885
That is not generative.

00:48:05.995 --> 00:48:10.415
Andrew Huberman: So should we consider
matching of level or expression of

00:48:10.415 --> 00:48:17.694
generative drives as perhaps what we
are all seeking in seeking relationship?

00:48:18.915 --> 00:48:24.195
And do you think that people tend to pair
up naturally, pair up with people that

00:48:24.195 --> 00:48:26.615
have a similar level of generative drive?

00:48:27.175 --> 00:48:30.175
Or if they don't, do you
think it leads to problems?

00:48:30.815 --> 00:48:34.135
Paul Conti: Yeah, I think that by and
large people don't, that it's not the

00:48:34.135 --> 00:48:37.654
thing that we're thinking about and
looking for in ourselves or others,

00:48:37.675 --> 00:48:42.155
and I absolutely believe that it
makes problems and let's take a look--

00:48:42.225 --> 00:48:42.855
Andrew Huberman: To not look.

00:48:42.895 --> 00:48:44.175
It makes problems to not look.

00:48:44.465 --> 00:48:44.775
Paul Conti: Right.

00:48:44.775 --> 00:48:45.285
It makes sense.

00:48:45.494 --> 00:48:49.455
The fact that we're not basing
it upon generative drive does

00:48:49.455 --> 00:48:54.464
indeed, I think, make many, many
and in fact, countless problems.

00:48:54.675 --> 00:48:59.165
And I would take as an example, think
about the idea of a trauma bond, right.

00:48:59.525 --> 00:49:01.865
A trauma bond is not a bad thing.

00:49:03.045 --> 00:49:06.845
That sounds like a strong statement,
because the way that I hear a

00:49:06.845 --> 00:49:12.085
trauma bond used is it's a bad
thing, b ut it doesn't have to be.

00:49:12.325 --> 00:49:13.565
So let's take a look at it.

00:49:14.025 --> 00:49:16.715
Imagine that you have what
people call a trauma bond.

00:49:16.715 --> 00:49:21.085
You have two people who will
make up a situation, right.

00:49:21.885 --> 00:49:25.115
They're functioning in the world
around them, b ut they each have

00:49:25.115 --> 00:49:29.875
had some very s ignificant trauma
that is creating issues in them.

00:49:29.875 --> 00:49:32.155
So let's say they're avoidance issues.

00:49:32.165 --> 00:49:34.855
A person doesn't feel safe or
comfortable in the world around them.

00:49:35.265 --> 00:49:39.154
They get invited to places that
they'd like to go, but they don't go.

00:49:39.845 --> 00:49:42.674
They want to go to the museum and see
something really new and cool there.

00:49:42.685 --> 00:49:43.865
But there might be a crowd.

00:49:43.874 --> 00:49:45.145
I mean, this happens.

00:49:45.145 --> 00:49:48.534
And let's say you have two people
who both have this in them.

00:49:48.965 --> 00:49:51.485
It could be because the trauma is similar.

00:49:51.525 --> 00:49:53.275
The trauma could be night and day.

00:49:53.965 --> 00:49:55.495
But they each have this in them.

00:49:55.805 --> 00:50:02.525
They could bond around that trauma
in a way that worsens the trauma.

00:50:02.525 --> 00:50:05.044
That's why people think
negatively of a trauma bond.

00:50:05.715 --> 00:50:11.065
So if the case is that the trauma
bond is not a good thing f or these

00:50:11.115 --> 00:50:12.895
two people, you say, why is that?

00:50:13.015 --> 00:50:16.675
It's not because both of them have h
ad trauma and both of them are impacted

00:50:16.675 --> 00:50:20.234
by trauma, and both of them a re
impacted by trauma in similar ways.

00:50:20.485 --> 00:50:21.134
It's not that.

00:50:21.515 --> 00:50:26.005
It's that the drives are not in a
healthy place and the gratification

00:50:26.335 --> 00:50:30.295
of the generative drive and the
pleasure drives are not high enough.

00:50:30.295 --> 00:50:36.425
So let's imagine the generative
drive could be relatively low in

00:50:36.895 --> 00:50:41.165
each of these people in one or the
other, or they could have a naturally

00:50:41.475 --> 00:50:44.075
high drive that's being thwarted.

00:50:45.285 --> 00:50:48.174
So there's something that's out
of balance where the drive is,

00:50:48.525 --> 00:50:50.865
the ability to express the drive.

00:50:50.865 --> 00:50:52.595
Is there enough agency?

00:50:53.175 --> 00:50:56.215
If there's not, like, let's say one
person is really, really shut down.

00:50:56.485 --> 00:50:58.645
That person can't stand their job, right?

00:50:58.815 --> 00:50:59.155
Okay.

00:50:59.155 --> 00:51:00.995
Something is shutting that person down.

00:51:01.005 --> 00:51:03.385
The generative drive wishes
for greater expression.

00:51:03.395 --> 00:51:04.765
They go back and look at themselves.

00:51:04.785 --> 00:51:06.085
You can bring that into line.

00:51:06.535 --> 00:51:09.134
But something is out
of balance at the time.

00:51:09.905 --> 00:51:14.925
The pleasure drive may be low or it may
be high, but it may not be gratified.

00:51:14.935 --> 00:51:18.545
It may be that that person loves
museums and wants to go to the museum,

00:51:18.905 --> 00:51:20.865
but can't find that gratification.

00:51:21.285 --> 00:51:22.235
That's a possibility.

00:51:22.475 --> 00:51:28.064
The aggression or assertion drive would
be on the low end, but it could be higher.

00:51:28.065 --> 00:51:32.725
Maybe if that person felt safer, that
drive within them has a lot of latitude

00:51:32.725 --> 00:51:34.174
in it, and maybe it could move higher.

00:51:34.655 --> 00:51:39.135
But something is out of balance in
the drives and in their expression.

00:51:40.235 --> 00:51:41.445
That's the problem.

00:51:41.445 --> 00:51:44.335
Because let's look at the
other side, where a trauma

00:51:44.335 --> 00:51:46.135
bond is a great thing, right?

00:51:46.465 --> 00:51:51.144
So each of the people in the example
has trauma, and they recognize it in

00:51:51.145 --> 00:51:55.975
themselves, and they understand how
it makes things harder for them, and

00:51:56.305 --> 00:52:00.330
then they're communicating about how
it makes it hard in the other, right?

00:52:00.615 --> 00:52:05.115
So maybe there's a lot of overlap
in social avoidance and sense of

00:52:05.115 --> 00:52:08.075
vulnerability, but maybe there
are things that are different

00:52:08.164 --> 00:52:09.685
in one person versus the other.

00:52:09.925 --> 00:52:14.805
So then they can come together and
say, look, what's the goal of life?

00:52:15.125 --> 00:52:17.535
I would like to be as
healthy a s I can be.

00:52:17.535 --> 00:52:18.525
I'm working on myself.

00:52:18.545 --> 00:52:20.045
You want to be as healthy as you can be?

00:52:20.275 --> 00:52:22.154
We want to be as healthy as we can be.

00:52:22.515 --> 00:52:26.195
And if we're healthy, we also
help e ach of us be healthy.

00:52:26.425 --> 00:52:30.795
So maybe those two people, neither
of w hich they would ever go to the

00:52:30.795 --> 00:52:35.358
m useum on their own, because the
trauma inside of them is at a point i

00:52:35.358 --> 00:52:39.365
t hasn't been worked through, or it's
a t a place where they just simply feel

00:52:39.365 --> 00:52:45.324
too vulnerable, but together they can
go to the museum, and then the bond

00:52:45.324 --> 00:52:48.904
around trauma helps them be healthy.

00:52:49.095 --> 00:52:53.025
The drives are in a better place
because they're able to recognize, hey,

00:52:53.025 --> 00:52:55.325
there are things going on in me that
I'd like to be different and better,

00:52:55.325 --> 00:52:58.275
and you recognize that, too, and we
can talk about it with one another.

00:52:58.275 --> 00:52:58.775
Right?

00:52:58.784 --> 00:53:02.584
So they're in a healthier place,
and then from that healthier

00:53:02.585 --> 00:53:05.005
place, they build greater health.

00:53:06.405 --> 00:53:09.905
Andrew Huberman: So much of what we
hear about in terms of friction points

00:53:09.905 --> 00:53:15.525
in relationship centers around it seems
communication or lack of communication.

00:53:16.015 --> 00:53:19.125
And as you're describing the role
of the generative drive in healthy

00:53:19.125 --> 00:53:24.495
relationship, it seems to me that
it ties back in every way to agency

00:53:24.495 --> 00:53:28.365
and gratitude and agency being such
a critical element of communication,

00:53:28.365 --> 00:53:31.955
because wherever you've identified that,
okay, there's a potential problem here.

00:53:32.685 --> 00:53:36.434
People with high generative drive in these
examples seem to be capable of, like,

00:53:37.855 --> 00:53:43.085
self inquiry, asking the other person
questions that bring them closer together

00:53:43.105 --> 00:53:44.855
and to a deeper understanding of the self.

00:53:45.575 --> 00:53:51.445
I raise this because one thing that's
often overheard, or that I've overheard,

00:53:51.525 --> 00:53:56.605
I of course have siblings and friends,
and I'll place this in the way that I've

00:53:56.994 --> 00:54:02.225
most typically heard it, but I'm sure it
exists in other ways too, which is the

00:54:02.345 --> 00:54:07.595
conversation in my head is one where a
woman is saying they're with somebody or

00:54:07.595 --> 00:54:10.775
dating somebody, and he's so clueless.

00:54:11.284 --> 00:54:13.345
I wish that he would just do this thing.

00:54:13.624 --> 00:54:18.054
Sometimes these are acts of chivalry,
like, maybe it's flowers or vacations, but

00:54:18.065 --> 00:54:23.685
more often than not, it's a request or a
complaint about a lack of proactiveness.

00:54:24.265 --> 00:54:27.725
This is also what ratchets up to
these very pan statements that you

00:54:27.725 --> 00:54:30.734
hear, like, oh, there's no real men
these days, or like, there are no

00:54:30.735 --> 00:54:32.575
real men left, or this kind of thing.

00:54:32.695 --> 00:54:34.245
You also hear it in the reverse, right?

00:54:34.245 --> 00:54:36.775
You hear men making pan
statements about women.

00:54:37.395 --> 00:54:40.535
And here we're doing this in the
context of heterosexual pairings.

00:54:40.555 --> 00:54:42.924
Paul Conti: But of course it
could all work just as well.

00:54:43.955 --> 00:54:48.024
Andrew Huberman: It could all work in
the context of homosexual pairings too.

00:54:48.585 --> 00:54:52.175
So you hear those sorts of things, and
it sounds like a lack of communication.

00:54:52.344 --> 00:54:56.755
Okay, maybe one person needs to be better
at asking for their needs to be met.

00:54:56.765 --> 00:54:59.055
Maybe the other person needs
to develop more of an awareness

00:54:59.055 --> 00:55:00.215
of what the other person needs.

00:55:00.215 --> 00:55:04.314
Of course, we all seem to kind of
intrinsically wish that things would

00:55:04.315 --> 00:55:07.615
just happen for us without the need
to request them or ask for them.

00:55:07.994 --> 00:55:14.275
But I'm realizing again that all of
that is distracting commotion, because

00:55:14.275 --> 00:55:15.935
that's not really what's going on.

00:55:16.385 --> 00:55:22.984
What's really going on, it seems, is
that the engine behind communication, the

00:55:22.985 --> 00:55:28.995
engine behind curiosity, a desire to learn
and know and create something from that

00:55:28.995 --> 00:55:34.625
learning and knowing in the relationship,
the generative drive, is really what's

00:55:34.635 --> 00:55:39.435
the issue, or the lack of generative
drive in any of those conversations.

00:55:39.985 --> 00:55:44.495
It seems one could circle back to that and
go, okay, well, someone's not asking the

00:55:44.495 --> 00:55:48.065
right questions and therefore not doing
the right things because they don't either

00:55:48.094 --> 00:55:51.985
have a sense of agency, or they don't have
gratitude for the situation they're in,

00:55:51.985 --> 00:55:53.975
including their own ability to do that.

00:55:54.335 --> 00:55:54.825
Right?

00:55:54.825 --> 00:55:58.415
So when you hear people aren't showing
up for the relationship, they're not

00:55:58.415 --> 00:56:00.705
showing up, or she's not showing up.

00:56:00.705 --> 00:56:06.874
Or again, let's make the pan statement
go in the other direction, that

00:56:07.045 --> 00:56:08.695
somebody just wants a lot of attention.

00:56:08.875 --> 00:56:11.155
They just need an excess amount
of attention, won't let me do

00:56:11.155 --> 00:56:13.785
my own thing, but also want
me to work and be successful.

00:56:14.185 --> 00:56:18.635
Again, these are stereotypes, but
all of that seems very distracting.

00:56:18.954 --> 00:56:24.865
However, all of it seems far simpler
if we push it through this filter

00:56:25.075 --> 00:56:28.675
of generative drives and whether
or not it's being expressed.

00:56:29.175 --> 00:56:29.675
Paul Conti: Yes.

00:56:30.175 --> 00:56:34.585
I think maybe the best example of t
his, because it's so highly charged,

00:56:34.595 --> 00:56:38.984
is, i magine that the pleasure drive is
through the lens of sex and sexuality.

00:56:38.994 --> 00:56:43.895
So imagine that people are in a pairing,
they're in a relationship, regardless

