01:09:28.755 --> 01:09:30.175
generative drive in the relationship.

01:09:31.244 --> 01:09:32.665
So what does that mean?

01:09:33.244 --> 01:09:37.485
The first person has to have a strong
generative drive, be healthy, understand

01:09:37.485 --> 01:09:41.645
that even though I n eed to live in North
America, does it actually mean that?

01:09:41.645 --> 01:09:43.235
That I really need to
live in North America?

01:09:43.235 --> 01:09:45.975
I have to be able to see beyond
myself, a nd what does that mean to

01:09:45.975 --> 01:09:48.785
the p erson I love who needs to live
o n the other side of the world?

01:09:49.025 --> 01:09:53.545
If the other person can do that
t oo, then in the relationship,

01:09:54.085 --> 01:09:57.995
which is a new entity, i t emerges
from one person and the other.

01:09:58.185 --> 01:10:01.085
You can know everything about one
person, everything about the other.

01:10:01.915 --> 01:10:03.655
You don't know about the
two of them together.

01:10:04.565 --> 01:10:07.264
So the two of them together are an "us".

01:10:07.624 --> 01:10:13.955
And if that us has a strong generative
drive, which it can If each person

01:10:14.005 --> 01:10:17.065
has a strong generative drive, they
can bring to the state of emergence

01:10:17.075 --> 01:10:19.005
of the us the generative drive.

01:10:19.265 --> 01:10:22.665
And we're talking about a relationship,
so say the love between them

01:10:22.975 --> 01:10:24.645
and they can find a way through.

01:10:24.935 --> 01:10:29.096
So the idea that love cures
all things, we have to define,

01:10:29.215 --> 01:10:30.445
what does that actually mean?

01:10:30.865 --> 01:10:33.395
It doesn't mean we have a lot
of pleasure together, right?

01:10:33.395 --> 01:10:35.305
Or we like a lot of the same things.

01:10:35.565 --> 01:10:39.955
That's not what that means, a nd people
can very much love one another but not be

01:10:39.955 --> 01:10:42.554
aware of the limitations inside of them.

01:10:42.775 --> 01:10:46.655
Because maybe there are other things going
on in them, like, say, childhood trauma.

01:10:46.655 --> 01:10:50.585
They love one another, but they can't
g et out of their comfort zone enough.

01:10:50.775 --> 01:10:55.565
So the point is, if we make ourselves
as healthy as we can be a nd then two

01:10:56.105 --> 01:11:00.094
selves come together that a re making
themselves as healthy as they c an be,

01:11:00.465 --> 01:11:06.935
then the us between them really can fit
that definition of love and do anything.

01:11:06.935 --> 01:11:10.705
But we have to define that in the
right way, in a way that honors

01:11:10.705 --> 01:11:13.755
the truth, which, again, is not
a higher degree of complexity.

01:11:14.124 --> 01:11:15.555
It's actually more simple.

01:11:15.954 --> 01:11:18.315
Andrew Huberman: So you gave an
example of a romantic relationship

01:11:18.345 --> 01:11:22.674
where one person has a strong sex
drive and the other a weaker drive.

01:11:23.145 --> 01:11:28.125
And then we talked about an example where
both people have an essentially high sex

01:11:28.125 --> 01:11:32.915
drive and where that could potentially be
beneficial, assuming that the generative

01:11:32.925 --> 01:11:34.515
drive is also high in both of them.

01:11:35.555 --> 01:11:39.365
And we also explored a little bit of
how it can be bad for relationship

01:11:39.374 --> 01:11:41.595
if it exceeds the generative drive.

01:11:42.924 --> 01:11:45.405
What about the aggressive
proactiveness drive?

01:11:45.415 --> 01:11:47.945
How does that show up
in romantic pairings?

01:11:48.505 --> 01:11:53.625
If one person has a high proactiveness,
aka aggressive drive, and the other

01:11:53.625 --> 01:11:56.685
person does not, what does that look like?

01:11:57.355 --> 01:11:59.364
Do you see that often in
your clinical practice?

01:12:00.075 --> 01:12:00.575
Paul Conti: Sure.

01:12:01.105 --> 01:12:04.335
If this makes sense, maybe we look
a t that example of the person

01:12:04.335 --> 01:12:08.275
who's a two and the person who's an
eight on the sex drive scale, right?

01:12:08.624 --> 01:12:12.645
You'd say, okay, the person who's
a t wo, who's trying to raise that

01:12:12.925 --> 01:12:14.934
right, has a strong generative drive.

01:12:15.175 --> 01:12:15.824
What does that mean?

01:12:15.824 --> 01:12:17.165
The person thinks, you know what?

01:12:17.165 --> 01:12:18.074
I think I can do this.

01:12:18.364 --> 01:12:19.575
I can set myself about this.

01:12:19.575 --> 01:12:22.365
I see it will make my relationship
better, I'm going to give it a try.

01:12:22.825 --> 01:12:25.575
That's a generative drive in
action, the pleasure drive.

01:12:25.585 --> 01:12:29.385
The person may look at that and say, look,
maybe this can be more fun for me, right?

01:12:29.715 --> 01:12:30.754
It hasn't been that fun for me.

01:12:30.975 --> 01:12:34.325
If I have a low pleasure drive,
could I start enjoying this more?

01:12:34.325 --> 01:12:35.785
And then maybe that moves up, right?

01:12:36.005 --> 01:12:39.251
Or maybe I have a higher pleasure drive,
but it's not been gratified because I

01:12:39.251 --> 01:12:40.685
haven't been able to be open and honest.

01:12:41.115 --> 01:12:42.495
Let me see if I can make that better.

01:12:43.245 --> 01:12:49.345
Then what's active, what the person is d
oing, is then going to be the aggressive

01:12:49.355 --> 01:12:54.825
or assertive, proactive to that drive
and going to the potential in that

01:12:54.825 --> 01:12:56.895
drive and mining some of what's in it.

01:12:57.165 --> 01:12:58.705
Like, yeah, how am I going to do that?

01:12:58.705 --> 01:13:02.965
I'm going to bring myself to bear,
a nd that's not an easy thing.

01:13:02.965 --> 01:13:07.045
It's not like that person then all of
a sudden decides to be more sexual.

01:13:07.055 --> 01:13:10.524
There has to be a lot of communication
between the two people, a lot of

01:13:10.524 --> 01:13:14.335
discussion of what setting might be best
for that, what helps the person feel

01:13:14.335 --> 01:13:15.935
understood or feel more comfortable.

01:13:16.355 --> 01:13:18.525
There's a lot then, to do there.

01:13:18.835 --> 01:13:22.405
And the person may even need to
g o back to the pillars because

01:13:22.405 --> 01:13:26.124
the p erson may feel like, whoa,
I don't think I can do that.

01:13:26.824 --> 01:13:27.465
And then it is...

01:13:27.675 --> 01:13:28.305
that's not good.

01:13:28.305 --> 01:13:29.215
I want to do that.

01:13:29.434 --> 01:13:30.434
What is it that I can't do?

01:13:30.435 --> 01:13:31.655
There's a realization there.

01:13:31.655 --> 01:13:35.655
And then maybe the person who's an eight
because they're so well connected gets it.

01:13:36.255 --> 01:13:36.615
Okay.

01:13:36.615 --> 01:13:37.525
You can't do that.

01:13:37.725 --> 01:13:41.534
Let me support you in whatever way
while you're figuring that out.

01:13:41.565 --> 01:13:43.495
Because they're both generative,
they want to figure that out.

01:13:44.425 --> 01:13:45.114
Andrew Huberman: Sorry to interrupt.

01:13:45.124 --> 01:13:48.755
When you say, go back to the pillars,
you mean go back to exploring

01:13:48.755 --> 01:13:51.895
the structure of themselves and
their function of themselves--

01:13:51.975 --> 01:13:52.215
Paul Conti: Yes.

01:13:52.215 --> 01:13:56.425
Andrew Huberman: --so that they can, for
instance, get some insight into what sorts

01:13:56.425 --> 01:13:58.504
of defense mechanisms might be in place--

01:13:58.805 --> 01:13:59.305
Paul Conti: Right.

01:13:59.635 --> 01:14:02.384
Andrew Huberman: --what they're paying
attention to or their behaviors,

01:14:02.385 --> 01:14:05.905
like maybe even health related
behaviors that could be impacting

01:14:05.944 --> 01:14:10.905
their sex drive, but maybe even
things that reside at a deeper level.

01:14:10.905 --> 01:14:15.744
The unconscious mind, talking to somebody
till they make a connection around shame

01:14:16.815 --> 01:14:21.565
or some story that they've integrated into
their thinking at a subconscious level.

01:14:21.605 --> 01:14:22.105
Paul Conti: Right.

01:14:22.264 --> 01:14:26.875
Just an example that I see a l ot is
avoidance, where, again, not always,

01:14:26.875 --> 01:14:32.041
but let's say the person who's the
two on the sex drive scale who f inds

01:14:32.215 --> 01:14:36.414
like, I can't do it, r ight, wow,
I just can't bring myself to do it.

01:14:36.515 --> 01:14:37.975
And then they go back and they explore.

01:14:38.624 --> 01:14:41.655
This happens a lot
where there's avoidance.

01:14:41.745 --> 01:14:45.005
And then we get curious about the
avoidance because the person's

01:14:45.015 --> 01:14:48.935
like, there's something that
just, I don't want to do that.

01:14:49.505 --> 01:14:50.245
So there's avoidance.

01:14:50.245 --> 01:14:51.425
We identify avoidance.

01:14:51.425 --> 01:14:54.545
We also identify that it's not healthy,
b ecause the person does actually

01:14:54.545 --> 01:14:57.324
want to do that because it's good
for the relationship and it could be

01:14:57.324 --> 01:15:01.275
good for them, too, a nd then maybe
we start looking at u nconscious

01:15:01.315 --> 01:15:02.835
mind, right, or conscious mind.

01:15:02.835 --> 01:15:05.725
And maybe we don't find
it, because it's easy.

01:15:05.745 --> 01:15:09.275
Now, if I said, oh, there was a major
trauma that the person hasn't processed.

01:15:09.425 --> 01:15:12.045
Sometimes it's that, but a
lot of times it's not that.

01:15:12.664 --> 01:15:16.485
Maybe that person just never learned
to feel comfortable with sexuality.

01:15:16.645 --> 01:15:22.045
Or maybe the way that they experience
o r are attracted to sexuality

01:15:22.275 --> 01:15:26.118
was disparaged or denigrated.

01:15:26.118 --> 01:15:28.434
Or they had some bad feeling
about it because the society

01:15:28.435 --> 01:15:29.604
and the culture told them that.

01:15:29.805 --> 01:15:32.105
Or maybe they had a couple
of bad experiences where they

01:15:32.105 --> 01:15:33.345
were treated in a certain way.

01:15:33.685 --> 01:15:36.394
And then it's like, okay, that
person comes by that honestly,

01:15:36.395 --> 01:15:37.925
there's not a major trauma there.

01:15:37.925 --> 01:15:42.415
In fact, unfortunately, there's a trauma
that's almost predictable from the

01:15:42.415 --> 01:15:45.235
way our society has been structured.

01:15:45.445 --> 01:15:46.870
So person can go, look at that.

01:15:46.870 --> 01:15:52.575
I'm like, right, I never
learned how t o do this or how

01:15:52.575 --> 01:15:53.864
to be comfortable with this.

01:15:53.885 --> 01:15:58.755
Or you know what I learned that
I'm not good at it, or I learned

01:15:58.795 --> 01:16:01.255
that no one will enjoy it with me.

01:16:01.505 --> 01:16:02.415
But that's not true.

01:16:02.925 --> 01:16:05.695
These are situations not on the
side o f a loving relationship.

01:16:06.215 --> 01:16:10.401
Now t hat person is able to bring that
knowledge to bear, whether it came from t

01:16:10.401 --> 01:16:12.535
he unconscious mind or the conscious mind.

01:16:12.835 --> 01:16:15.455
And then that's how you work
to start shifting things.

01:16:15.455 --> 01:16:16.965
Like, that was then, this is now.

01:16:17.805 --> 01:16:18.685
It is not then.

01:16:18.685 --> 01:16:22.275
when, say, the person, say in
high s chool, had a sexuality that

01:16:22.305 --> 01:16:24.755
others didn't a pprove of and made
them feel bad, well, guess what?

01:16:24.755 --> 01:16:25.594
It's not then.

01:16:25.675 --> 01:16:28.044
And that's unfortunate.

01:16:28.065 --> 01:16:28.604
It's wrong.

01:16:28.604 --> 01:16:29.224
It's unjust.

01:16:29.225 --> 01:16:31.805
We're going to honor and
validate all that that is.

01:16:32.125 --> 01:16:33.705
But we're also going to look at that.

01:16:33.755 --> 01:16:35.645
That was then, this is now.

01:16:35.825 --> 01:16:37.725
And you get to behave differently now.

01:16:37.945 --> 01:16:41.024
You're an adult now, and adults
get to choose their relationships,

01:16:41.024 --> 01:16:42.244
and you chose a good relationship.

01:16:43.244 --> 01:16:46.675
So this is the kind of thing that can
get that person to the p oint where

01:16:46.895 --> 01:16:48.665
they can go back up through now.

01:16:48.675 --> 01:16:49.805
What happens at the end of it?

01:16:49.815 --> 01:16:51.145
The geyser is stronger.

01:16:51.395 --> 01:16:53.705
There's more agency, there's
more of a sense of gratitude.

01:16:54.285 --> 01:16:54.615
Why?

01:16:54.615 --> 01:16:58.915
Because the person can attach themselves
more to, I'm so lucky to have this person,

01:16:58.954 --> 01:17:02.624
as opposed to, damn it, I wish that person
didn't have a higher sex drive, right?

01:17:03.534 --> 01:17:06.195
And they feel better about themselves
instead o f, what's wrong with me

01:17:06.195 --> 01:17:07.645
that I d on't have a higher sex drive?

01:17:08.575 --> 01:17:11.100
Or I said they wish the other person
had a lower sex drive or wish they had

01:17:11.205 --> 01:17:13.195
a higher sex drive that's not grateful.

01:17:13.205 --> 01:17:16.535
Like I'm grateful I am who I a m and
that I have any sex drive at all.

01:17:16.535 --> 01:17:17.915
And I'm grateful for this other person.

01:17:18.244 --> 01:17:27.305
Now, through that, that change, they can
go back and better access the assertion,

01:17:28.135 --> 01:17:30.635
t he proactive drive, the aggressive
drive, whatever w e want to call it,

01:17:30.635 --> 01:17:34.695
because they've taken away, they've gone
and worked through the barrier to it.

01:17:34.954 --> 01:17:37.694
Then they're able to be a little m
ore out of their comfort zone, and the

01:17:37.695 --> 01:17:39.174
other person meets them there, right?

01:17:39.195 --> 01:17:45.115
And they start having this healthy thing
b etween them where the us that is them,

01:17:45.325 --> 01:17:48.085
the love between them, gets better, right?

01:17:48.085 --> 01:17:49.345
And then where do they find themselves?

01:17:49.345 --> 01:17:53.765
That's how they get to the five,
which is not a compromise between

01:17:53.784 --> 01:17:55.695
eight a nd two that satisfies neither.

01:17:55.895 --> 01:17:59.625
It is a compromise between eight
and t wo that is way better for

01:17:59.635 --> 01:18:01.625
both and for the relationship.

01:18:02.925 --> 01:18:06.725
Andrew Huberman: I'm curious about
common pairings that you observe in

01:18:06.725 --> 01:18:14.005
the clinical setting, and not to focus
on the bad, but if there are common

01:18:14.005 --> 01:18:20.705
pairings that rarely lead to a good
outcome, I think it's worth us learning

01:18:20.705 --> 01:18:25.085
about those, simply because they're
common and they lead to a bad outcome.

01:18:25.865 --> 01:18:30.764
And a discussion of this sort could
potentially help people avoid such

01:18:31.284 --> 01:18:34.325
pairings, or at least recognize
that they're in such pairings.

01:18:34.945 --> 01:18:38.195
And I realize, of course, because
of the way that we're framing things

01:18:38.195 --> 01:18:42.835
during this series, that anytime we
talk about a pairing, we're really

01:18:42.835 --> 01:18:49.835
talking about two maps coming together,
forming a new and somewhat independent

01:18:49.835 --> 01:18:51.885
map that represents the relationship.

01:18:53.395 --> 01:18:57.415
I have to assume that many people in
the world have maps that are very,

01:18:57.415 --> 01:19:02.025
very healthy, and they're probably
even those rare individuals that don't

01:19:02.025 --> 01:19:05.414
need to go into those cupboards that
reside within structure of self and

01:19:05.414 --> 01:19:06.744
function of self and do any work.

01:19:06.765 --> 01:19:09.549
But for those of you that are just
listening and not watching this,

01:19:09.624 --> 01:19:11.915
I'm sort of smiling as I'm saying
that, because I don't actually

01:19:11.915 --> 01:19:14.765
believe any such person exists.

01:19:14.975 --> 01:19:18.935
I think all of us, even the healthiest
among us, could be even healthier

01:19:19.445 --> 01:19:23.494
and express more generative drive and
more positivity for ourselves and for

01:19:23.494 --> 01:19:24.900
the world were we to do that work.

01:19:24.900 --> 01:19:25.335
Paul Conti: Yes.
Absolutely.

01:19:25.335 --> 01:19:26.335
Andrew Huberman: So it's a lifelong--

01:19:26.335 --> 01:19:27.495
Paul Conti: There's no endpoint to that.

01:19:27.904 --> 01:19:28.665
That's beautiful.

01:19:28.694 --> 01:19:30.805
We can do that forever
as long as we're here.

01:19:31.505 --> 01:19:33.125
Andrew Huberman: And you
should, I do believe.

01:19:33.585 --> 01:19:35.795
Which is one of the reasons
for having this discussion.

01:19:35.795 --> 01:19:36.345
Exactly.

01:19:38.845 --> 01:19:42.115
Nonetheless, I have to imagine that
there are also a lot of people out

01:19:42.115 --> 01:19:45.785
there, perhaps most people, who
still have a lot of work to do.

01:19:46.505 --> 01:19:50.545
And those words, a lot, perhaps,
are in bold faced capital

01:19:50.555 --> 01:19:52.205
underlined, highlighted letters.

01:19:54.685 --> 01:19:58.965
And as a consequence, we see relationships
in the world that are not healthy.

01:19:59.695 --> 01:20:01.885
What are some of those
common unhealthy pairings?

01:20:01.925 --> 01:20:04.125
I think it's worth spending at
least a little bit of time on this.

01:20:04.145 --> 01:20:05.285
Paul Conti: Yeah, sure.

01:20:05.335 --> 01:20:10.265
As we talk about this, I think that we
should make a sort of somewhat simplified

01:20:10.275 --> 01:20:12.255
but hopefully helpful distinction.

01:20:12.265 --> 01:20:17.545
So there are people who are coming a t
coupling from primarily health, right?

01:20:17.675 --> 01:20:20.224
And again, this isn't to criticize
people who aren't coming at

01:20:20.225 --> 01:20:21.305
it from primarily health.

01:20:21.305 --> 01:20:24.025
We all want to get to where
we're coming at anything and

01:20:24.025 --> 01:20:25.495
everything from primarily health.

01:20:25.865 --> 01:20:29.745
And there a lot of the problems are
those simple things like, wow, I

01:20:29.745 --> 01:20:30.975
can't believe it didn't work out.

01:20:31.005 --> 01:20:33.494
I play the trumpet and
he plays the clarinet.

01:20:33.515 --> 01:20:36.675
It's like, no, that wasn't
a real set of factors.

01:20:36.675 --> 01:20:37.665
That's why it didn't work out.

01:20:38.065 --> 01:20:40.285
It's more in that realm.

01:20:40.315 --> 01:20:43.265
It's not always just that, but that
k ind of stuff is a big factor.

01:20:44.005 --> 01:20:51.285
When we look in the other realm, where
there are significantly problematic

01:20:52.145 --> 01:20:58.684
mental health issues, which people
come by honestly but need to face if

01:20:58.694 --> 01:21:03.995
life is going to get better, there
are two common paradigms there.

01:21:04.025 --> 01:21:07.845
Now, again, there are way more than
t wo ways this can happen, but I'm

01:21:07.845 --> 01:21:12.414
going to highlight two that we see
a lot and maybe more than anything

01:21:12.414 --> 01:21:14.275
else in the clinical setting.

01:21:14.704 --> 01:21:18.645
So the first gets called
repetition compulsion.

01:21:19.335 --> 01:21:23.285
But I want to be very clear, I do
not believe that there is any such

01:21:23.285 --> 01:21:25.315
thing as a repetition compulsion.

01:21:25.325 --> 01:21:27.805
A compulsion is something
that one cannot control.

01:21:28.445 --> 01:21:31.864
What's going on inside the person is
very, very complicated when they're making

01:21:31.864 --> 01:21:34.335
decisions that lead to repeating a cycle.

01:21:34.524 --> 01:21:38.435
And those are things that can change
and do not have to be compulsive.

01:21:38.695 --> 01:21:45.385
So people are often struck by why a person
is in the same bad relationship, seemingly

01:21:45.655 --> 01:21:49.614
that they have always been in, but with
a different name on the other person.

01:21:49.715 --> 01:21:54.134
I mean, people will say this like i t's
that person's fourth abusive relationship

01:21:54.135 --> 01:21:57.515
in a row, and it seems to be, it's just
the same person with a different name.

01:21:57.845 --> 01:21:59.355
People say that a lot.

01:21:59.375 --> 01:22:01.585
They say that about themselves,
they say that about others,

01:22:01.965 --> 01:22:03.265
and it's very, very baffling.

01:22:03.265 --> 01:22:07.415
I mean, people have often been in my
office very upset about that, like

01:22:07.415 --> 01:22:10.854
realizing they're repeating and they
don't know and it's frightening.

01:22:10.895 --> 01:22:14.805
And you can get a lot of fear
and vulnerability from that.

01:22:15.005 --> 01:22:21.015
But there's a way of understanding it
that, because the limbic system, the

01:22:21.015 --> 01:22:25.915
emotional parts of our minds don't
care about the clock and the calendar.

01:22:26.324 --> 01:22:31.135
So trauma impacts, it impacts
the whole brain, but trauma

01:22:31.165 --> 01:22:32.608
impacts the limbic system.

01:22:32.835 --> 01:22:38.535
It creates strong negative emotion that
then stays with us regardless of time.

01:22:38.595 --> 01:22:39.765
It doesn't care about time.

01:22:40.465 --> 01:22:46.685
So imagine now a person gets in a
relationship and the relationship

01:22:47.274 --> 01:22:48.625
starts to become abusive.

01:22:49.315 --> 01:22:53.445
I mean, it's easy to say that and
then move on to something else.

01:22:54.045 --> 01:22:55.415
What's that like for that person?

01:22:55.985 --> 01:22:58.745
What does that feel like when
that person, who they may have

01:22:58.745 --> 01:23:04.104
seen as a protector, a friend, is
now cursing at them, denigrating

01:23:04.104 --> 01:23:05.395
them, pushing them, hitting them?

01:23:05.405 --> 01:23:06.494
I mean, it's terrifying.

01:23:06.835 --> 01:23:07.655
It's horrible, right?

01:23:08.755 --> 01:23:11.695
So it is a deep impact, a t
raumatic impact on the person.

01:23:11.935 --> 01:23:18.444
And a lot of the time, most of the time,
like all trauma, it triggers shame.

01:23:18.595 --> 01:23:20.785
So trauma triggers shame in us.

01:23:20.785 --> 01:23:25.155
If the trauma is strong enough, it changes
us as we move forward in the world.

01:23:25.425 --> 01:23:29.245
And then we are different
in a way that makes the past

01:23:29.575 --> 01:23:31.635
very immediate in the present.

01:23:32.315 --> 01:23:37.275
So what the person then is trying to
do, and there's been a lot of thought

01:23:37.275 --> 01:23:40.614
about this over the years in the
fields, trying to understand this,

01:23:40.614 --> 01:23:45.145
and I think in many ways doing a good
job of understanding this, that the

01:23:45.785 --> 01:23:51.085
"repetition compulsion" is the drive,
and I don't mean drive like we're saying.

01:23:51.085 --> 01:23:56.798
There's a push inside the person to
t ry and make that right with the i

01:23:56.798 --> 01:24:00.815
dea that if that person can be in that
situation again, and I don't mean in

01:24:00.875 --> 01:24:04.755
the exact situation when someone raises
a hand to them, but they can navigate

01:24:04.755 --> 01:24:07.545
a relationship because they thought
it was a good idea the first time.

01:24:07.805 --> 01:24:10.754
So they don't want to feel
like that was a very bad idea.

01:24:10.755 --> 01:24:12.854
So I'm going to make it right, a nd
then I'm going to be okay and I'm

01:24:12.914 --> 01:24:16.264
going to be whole because the person
is driven by fear and v ulnerability

01:24:16.264 --> 01:24:17.485
and the shame of the trauma.

01:24:18.065 --> 01:24:22.175
So then they're trying to make it
right because the limbic system doesn't

01:24:22.175 --> 01:24:24.155
care about the clock or the calendar.

01:24:24.165 --> 01:24:25.475
If you can make it right now.

01:24:25.485 --> 01:24:29.614
You make it right in the past, b ecause
that system doesn't care about the past.

01:24:29.615 --> 01:24:33.834
It's driven by trauma, s o then
the person's trying to figure

01:24:33.835 --> 01:24:35.225
out something that is different.

01:24:35.225 --> 01:24:38.665
They're trying to choose that person,
behave in the relationship, they're trying

01:24:38.665 --> 01:24:45.035
to make t hings different, but they've
selected for a d ramatically high pretest

01:24:45.065 --> 01:24:51.374
probability of the same t hing happening
again, which is why if I could count the

01:24:51.374 --> 01:24:56.405
number of times t hat someone has said
to me, oh, you're not going to help me,

01:24:56.415 --> 01:24:57.665
especially a t the beginning of therapy.

01:24:57.665 --> 01:24:58.855
There's no way you can help me.

01:25:00.605 --> 01:25:02.975
Five relationships were totally terrible.

01:25:03.395 --> 01:25:04.284
There's no way.

01:25:04.295 --> 01:25:05.235
There's no hope, right?

01:25:05.435 --> 01:25:06.825
People have said that to me so many times.

01:25:06.965 --> 01:25:09.015
The last three, the last
five, the last nine.

01:25:09.485 --> 01:25:15.455
And I will say back, if you t ell me
seven different stories of relationships

01:25:15.455 --> 01:25:19.145
with seven pretty different people,
several different relationships as

01:25:19.145 --> 01:25:22.925
they evolved, and the same really
bad outcome, I'll agree with you,

01:25:24.015 --> 01:25:25.304
but you're not going to tell me that.

01:25:25.725 --> 01:25:26.985
And that's why there's hope.

01:25:27.855 --> 01:25:32.535
And no one has ever told me s even
different stories where I feel like,

01:25:32.535 --> 01:25:34.925
wow, you just can't do it, right?

01:25:35.165 --> 01:25:35.485
No.

01:25:35.485 --> 01:25:38.215
And again, even if there were seven,
I'm saying things for exaggeration.

01:25:38.215 --> 01:25:40.155
But there aren't.

01:25:40.585 --> 01:25:41.165
What is it?

01:25:41.165 --> 01:25:42.685
It's the same thing seven times.

01:25:43.445 --> 01:25:44.085
That's what it is.

01:25:44.114 --> 01:25:45.504
Because the person is repeating.

01:25:45.505 --> 01:25:49.075
And if they can understand the what
and the why, why are they selecting?

01:25:49.345 --> 01:25:50.505
How are they selecting someone?

01:25:50.505 --> 01:25:51.905
What's going on inside of them?

01:25:52.195 --> 01:25:55.725
We go back to the structure
of self, the function of self.

01:25:55.725 --> 01:26:01.005
We address what is there then
that absolutely can change.

01:26:01.204 --> 01:26:04.575
And then what we will talk about
is that person then goes out

01:26:04.575 --> 01:26:05.794
to find another relationship.

01:26:05.795 --> 01:26:07.775
Even if they said they
never, never would again.

01:26:07.785 --> 01:26:10.385
Like now they're making the
decision to go out, as to say, what

01:26:10.385 --> 01:26:11.655
relationship a re you looking for?

01:26:12.015 --> 01:26:12.685
Your second.

01:26:13.635 --> 01:26:15.104
You're the first one seven times.

01:26:15.235 --> 01:26:16.245
No harm, no foul.

01:26:16.874 --> 01:26:21.155
But you can navigate the second
because they're in a different place.

01:26:21.635 --> 01:26:24.394
They're coming through
agency and gratitude instead

01:26:24.395 --> 01:26:26.005
of denigrating themselves.

01:26:26.005 --> 01:26:30.085
If you're what's wrong with me, what's
wrong with everybody else, I'm here.

01:26:30.855 --> 01:26:33.075
Even though some bad things
have happened to me, look at

01:26:33.075 --> 01:26:34.324
what I can bring myself to bear.

01:26:34.325 --> 01:26:39.135
I can go out there and try and find
someone I can see with clear vision now.

01:26:39.654 --> 01:26:42.755
There's agency, there's gratitude,
and then that p erson can go

01:26:42.755 --> 01:26:45.195
out and find relationship number
two that's a really good one.

01:26:46.275 --> 01:26:51.035
Andrew Huberman: In the example you gave,
it's very powerful and also very extreme.

01:26:51.204 --> 01:26:56.355
An abusive relationship where someone
is sadly getting hit or screamed at.

01:26:58.025 --> 01:27:02.335
Oftentimes, it seems people end up in
repeated unsuccessful pairings, meaning

01:27:02.375 --> 01:27:08.545
unhappy pairings of maybe someone who's
very strongly assertive, maybe we would

01:27:08.545 --> 01:27:10.405
say has a strong aggressive drive.

01:27:11.075 --> 01:27:12.925
Some people might even
call them a narcissist.

01:27:13.425 --> 01:27:15.605
That phrase is thrown
around a lot these days.

01:27:15.654 --> 01:27:18.355
I mean, narcissists,
gaslighting, like projection.

01:27:18.355 --> 01:27:21.915
These phrases are thrown around all
the time, I think, with frankly,

01:27:22.005 --> 01:27:26.115
very little understanding of what
they actually mean, unless they're

01:27:26.115 --> 01:27:27.545
coming from a qualified clinician.

01:27:29.865 --> 01:27:35.824
Is it the case that somewhat
passive or submissive people

01:27:36.725 --> 01:27:37.765
are drawn to narcissists?

01:27:38.575 --> 01:27:41.045
Are narcissists drawn
to submissive people?

01:27:41.045 --> 01:27:43.455
I think these questions ring
in a lot of people's minds.

01:27:43.505 --> 01:27:46.094
Paul Conti: If I could clarify
something that I think I maybe

01:27:46.355 --> 01:27:47.915
could have said in a clearer way.

01:27:48.085 --> 01:27:51.864
You pointed out that the example
I gave was a very strong one

01:27:51.864 --> 01:27:52.995
or an extreme one, right?

01:27:53.245 --> 01:27:57.875
So as a physician, I think I
naturally gravitate to the extreme

01:27:57.875 --> 01:28:00.615
circumstances because they're the
ones that bring the greatest risk.

01:28:01.205 --> 01:28:06.945
But we are talking to people in those
settings, but most people are not

01:28:07.095 --> 01:28:12.134
in those extreme settings, but that
extreme serves as a model for how

01:28:12.135 --> 01:28:14.165
things that are less extreme can happen.

01:28:14.165 --> 01:28:18.235
So when I think, we're sitting here
talking to everyone across the spectrum,

01:28:18.485 --> 01:28:21.985
and most relationships that are not
going well, thank goodness, are not

01:28:21.985 --> 01:28:25.195
going to be that extreme, or the repeated
patterns aren't going to be that extreme.

01:28:26.085 --> 01:28:30.815
But it's just as important to pay
attention to a pattern where one person

01:28:32.155 --> 01:28:35.805
really is sort of more deferential than
they would like to be to the other,

01:28:35.805 --> 01:28:39.475
than is comfortable to them, and they're
seeking someone, ultimately, who's just

01:28:39.475 --> 01:28:44.765
a little bit more assertive, and that's
not s ome disaster, but it cannot go well

01:28:44.765 --> 01:28:48.394
over time, where that person slowly gives
up, say, more and more of themselves.

01:28:48.735 --> 01:28:50.345
And there can be that repeated pattern.

01:28:50.345 --> 01:28:53.715
You hear people say, yeah, it was
pretty good or pretty equal at the

01:28:53.715 --> 01:29:01.025
beginning, but then I just had l ess and
less of a say, or t hen more and more.

01:29:01.565 --> 01:29:03.905
I just became the whole relationship.

01:29:04.354 --> 01:29:08.015
So it's no less important
when it's less dramatic.

01:29:08.215 --> 01:29:11.565
Because the relationship still goes in
a wrong way when it's more dramatic.

01:29:11.575 --> 01:29:15.275
There's more risk, of course, but most
p eople are going to be in the less

01:29:15.275 --> 01:29:19.315
dramatic but super important category.

01:29:19.315 --> 01:29:22.965
And I think it's important
to point that out, too.

01:29:24.165 --> 01:29:28.905
Andrew Huberman: How much of what we're
talking about is nested on people's deep,

01:29:28.965 --> 01:29:33.925
perhaps understandable desire to just make
sure that the other person doesn't leave?

01:29:35.235 --> 01:29:38.724
I can't tell you how many times I've heard
about or observed relationships that from

01:29:38.725 --> 01:29:40.355
the outside that seemed really unhealthy.

01:29:41.245 --> 01:29:46.585
But it also occurred to me that people
are doing things on both sides of

01:29:46.585 --> 01:29:50.575
the relationship that ensure that
the other person doesn't leave.

01:29:50.575 --> 01:29:53.155
So I'm not talking about a physical
trapping of someone in the environment.

01:29:53.155 --> 01:29:54.305
I'm actually talking about the opposite.

01:29:54.305 --> 01:29:59.815
I'm talking about somebody going against
their better judgment and doing things

01:29:59.815 --> 01:30:05.215
for the other person or against themselves
or both, perhaps even in the context

01:30:05.235 --> 01:30:10.925
of what looks like healthy family, like
doing an excessive amount of raising

01:30:10.925 --> 01:30:17.334
of children or excessive amount of work
to support the family, simply to ensure

01:30:17.335 --> 01:30:19.065
that the other person doesn't leave.

01:30:19.485 --> 01:30:19.985
Paul Conti: Right.

01:30:20.645 --> 01:30:23.125
Here, again, we can look
across the spectrum.

01:30:23.555 --> 01:30:26.455
So before this, you had talked
about relationships that can be very

01:30:26.455 --> 01:30:28.365
unhealthy, like a narcissistic person.

01:30:28.365 --> 01:30:29.865
And how can those relationships develop?

01:30:30.385 --> 01:30:34.575
It's important that we touch on
that in the context of this because

01:30:34.575 --> 01:30:36.225
that's sort of the extreme example.

01:30:36.405 --> 01:30:40.365
So people who are narcissistic, meaning
a narcissistic character structure.

01:30:40.365 --> 01:30:43.925
Now we're getting into the realm of
significant psychiatric problems.

01:30:44.115 --> 01:30:47.235
And people who are
narcissistic are exploitative.

01:30:47.235 --> 01:30:50.435
They're a bottomless pit of
need arising from vulnerability,

01:30:50.654 --> 01:30:52.144
and yes, they can be helped.

01:30:52.145 --> 01:30:54.965
And if someone out there thinks I'm
narcissistic, then not trying to

01:30:54.965 --> 01:30:57.705
be negative or mean like you can
get help and you can be better.

01:30:57.705 --> 01:31:00.545
That's the whole point of what
you and I are talking about here.

01:31:01.285 --> 01:31:05.065
So people can change, but someone
who's coming at the world through a

01:31:05.065 --> 01:31:08.575
narcissistic character structure is
exploitive of the other and then can

01:31:08.575 --> 01:31:13.085
say, survey a room and find that person
who's had trauma such that that person

01:31:13.085 --> 01:31:16.075
is going to be desperate not to leave a
relationship, and then they can exploit

01:31:16.085 --> 01:31:19.315
that person and often behave in awful
ways and the other person won't leave.

01:31:19.675 --> 01:31:20.945
Now I'm giving an example.

01:31:20.945 --> 01:31:24.185
It's not the case that
way every single time.

01:31:24.185 --> 01:31:27.715
But that's the dominant picture.

01:31:27.765 --> 01:31:32.285
If you have a narcissistic person and who
is that person more likely to pair with,

01:31:32.285 --> 01:31:36.415
and then you sense the vulnerability, the
desire for the other person not to leave,

01:31:36.415 --> 01:31:40.474
and that person would have to have then
significant problems in themselves, like

01:31:40.615 --> 01:31:45.514
potentially, instead of a pathological
level of narcissism, a pathological

01:31:45.515 --> 01:31:47.295
level of dependence, for example.

01:31:47.565 --> 01:31:52.345
So we're in the realm
then, of real problems.

01:31:52.345 --> 01:31:55.535
And again, real problems can be treated
and real problems can be improved,

01:31:55.535 --> 01:31:59.945
and that's why we have clinical care,
but it can also serve as a model for

01:31:59.945 --> 01:32:04.185
seeing the ways that are less dramatic.

01:32:05.274 --> 01:32:10.015
So there are many people who,
through the desire for the other

01:32:10.015 --> 01:32:13.185
person not to leave, and we could
call that attachment insecurity.

01:32:14.045 --> 01:32:18.445
And maybe if we leave away the stereotype
version of it, the person doesn't feel

01:32:18.445 --> 01:32:20.144
securely attached to the other person.

01:32:21.045 --> 01:32:27.495
And somewhere inside of them, they've
learned that if they're more pleasing, if

01:32:27.495 --> 01:32:31.465
they give up little parts of themselves,
that person won't leave, right?

01:32:31.475 --> 01:32:35.045
It might be because that's why their
m other didn't leave their father,

01:32:35.065 --> 01:32:37.805
when they think about it, it might
be that's why their father left

01:32:37.805 --> 01:32:40.754
their mother, because their mother
wouldn't do that, whatever it may be.

01:32:41.005 --> 01:32:43.565
Or it may be their own prior
life experience or who knows,

01:32:43.565 --> 01:32:45.295
something they read but forgot?

01:32:45.315 --> 01:32:48.505
I mean, usually it's something
more salient, not always, but

01:32:48.515 --> 01:32:53.415
they've internalized something that
leads to a compromise of the self.

01:32:53.775 --> 01:32:57.385
Now, again, that's not what
attachment insecurity is.

01:32:57.574 --> 01:33:02.895
Attachment insecurity is just feeling
some insecurity about the attachment.

01:33:02.895 --> 01:33:07.355
So, for example, I feel attachment
insecurity about just about everything.

01:33:07.355 --> 01:33:15.164
I've had the experience of sudden,
very painful losses happening,

01:33:15.175 --> 01:33:19.745
so I have attachment insecurity
in the sense that I'm worried.

01:33:19.755 --> 01:33:24.605
I get anxious about people going
away, and I can want to over control,

01:33:24.925 --> 01:33:26.935
but I recognize this inside myself.

01:33:27.175 --> 01:33:30.505
I try and do my best to say, look, I
don't want that to make me unhealthy.

01:33:30.505 --> 01:33:31.544
I want to recognize it.

01:33:31.545 --> 01:33:34.864
I want to find some happy medium
where I honor that in me, but don't

01:33:34.874 --> 01:33:38.755
get carried away with it to the point
where I try and over control and then

01:33:38.755 --> 01:33:40.495
I push my relationships away, right?

01:33:40.815 --> 01:33:44.745
So I say that because there's such
a contrast between that kind of

01:33:44.745 --> 01:33:49.344
attachment insecurity, which can
involve over control, and the kind

01:33:49.345 --> 01:33:52.735
of attachment insecurity that can
involve compromise of the self.

01:33:52.735 --> 01:33:54.895
And we want to be very, very careful.

01:33:54.905 --> 01:33:55.835
We want to be careful.

01:33:55.945 --> 01:33:57.745
What is a trauma bond
and what does it mean?

01:33:58.005 --> 01:33:58.795
We want to be careful.

01:33:58.795 --> 01:34:00.895
What is attachment insecurity
and what does it mean?

01:34:01.165 --> 01:34:05.095
A certain kind of attachment
insecurity can lead a person to make

01:34:05.115 --> 01:34:10.504
progressive compromises of self that
are not good and healthy for them.

01:34:10.965 --> 01:34:14.495
Andrew Huberman: I'm curious about some
of these unhealthy relationships from

01:34:14.495 --> 01:34:19.725
the other side, meaning from the side of
the person who is, let's hope not, but

01:34:19.735 --> 01:34:25.395
in some cases exploited or who's being
taken advantage of, or in this scenario

01:34:25.435 --> 01:34:30.685
of a narcissistic person or an abusive
person is the one who's taking the abuse.

01:34:31.835 --> 01:34:35.505
Often, it seems the victim in
that scenario will be truly

01:34:35.515 --> 01:34:37.175
stuck in that situation.

01:34:37.645 --> 01:34:42.055
They can't leave for financial reasons
or kids or their own internal workings.

01:34:42.055 --> 01:34:46.265
Their psychological machinery has
them locked into that in some way.

01:34:46.265 --> 01:34:50.285
And it's that locked in by psychological
machinery that I'm interested in.

01:34:50.285 --> 01:34:53.685
And I think a lot of people are
interested in, because from the

01:34:53.685 --> 01:34:58.425
outside we just look at that and
say, why doesn't the person leave?

01:34:58.454 --> 01:34:59.625
Why don't they just leave?

01:35:00.215 --> 01:35:06.185
But clearly there's something about
that "situation" that works for them.

01:35:06.185 --> 01:35:09.125
And by works for them, I don't mean
that it's adaptive, clearly it's

01:35:09.135 --> 01:35:11.305
maladaptive, but that works for them.

01:35:11.765 --> 01:35:14.765
The possibility I raised earlier
perhaps is relevant here.

01:35:15.145 --> 01:35:20.035
Maybe it works for them because they know
that the narcissist gets some sort of

01:35:20.475 --> 01:35:25.615
internal reward for engaging in this kind
of dynamic and therefore won't leave.

01:35:25.985 --> 01:35:31.055
And the other person not leaving is of
more value to them than feeling safe.

01:35:31.115 --> 01:35:34.535
Even so, it's trading one
form of safety for another.

01:35:34.985 --> 01:35:39.275
Or perhaps the victim, as we're calling
them in this scenario, is somebody

01:35:39.275 --> 01:35:44.605
who feels a great sense of reward by
serving somebody more powerful than them.

01:35:45.625 --> 01:35:48.665
This raises all sorts of interesting
questions, perhaps about power

01:35:48.665 --> 01:35:51.444
dynamics as well, which I
certainly have questions about.

01:35:51.874 --> 01:35:57.815
But what are some examples of the internal
workings of such a person that reside

01:35:57.815 --> 01:36:02.935
within those bins of structure of self or
function of self that would put someone

01:36:02.935 --> 01:36:07.045
into that sort of situation and lead
them to stay in that kind of situation?

01:36:08.315 --> 01:36:12.695
Paul Conti: Well, any situation like
that, if it's, as you say, working for a

01:36:12.695 --> 01:36:17.054
person, which at times we see that from
t he outside because things continue

01:36:17.055 --> 01:36:22.405
to go along and things continue to go
forward, b ut that only works for someone

01:36:22.705 --> 01:36:28.244
if t hey don't have the understanding,
the empowerment, t hat we would wish

01:36:28.244 --> 01:36:30.704
for anyone and everyone to have.

01:36:31.074 --> 01:36:38.385
So what is going on inside leads that
person to feel a sense of an inability

01:36:38.385 --> 01:36:42.495
to change, whether they can't see their
way out of it or they kind of could, but

01:36:42.495 --> 01:36:48.115
could never navigate to it, and there's
a problem there if that's working for

01:36:48.115 --> 01:36:51.654
someone, b ecause we're talking about
something that's exploitive, right?

01:36:51.654 --> 01:36:54.645
That's abusive regardless
of how it's abusive.

01:36:54.895 --> 01:37:00.275
And I think, I believe, that can
only happen in the context of being

01:37:00.275 --> 01:37:05.094
demoralized or the circumstances
of being so disempowered.

01:37:05.265 --> 01:37:08.255
If someone just can't go 10 feet
from home, like there are situations

01:37:08.324 --> 01:37:13.975
of outrageous amounts of oppression
of a person where the person

01:37:13.975 --> 01:37:15.985
just cannot choose differently.

01:37:15.985 --> 01:37:19.930
And there are situations where we,
as stewards of all of us, where

01:37:20.045 --> 01:37:23.035
the government may intervene, for
example, ideally, right, as someone

01:37:23.035 --> 01:37:24.435
would come in and help that person.

01:37:24.765 --> 01:37:30.324
But barring that kind of outlier, a
person would only be in that situation

01:37:30.335 --> 01:37:32.655
in the context of being demoralized.

01:37:32.955 --> 01:37:39.835
And that demoralization would come
from too low a drive for assertion,

01:37:39.865 --> 01:37:41.975
being proactive aggression, right?

01:37:42.215 --> 01:37:46.314
That that drive, its
realization, is too low.

01:37:46.314 --> 01:37:53.165
And if we give people the understanding
and we give them the help, the means, they

01:37:53.165 --> 01:37:55.545
don't have to stay in abusive situations.

01:37:55.545 --> 01:38:00.694
Like we see this, in situations where our
society will intervene or someone helpful

01:38:00.695 --> 01:38:05.814
will intervene, some person or entity
will intervene and help a person, help

01:38:05.864 --> 01:38:12.105
that person to understand what's going on
inside of them, that leads them to feel

01:38:12.115 --> 01:38:15.585
that things can't be better, they can't
do better, or that they're not worth

01:38:15.624 --> 01:38:21.774
better, or whatever that may be, and help
them to find the empowerment, to then

01:38:21.784 --> 01:38:24.735
navigate themselves out of the situation.

01:38:25.175 --> 01:38:28.535
And here again, I think it's
not just agency, but it's

01:38:28.555 --> 01:38:30.985
agency plus gratitude, right?

01:38:30.985 --> 01:38:34.515
Including gratitude for having a
self that could leave a situation.

01:38:34.715 --> 01:38:40.195
When people are in situations like that,
we talk about people as beaten down.

01:38:40.524 --> 01:38:46.375
It's a way to kind of capture what I think
that feels like and how much suffering

01:38:46.375 --> 01:38:52.955
there is in those situations to be in
that place and to not have the agency

01:38:52.955 --> 01:38:56.765
to get out of it, then the person is not
feeling a sense of gratitude for self.

01:38:57.225 --> 01:39:00.364
They feel so bad about self that
there's nothing to be grateful for.

01:39:01.325 --> 01:39:03.505
And there is so much to be grateful for.

01:39:04.055 --> 01:39:07.725
We could go through any person in a
situation like that, and we could list

01:39:07.725 --> 01:39:11.095
wonderful things about that person,
adaptive things about that person, kind

01:39:11.095 --> 01:39:12.974
things about that person, diligent things.

01:39:13.355 --> 01:39:16.445
There are things like
that in every person.

01:39:16.704 --> 01:39:21.515
But if the person can't see that, they
can't attach to that, they can't have the

01:39:21.515 --> 01:39:28.765
agency and gratitude, then they stay in
a situation that is really defined by the

01:39:28.815 --> 01:39:33.275
agency and gratitude, and therefore that
aggressive assertion, proactive drive

01:39:33.574 --> 01:39:36.384
being expressed at such a low level.

01:39:37.385 --> 01:39:42.375
Andrew Huberman: I can see how a healthy
relationship could exist in relative

01:39:42.375 --> 01:39:44.885
isolation, not complete isolation.

01:39:44.885 --> 01:39:48.664
And here again, I'm referring to or
thinking about a romantic relationship.

01:39:48.664 --> 01:39:52.704
I can see how a healthy romantic
relationship could exist in relative

01:39:52.704 --> 01:39:59.495
isolation, a few friends, some contact
with family members, or in great

01:39:59.965 --> 01:40:04.655
connectivity with friends and family
and neighbors, etc., and still be a

01:40:04.655 --> 01:40:06.445
really great romantic relationship.

01:40:06.454 --> 01:40:06.954
Paul Conti: Sure.

01:40:07.704 --> 01:40:09.795
Andrew Huberman: I can also see how
the sorts of relationships that we

01:40:09.795 --> 01:40:12.495
happen to be focusing on at this
moment, which are these unhealthy

01:40:12.495 --> 01:40:18.845
dynamics, are made far worse by
lack of connectivity to outsiders.

01:40:18.905 --> 01:40:22.214
In fact, a previous guest on this
podcast was David Buss, who's a

01:40:22.215 --> 01:40:25.035
professor of evolutionary psychology
down at the University of Texas,

01:40:25.035 --> 01:40:27.865
Austin, and he talked about some of
his research into the dark triad.

01:40:28.575 --> 01:40:32.785
These are narcissistic, machiavellian,
sometimes also sociopathic

01:40:32.835 --> 01:40:35.935
individuals, and how that plays
out in romantic relationships.

01:40:36.315 --> 01:40:41.625
And it's a terrible thing, but an
important thing to understand given the

01:40:41.835 --> 01:40:43.625
unfortunate frequency that that occurs.

01:40:43.895 --> 01:40:48.025
But one of the things that I remember
so clearly from that discussion with

01:40:48.435 --> 01:40:54.635
David Buss was that even when there
isn't sociopathy or a strong desire to

01:40:54.635 --> 01:40:58.385
destroy the other, that in these sorts
of relationships, there's often an

01:40:58.385 --> 01:41:04.335
attempt to isolate the person, first by
isolating them from their family, also

01:41:04.335 --> 01:41:08.795
from friends and coworkers, but all
with the goal of convincing that person

01:41:08.795 --> 01:41:14.995
that no one else would want them as a
way to make them "voluntarily" stay.

01:41:15.204 --> 01:41:19.014
In other words, to undermine
their sense of safety, to ramp up

01:41:19.015 --> 01:41:22.145
their sense of anxiety, except in
the presence of that individual.

01:41:23.194 --> 01:41:26.805
And I'm remembering that now
because as we're talking about,

01:41:26.825 --> 01:41:28.285
why wouldn't somebody just leave?

01:41:28.295 --> 01:41:31.295
Why wouldn't they just tap
into that agency and gratitude?

01:41:31.995 --> 01:41:38.245
It's clear that the oppressor in this kind
of relationship has a real incentive to

01:41:38.245 --> 01:41:43.405
try and undermine agency and gratitude,
because, of course, with those, they

01:41:43.405 --> 01:41:47.065
would be revealed for what they are and
the person would feel unable to leave.

01:41:47.705 --> 01:41:52.075
Taking us back once again to the
critical need to cultivate agency and

01:41:52.075 --> 01:41:56.955
gratitude, not just in unhealthy, but
certainly in healthy relationships.

01:41:57.105 --> 01:42:00.514
Paul Conti: Right, and I think the
principle here is that darkness

01:42:00.545 --> 01:42:02.135
always favors the oppressor.

01:42:03.115 --> 01:42:05.835
So the oppressor wishes for darkness.

01:42:06.355 --> 01:42:10.865
So you want to isolate that person
because when people see that things

01:42:10.945 --> 01:42:14.705
are better for someone else, they
realize things can be better.

01:42:15.864 --> 01:42:19.405
When people are told by someone
that they're worthwhile or they're

01:42:19.405 --> 01:42:22.045
funny or they're pretty or they're
smart, or whatever the case may

01:42:22.045 --> 01:42:23.265
be, they may take that inside.

01:42:24.445 --> 01:42:29.305
They may take inside, oh, maybe I
am, I'm handsome and smart, maybe.

01:42:29.305 --> 01:42:30.715
Or they start thinking about that.

01:42:30.715 --> 01:42:35.885
They're very basic concepts that
allow a person to entertain new

01:42:37.235 --> 01:42:39.275
ways to look at themselves, right?

01:42:39.605 --> 01:42:43.875
So the person who's oppressing wants
that person to live in darkness.

01:42:43.875 --> 01:42:46.425
They don't want them to see
that there can be better.

01:42:47.015 --> 01:42:51.915
They don't want them to be directly told
that they're better than how things are.

01:42:52.245 --> 01:42:56.035
And that same darkness on the
outside, say the lights are out

01:42:56.065 --> 01:43:00.495
around the relationship is the goal
of the oppressor on the inside.

01:43:00.855 --> 01:43:04.384
Now, again, the oppressor may know this
and be doing it consciously, but often

01:43:04.385 --> 01:43:07.955
this gets played out in unconscious ways,
like the constant denigration of someone.

01:43:08.345 --> 01:43:13.225
A person isn't saying inside, gosh, I'm
trying to reduce their agency, their

01:43:13.225 --> 01:43:15.065
aggressive drive, really down to zero.

01:43:15.745 --> 01:43:16.805
But that's what they're doing.

01:43:16.805 --> 01:43:20.984
Somewhere inside, there's a knowing of
that, even if it's an unconscious knowing.

01:43:21.385 --> 01:43:28.124
And then the impact of that kind of
abuse over time is a lower and lower and

01:43:28.124 --> 01:43:33.635
lower ability to bring oneself to bear,
less proactive, aggressive, assertive,

01:43:34.125 --> 01:43:36.145
lower sense of agency and gratitude.

01:43:36.755 --> 01:43:40.235
That's really the definition
of being demoralized.

01:43:40.905 --> 01:43:46.145
And that kind of abuse is always promoting
demoralization, because demoralization

01:43:46.165 --> 01:43:47.665
is the darkness on the inside.

01:43:48.625 --> 01:43:53.765
Just as envy is too, demoralization
is a form of darkness on the inside.

01:43:53.985 --> 01:43:55.775
Envy is no less dark.

01:43:55.785 --> 01:44:01.055
Envy may be a lot more active,
but in both cases, there's no

01:44:01.055 --> 01:44:04.264
knowledge, there's no growth, there's
no wisdom, there's no learning.

01:44:04.274 --> 01:44:06.835
So they're the states of darkness.

01:44:07.245 --> 01:44:11.265
And what can happen in a way that's
really so tragic to think about is you

01:44:11.265 --> 01:44:18.425
often can have an abuser, an oppressor,
who is living in total darkness inside,

01:44:18.425 --> 01:44:21.615
living through the lens of envy.

01:44:21.735 --> 01:44:25.715
And then you can have a person who is
being oppressed, who is being exploited,

01:44:25.935 --> 01:44:31.325
who is living in darkness, but they're
living in the darkness of demoralization.

01:44:31.335 --> 01:44:36.465
And that's a very sad thing to
say, to imagine, and it's been a

01:44:36.465 --> 01:44:38.504
very, very sad thing to see, right?

01:44:38.784 --> 01:44:43.464
If you do clinical psychiatry for
long enough, you see a lot of this,

01:44:43.504 --> 01:44:45.475
and it doesn't have to be this way.

01:44:45.975 --> 01:44:49.995
Even the people who are oppressing
have it within them the majority

01:44:49.995 --> 01:44:51.415
of time to make things better.

01:44:51.685 --> 01:44:52.765
And we do see that.

01:44:52.765 --> 01:44:57.865
I took care of a man a long time ago
who had been terribly abusive to his

01:44:57.865 --> 01:45:02.315
family and it was always unclear to
me, it had happened years before that

01:45:03.855 --> 01:45:09.975
he just understood, and again, I never
understood what happened, that he got

01:45:09.994 --> 01:45:12.894
it and the man really made change again.

01:45:13.124 --> 01:45:15.955
I don't know what the circumstance was
that made that motivation to him, but he

01:45:15.955 --> 01:45:20.545
went back and looked at himself, and he
went back and looked at how his father

01:45:20.655 --> 01:45:25.415
oppressed and terrorized the family
and how he just did it automatically.

01:45:25.645 --> 01:45:29.254
Because what was rooted in his own
fear of vulnerability, and he's going

01:45:29.255 --> 01:45:32.035
to lose his family and not be a man
anymore unless he oppresses them.

01:45:32.475 --> 01:45:37.475
He recognized all of it in him, and
he had changed it so dramatically.

01:45:37.475 --> 01:45:39.685
He had removed himself
from the family system.

01:45:39.985 --> 01:45:44.475
And when I got to know him, it was
years later and he was reintegrating

01:45:44.505 --> 01:45:48.315
back into the system because there
had been such prolonged change.

01:45:48.555 --> 01:45:52.245
And he had communicated to the
family his understanding of self.

01:45:52.565 --> 01:45:54.505
So, it's not impossible.

01:45:54.625 --> 01:45:57.765
Even someone who is abusing,
oppressing, coming through a

01:45:57.765 --> 01:45:59.425
narcissistic character structure.

01:45:59.825 --> 01:46:02.165
There's no therapeutic nihilism here.

01:46:02.635 --> 01:46:06.064
Things can change and things can
change in the oppressor, which

01:46:06.105 --> 01:46:08.305
isn't excusing that, right?

01:46:08.305 --> 01:46:11.604
I mean, a person who's doing
things is morally culpable.

01:46:12.495 --> 01:46:14.535
They often are criminally,
legally culpable.

01:46:14.535 --> 01:46:18.705
So that is all true, just as t
he ability to change is true.

01:46:18.705 --> 01:46:22.985
And then the person we think about more
commonly in this situation, of course,

01:46:22.994 --> 01:46:24.615
is the person who's being oppressed.

01:46:24.905 --> 01:46:27.045
And there can be change there, too.

01:46:27.335 --> 01:46:30.205
But a problem is how hard it can be.

01:46:30.364 --> 01:46:36.875
The impact of the isolation, and also the
dearth of resources to really help people.

01:46:36.875 --> 01:46:42.245
It's an actual true story of a woman
who I believed with all my heart could

01:46:42.255 --> 01:46:47.435
get out of the cyclic abusive situation
she was in if she had a carburetor.

01:46:48.395 --> 01:46:49.985
A carburetor, right.

01:46:50.395 --> 01:46:51.095
She had a car.

01:46:51.555 --> 01:46:52.685
The car didn't function.

01:46:52.765 --> 01:46:57.465
She had a place to go where others
wouldn't know that she was there.

01:46:57.755 --> 01:47:01.645
And the problem was just a few hundred
dollars to change the carburetor.

01:47:01.645 --> 01:47:03.104
But it's like there's no place to go.

01:47:03.105 --> 01:47:04.214
There's no resources.

01:47:04.245 --> 01:47:06.965
We as a society don't help people.

01:47:06.965 --> 01:47:10.275
Now, it may be that someone helped
that woman and got her a carburetor

01:47:10.275 --> 01:47:11.925
and she drove away, right?

01:47:11.925 --> 01:47:16.245
But it shows how we can bring
ourselves to bear as a society to

01:47:16.265 --> 01:47:21.394
offer people help, which sometimes is
like the lifeline the person needs.

01:47:21.395 --> 01:47:25.195
And maybe that person really needs
a safe house or something very

01:47:25.195 --> 01:47:29.445
dramatic, or they need a carburetor
to get away from being terrorized.

01:47:29.655 --> 01:47:34.275
But a lot of times it's just community
support structures, good structures

01:47:34.565 --> 01:47:38.985
around people in communities that can
offer them support in situations that

01:47:38.985 --> 01:47:42.665
may be less dramatic, opportunities
for interconnection, which sometimes

01:47:42.665 --> 01:47:47.515
people can find through social venues or
through religiously affiliated venues.

01:47:47.765 --> 01:47:51.175
But the idea that community
support systems on all levels

01:47:51.505 --> 01:47:52.855
make a huge difference.

01:47:54.244 --> 01:47:56.395
There's another level, right,
beyond the relationship, right?

01:47:56.395 --> 01:47:58.615
It's the society, it's the culture.

01:47:59.014 --> 01:48:03.045
And that's how we, on that next level
of emergence, beyond the individual

01:48:03.045 --> 01:48:08.885
relationships, can foster goodness, can
foster health in each and every one of us.

01:48:10.095 --> 01:48:12.284
Andrew Huberman: How do some
of these same dynamics play out

01:48:12.295 --> 01:48:14.194
in non-romantic relationships?

01:48:14.525 --> 01:48:20.075
So, for instance, in the workplace, I
was weaned in academic laboratories, so

01:48:20.075 --> 01:48:25.375
what's most familiar to me are, gosh,
unfortunately, numerous examples where

01:48:25.615 --> 01:48:29.065
people working in laboratories, not the
same as mine, because I've been very

01:48:29.065 --> 01:48:34.835
fortunate to have amazing benevolent
mentors, quirky and outrageous at

01:48:34.874 --> 01:48:36.994
times, but benevolent nonetheless.

01:48:37.435 --> 01:48:41.015
But others around me have been in
laboratories where, for instance, the

01:48:41.015 --> 01:48:43.505
workload was just ridiculously high.

01:48:43.505 --> 01:48:46.704
Like the demand far exceeded
what any person could do.

01:48:46.704 --> 01:48:52.614
And if somebody had, God forbid,
a cold or children, it was near

01:48:52.614 --> 01:48:55.275
impossible to impossible that they
could meet the standard there.

01:48:56.125 --> 01:49:03.055
Or stress dynamics, so pressure
cooker dynamics, that made it what

01:49:03.135 --> 01:49:04.905
anyone would call a toxic environment.

01:49:05.525 --> 01:49:07.274
You see this also in law firms.

01:49:07.275 --> 01:49:08.475
You see it in companies.

01:49:08.475 --> 01:49:09.645
You see it in families.

01:49:09.645 --> 01:49:11.975
You see it in friendship circles, right?

01:49:11.975 --> 01:49:17.585
I mean, how many movies are about
teens oppressing one another through

01:49:17.595 --> 01:49:23.665
bullying and ridicule and practical
jokes that are anything but funny, right,

01:49:23.865 --> 01:49:25.395
that are downright destructive, okay?

01:49:25.435 --> 01:49:26.425
And on and on.

01:49:27.185 --> 01:49:31.255
And so often the victims in these
cases, if it's not a Hollywood movie,

01:49:32.615 --> 01:49:35.095
feel as if that's their only choice.

01:49:35.185 --> 01:49:40.375
Because to leave is to essentially
have no other options, right?

01:49:40.405 --> 01:49:42.975
It's not that the only option is to stay.

01:49:42.975 --> 01:49:45.305
It's that to leave is
essentially to leave science.

01:49:46.585 --> 01:49:49.305
Because these people in positions
of power have the louder

01:49:49.305 --> 01:49:50.605
voice, they have the megaphone.

01:49:50.645 --> 01:49:53.475
They write the letters of
recommendation, the law firms.

01:49:53.475 --> 01:49:54.385
People talk.

01:49:56.055 --> 01:50:00.505
As I kind of spool this out in a
long form question, I'm realizing I

01:50:00.505 --> 01:50:02.034
can't think of a single exception.

01:50:02.305 --> 01:50:06.084
Like, as long as there are going to be
people interacting and people talking

01:50:06.325 --> 01:50:10.955
about their interactions and people
in positions of power, this sort of

01:50:10.955 --> 01:50:12.885
dynamic is going to take place, right?

01:50:13.185 --> 01:50:13.555
Paul Conti: Absolutely.

01:50:13.555 --> 01:50:18.655
Anytime you have a closed system without
accountability, you're just rolling

01:50:18.655 --> 01:50:21.545
the dice for that kind of oppression.

01:50:22.415 --> 01:50:23.735
We need accountability.

01:50:24.075 --> 01:50:26.465
Think about theoretically what
should have happened there.

01:50:26.725 --> 01:50:30.615
There should be higher order
accountability, whether it's in a company

01:50:30.615 --> 01:50:32.495
or a university or wherever it may be.

01:50:32.805 --> 01:50:35.265
There should be higher
order accountability that

01:50:35.275 --> 01:50:36.695
is reasonable and rational.

01:50:36.925 --> 01:50:40.915
So it's not then acting in a top down
way that would be over controlling,

01:50:41.325 --> 01:50:44.275
telling exactly what you can and can't
say or what you can and can't do.

01:50:44.275 --> 01:50:48.375
And sometimes there's too much
rigidity that c an be enacted top down.

01:50:48.715 --> 01:50:50.495
But the problem runs both ways.

01:50:50.744 --> 01:50:54.795
If there's no accountability from
the bottom up, then that person

01:50:54.815 --> 01:50:57.625
is just simply stuck, right?

01:50:57.625 --> 01:50:57.975
Why?

01:50:57.975 --> 01:51:01.395
Because the system we've
put into place has failed.

01:51:01.875 --> 01:51:05.705
And this is where we're talking
now about not just individual

01:51:05.705 --> 01:51:07.565
relationships, two people relationships.

01:51:07.735 --> 01:51:09.935
But now we start talking
about systems of people.

01:51:09.935 --> 01:51:14.374
And systems of people are another level
of emergence with a personality, so to

01:51:14.374 --> 01:51:16.025
speak, an environment all their own.

01:51:16.235 --> 01:51:19.025
You can know everything there is to
know about each person in the system.

01:51:19.055 --> 01:51:23.135
You don't know about the system,
but what you do know is that

01:51:23.135 --> 01:51:25.665
accountability is necessary.

01:51:25.705 --> 01:51:30.795
I mean, probably if we added up all
the examples in history, we'd have

01:51:30.795 --> 01:51:32.644
to talk about them over 1000 years.

01:51:32.985 --> 01:51:37.215
How many examples do we have that
closed systems without accountability

01:51:37.495 --> 01:51:38.715
are just rolling the dice?

01:51:38.715 --> 01:51:40.595
They breed oppression.

01:51:40.795 --> 01:51:42.395
This is true.

01:51:42.395 --> 01:51:43.865
And I've been at several different places.

01:51:43.865 --> 01:51:48.525
So I'm not trying to implicate this
any one place versus another, but I

01:51:48.525 --> 01:51:50.534
was part of a medical treatment team.

01:51:50.614 --> 01:51:55.025
So this is a hierarchical medical
treatment team of maybe seven people,

01:51:55.225 --> 01:51:58.805
give or take one, depending upon the
circumstances, where there was physical

01:51:58.805 --> 01:52:00.725
abuse going on in the treatment team.

01:52:01.815 --> 01:52:08.595
It's true, and I'm not saying this in some
exaggerated manner where somebody brushed

01:52:08.595 --> 01:52:10.305
up too close to someone and they slipped.

01:52:10.565 --> 01:52:12.885
No, hurting someone on the treatment team.

01:52:14.145 --> 01:52:19.575
Two different people who are being
hurt by someone, seniors at a major

01:52:19.575 --> 01:52:21.485
university in a clinical setting.

01:52:22.345 --> 01:52:26.755
Again, it's not like that's the be all and
end all, but you think about the alleged.

01:52:26.755 --> 01:52:30.235
The sophistication of the people in
the system, the alleged empowerment

01:52:30.255 --> 01:52:34.425
of the people in the situation,
which did not put a stop to that.

01:52:34.445 --> 01:52:37.135
So we need to have accountability.

01:52:37.175 --> 01:52:39.455
The accountability has to be reasonable.

01:52:39.455 --> 01:52:43.375
It doesn't mean over control, but it
doesn't mean under control either.

01:52:43.624 --> 01:52:47.635
And if that person is being overworked,
as you described, with no way of

01:52:47.635 --> 01:52:54.325
winning, or harmed on a medical team,
and there's no way that that person can

01:52:54.325 --> 01:52:58.445
change that, the system has failed that
person and failed them dramatically.

01:52:58.445 --> 01:52:59.454
And guess who suffers?

01:52:59.994 --> 01:53:01.404
Everyone does, right?

01:53:01.744 --> 01:53:04.064
That person suffers.

01:53:04.315 --> 01:53:08.005
And the science that's being done
or the medical care that's being

01:53:08.005 --> 01:53:10.135
provided is important to all of us.

01:53:10.245 --> 01:53:16.134
Anyone could be the patient who is being
taken care of by the team, where one

01:53:16.135 --> 01:53:17.925
person is hurting a couple of the others.

01:53:17.925 --> 01:53:20.664
You think that medical care is
going to be optimized, right?

01:53:20.815 --> 01:53:25.025
Is the science going to be optimized, that
we're utilizing to try and make our lives

01:53:25.025 --> 01:53:27.005
better, live longer, be in less pain?

01:53:27.725 --> 01:53:29.345
What's happening there is envy.

01:53:29.355 --> 01:53:33.035
That person who's oppressing other
people is driven by envy, whether

01:53:33.035 --> 01:53:36.915
it's a narcissistic character
structure that acts almost exclusively

01:53:37.204 --> 01:53:41.065
through envy, or it's envy that is
in this person's life, in this way

01:53:41.065 --> 01:53:42.775
or not another way, does it matter?

01:53:43.385 --> 01:53:45.105
What's going on there is envy.

01:53:45.135 --> 01:53:47.115
And envy is nothing but destructive.

01:53:47.605 --> 01:53:50.295
The generative drive is
nothing but productive.

01:53:50.755 --> 01:53:53.195
Envy is nothing but destructive.

01:53:53.624 --> 01:53:55.675
Andrew Huberman: Could you remind,
for people that perhaps may not

01:53:55.815 --> 01:54:02.485
have heard episodes one and two
yet, that envy is perhaps not just

01:54:02.485 --> 01:54:04.665
a desire to be like somebody else?

01:54:04.705 --> 01:54:07.035
That envy, in the context
that we're discussing here, is

01:54:07.075 --> 01:54:09.655
something quite possibly different?

01:54:09.704 --> 01:54:12.285
Paul Conti: Right, and there's a
varying lexicon, which is why it is

01:54:12.285 --> 01:54:15.275
important to define it, because I think
people learn it in different ways.

01:54:15.275 --> 01:54:16.854
I've heard it talked
about in different ways.

01:54:17.844 --> 01:54:22.055
So, to define that, there's a
difference between jealousy and envy.

01:54:22.055 --> 01:54:23.990
And again, we could choose
different words, but this is the

01:54:23.990 --> 01:54:29.045
way I learned it that's been most
impactful, where jealousy is benign.

01:54:29.425 --> 01:54:33.045
It's the idea that if I see t hat
you have something that I don't,

01:54:33.284 --> 01:54:36.075
then I think, oh, well, maybe I
could work harder and get that thing.

01:54:36.565 --> 01:54:40.785
Or if I can't have that thing,
like maybe a person is younger.

01:54:41.025 --> 01:54:43.285
Okay, I can't make myself
younger, then I can think.

01:54:43.535 --> 01:54:45.745
Okay, through a lens of gratitude.

01:54:45.745 --> 01:54:47.345
Is that the only thing about me?

01:54:47.685 --> 01:54:49.765
I can't think of anything
good about myself, right?

01:54:49.815 --> 01:54:51.194
Other than maybe I could be younger.

01:54:51.195 --> 01:54:56.325
I mean, if you come at that through the
lens of health, it's like, it's okay.

01:54:56.905 --> 01:55:00.745
It can serve to motivate people to try
harder, work harder, take a look at

01:55:00.745 --> 01:55:04.855
themselves and be more accepting of who
they are and what their circumstances

01:55:04.855 --> 01:55:09.035
in life are, and what kind of control
they can enact and what kind they can't.

01:55:09.454 --> 01:55:10.415
It's okay.

01:55:11.534 --> 01:55:13.485
Envy is different.

01:55:14.345 --> 01:55:22.005
Envy comes at that problem from
the perspective that bringing

01:55:22.015 --> 01:55:27.725
down the other person is just as
effective as bringing up the self.

01:55:27.805 --> 01:55:30.985
That's why envy is destructive.

01:55:31.255 --> 01:55:39.785
So someone who might see another person
and they envy them their youth, right?

01:55:40.175 --> 01:55:40.775
They envy.

01:55:40.775 --> 01:55:44.910
They want that, and they want to
bring that person down because

01:55:45.185 --> 01:55:46.565
I can't make myself younger.

01:55:46.565 --> 01:55:48.985
But then they realize I can't
make that person older either.

01:55:49.255 --> 01:55:51.595
But they can do other
things to them, right?

01:55:51.795 --> 01:55:53.175
They can sexually harass them.

01:55:53.545 --> 01:55:57.765
Maybe they can make terrible
jokes that are really insulting

01:55:57.765 --> 01:55:59.785
and humiliating, maybe.

01:55:59.785 --> 01:56:03.675
There are all sorts of things a person
can do to bring down someone else.

01:56:03.675 --> 01:56:06.695
The inaction of envy is destructive.

01:56:07.135 --> 01:56:12.205
And I truly believe this, that
people who come at the world very

01:56:12.205 --> 01:56:16.155
strongly through envy, by and large,
a narcissistic character structure.

01:56:16.515 --> 01:56:21.595
This is a small percentage of the
population, but that small percentage

01:56:21.885 --> 01:56:25.015
does most of the damage on Earth.

01:56:25.425 --> 01:56:30.635
It's a strong thing to say, but when I
think about studying political science

01:56:30.655 --> 01:56:34.395
and thinking about history and learning
medicine and learning about psychiatry

01:56:34.395 --> 01:56:40.304
and sociology and really trying to look
at the world and thinking, what drives

01:56:40.594 --> 01:56:45.385
a person on a medical team who's gotten
to be a senior physician to physically

01:56:45.395 --> 01:56:47.715
hurt doctors lower on the hierarchy?

01:56:47.795 --> 01:56:48.295
Envy.

01:56:49.085 --> 01:56:51.475
That person feels terrible about
themselves and then is being

01:56:51.475 --> 01:56:53.265
destructive to those people.

01:56:53.275 --> 01:56:53.825
Same thing.

01:56:53.825 --> 01:56:56.225
That same thing is at work in the lab.

01:56:56.235 --> 01:56:59.914
The same thing is at work when
people start wars of destruction

01:57:00.124 --> 01:57:02.105
that just simply harm other people.

01:57:02.105 --> 01:57:04.105
And we can see no other sense of it.

01:57:04.115 --> 01:57:09.245
That from the individual setting
all the way up to the world setting,

01:57:09.255 --> 01:57:11.035
we see the destruction of envy.

01:57:11.235 --> 01:57:15.415
And we also see it inside of us,
because a person who's enacting envy in

01:57:15.415 --> 01:57:17.684
the world around them is never happy.

01:57:17.694 --> 01:57:19.175
There's no chance of happiness.

01:57:19.625 --> 01:57:21.685
Hence the idea of a bottomless pit.

01:57:22.295 --> 01:57:25.985
Whatever you get, let's say an
envious person who wants more money.

01:57:25.995 --> 01:57:27.534
I want to have more money than anybody.

01:57:27.545 --> 01:57:32.585
I want everyone to have less money
than me/ So then they come at the

01:57:32.585 --> 01:57:33.865
world t hrough the lens of greed.

01:57:34.505 --> 01:57:37.235
When they get more money, how
long does that make them happy?

01:57:37.685 --> 01:57:37.975
Right?

01:57:37.975 --> 01:57:40.205
This doesn't mean money is
bad or having money is bad.

01:57:40.395 --> 01:57:44.025
It just means if you're coming at
the world through the lens of envy,

01:57:44.025 --> 01:57:47.764
and that lens is specifically focused
on money, then you will be a greedy

01:57:47.765 --> 01:57:51.045
person who is never satisfied, even
if you have 10 trillion dollars.

01:57:51.864 --> 01:57:53.164
So it's never good.

01:57:53.174 --> 01:57:57.625
It takes away from that person
any possibility of happiness.

01:57:57.905 --> 01:58:01.375
And if you see people work with
people with a narcissistic character

01:58:01.375 --> 01:58:06.095
structure, there can be a sense of a
very brief happiness in the moment.

01:58:06.155 --> 01:58:09.465
Like, I'm happy because I realize I
have something someone else doesn't.

01:58:09.465 --> 01:58:11.635
I'm happy because I'm thinking of myself.

01:58:11.645 --> 01:58:15.165
And even though I feel very insecure
and vulnerable inside, I have a

01:58:15.165 --> 01:58:18.635
whole set of defense mechanisms
that let me turn those tables around

01:58:18.965 --> 01:58:22.275
and then feel good about myself in
a way that places me above others.

01:58:22.275 --> 01:58:27.455
Whatever's going on in that person, it
may bring some very brief gratification

01:58:27.455 --> 01:58:29.774
in the moment, but that's not happiness.

01:58:29.784 --> 01:58:33.425
Hence the need for the gratification
over and over and over again.

01:58:33.725 --> 01:58:39.355
Narcissistic people are the least
secure, most definite people on Earth.

01:58:39.685 --> 01:58:44.655
They just have a phenomenally healthy
defensive structure that comes about

01:58:44.664 --> 01:58:46.455
in order to try and protect them.

01:58:46.715 --> 01:58:49.845
That leads them to go to the opposite,
some of what's called a reaction

01:58:49.845 --> 01:58:54.085
formation, going to the very opposite,
denial, avoidance, rationalization,

01:58:54.085 --> 01:59:00.245
projection, very unhealthy defenses
that then leads that person to protect

01:59:00.245 --> 01:59:05.765
themselves from any help while they are
frantically trying to gain some goodness

01:59:05.784 --> 01:59:09.445
that makes them feel good for a split
second and then disappears forever.

01:59:09.475 --> 01:59:13.245
Hence the tremendous
predilection for destruction.

01:59:14.145 --> 01:59:16.015
Andrew Huberman: How do you,
and how should we, think about

01:59:16.465 --> 01:59:18.455
power dynamics in relationships?

01:59:18.715 --> 01:59:21.475
And perhaps starting with
romantic relationships?

01:59:21.815 --> 01:59:25.085
I've heard it said before that
there's always power dynamics

01:59:25.085 --> 01:59:26.695
in relationships of all kinds.

01:59:26.845 --> 01:59:30.745
I don't know if that's true or
not, but I've heard that, and I've

01:59:30.745 --> 01:59:34.194
also heard that that's particularly
salient in romantic relationships,

01:59:34.355 --> 01:59:37.795
independent of whether or not it's a
homosexual or heterosexual relationship.

01:59:37.795 --> 01:59:41.845
That there's always, to some extent or
another, one leader and one follower.

01:59:42.735 --> 01:59:48.115
This is an interesting, perhaps
a controversial idea, but I've

01:59:48.115 --> 01:59:50.885
heard it enough times that I
want to know more about it.

01:59:51.195 --> 01:59:51.695
Paul Conti: Sure.

01:59:52.445 --> 01:59:57.625
Power dynamics, of course, are very
important, but also something we tend

01:59:57.685 --> 01:59:59.424
to be so over reductionist about.

01:59:59.424 --> 02:00:01.435
I mean, this idea, think about
that, that there's always a leader

02:00:01.435 --> 02:00:02.545
and there's always a follower.

02:00:02.675 --> 02:00:04.485
Every single relationship, right?

02:00:04.745 --> 02:00:09.505
That's true o n balance, we tend
to be so reductionist, and then

02:00:09.515 --> 02:00:13.435
what we do is we miss the real
power dynamics that are going on.

02:00:13.675 --> 02:00:17.284
Just like I had said in a previous
episode, that I think my math minor

02:00:17.295 --> 02:00:19.535
has helped me the most for all of life.

02:00:20.215 --> 02:00:23.455
I think a power dynamics course
that I took as an undergraduate

02:00:23.455 --> 02:00:27.534
in political science has helped
me the most as a psychiatrist.

02:00:27.534 --> 02:00:31.035
Took it long before medical
school, because that class taught

02:00:31.035 --> 02:00:33.055
me so much about power dynamics.

02:00:33.085 --> 02:00:36.345
I thought it was going to be about
overt power, who has power over

02:00:36.355 --> 02:00:37.985
whom and tells whom to do what.

02:00:38.025 --> 02:00:38.495
Right?

02:00:38.495 --> 02:00:42.265
But what I learned is that so
much of power dynamics are covert.

02:00:42.305 --> 02:00:45.415
They're under the surface, for example,
they're the things that are not said.

02:00:45.835 --> 02:00:49.055
Something that was called at the time,
at least, the issue of the non issue,

02:00:49.704 --> 02:00:51.985
where there's an issue between two people.

02:00:51.994 --> 02:00:54.655
Like, this person never
takes out the garbage.

02:00:54.665 --> 02:00:56.195
That person always takes out the garbage.

02:00:56.195 --> 02:00:58.375
That person is resentful about it.

02:00:58.715 --> 02:00:59.715
Both of them know.

02:00:59.765 --> 02:01:03.135
But the person who always does it can't
say so, because if they say so, the

02:01:03.135 --> 02:01:07.484
first person will punish them in some
other way by not taking out the garbage.

02:01:07.485 --> 02:01:09.825
Then the place smells, that person
goes to work, and the person

02:01:09.825 --> 02:01:11.124
who's at home has to take it out.

02:01:11.364 --> 02:01:11.864
Right.

02:01:12.024 --> 02:01:15.705
That's one example of the
issue of the non issue.

02:01:15.935 --> 02:01:20.635
Other examples are where someone is
looking and smiling and being really

02:01:20.635 --> 02:01:25.025
nice to someone who they know is going
to physically harm them if they bring

02:01:25.025 --> 02:01:28.624
to light that they're actually not happy
and don't feel good about that person.

02:01:29.065 --> 02:01:33.235
So, from the dramatic to the
nondramatic, unstated power dynamics

02:01:33.475 --> 02:01:35.245
are going on all over the place.

02:01:35.245 --> 02:01:37.175
And they're in every single relationship.

02:01:37.495 --> 02:01:39.745
I mean, less a person is in a
relationship with someone else

02:01:39.745 --> 02:01:40.885
and they're both comatose.

02:01:41.695 --> 02:01:43.235
I guess only one of
them has to be comatose.

02:01:43.855 --> 02:01:47.194
There are power dynamics going
on, but again, it doesn't mean

02:01:47.194 --> 02:01:51.005
that they're unhealthy, because
there are always power dynamics.

02:01:51.235 --> 02:01:53.105
So then what are we looking for?

02:01:53.305 --> 02:01:56.095
And again, there are a lot of things
we could look for, but we could talk

02:01:56.105 --> 02:01:58.345
about, really two primary things.

02:01:58.704 --> 02:02:04.884
One is, look for the nonobvious,
and t wo is the give and take.

02:02:05.444 --> 02:02:07.955
So the first is even in
our own relationships.

02:02:08.235 --> 02:02:11.885
Because it's interesting how many
people will, say, talk about the

02:02:11.895 --> 02:02:13.695
power dynamics in their relationship.

02:02:13.705 --> 02:02:16.955
They're not always saying, here are
the power dynamics in my relationship,

02:02:17.215 --> 02:02:18.675
but they're telling me about them.

02:02:19.135 --> 02:02:21.665
And they're telling me about
the things that are overt.

02:02:22.124 --> 02:02:25.605
Right now, somewhere inside of them,
they know that they can't really raise

02:02:25.625 --> 02:02:29.585
issue A, B, or C, or there'll be some
retribution, whether it's small or large.

02:02:29.795 --> 02:02:33.094
Or they might even know everything's
okay and that person is happy.

02:02:33.095 --> 02:02:35.754
But they know I'm not giving them
room to say that they're not happy.

02:02:35.965 --> 02:02:40.105
And that could just mean if they say
they're not happy, then I'll come home

02:02:40.114 --> 02:02:41.864
a couple of hours late the next day.

02:02:41.864 --> 02:02:44.555
And that person will feel some
attachment insecurity because of that.

02:02:44.555 --> 02:02:47.675
And there are all sorts of
ways t his can play out.

02:02:47.905 --> 02:02:54.285
But since so much of power dynamics are
unstated or covert, kind of like the

02:02:54.285 --> 02:02:57.665
iceberg of conscious and unconscious,
there's an iceberg of power dynamics.

02:02:57.895 --> 02:03:01.775
So, to think about including in one's own
relationships, whether it's a neighbor,

02:03:01.775 --> 02:03:05.675
it's a friend, it's a work relationship,
or most charged, it's romance.

02:03:06.145 --> 02:03:07.715
What's really going on between us?

02:03:08.664 --> 02:03:10.035
What's really going on between us?

02:03:10.035 --> 02:03:13.824
I'm not looking to tell myself lies.

02:03:13.835 --> 02:03:17.004
I know that I may not be able
to understand all of it, but

02:03:17.005 --> 02:03:18.375
let me stop and think about it.

02:03:18.635 --> 02:03:21.575
And if you ask people to stop
and do that, they can say,

02:03:22.915 --> 02:03:24.495
yeah, that's really not okay.

02:03:25.235 --> 02:03:28.125
Because sometimes they don't know
that it's there, or they don't

02:03:28.125 --> 02:03:29.095
want to know that it's there.

02:03:29.095 --> 02:03:32.254
And the therapy work is
trying to guide them to.

02:03:32.265 --> 02:03:35.884
There's an immense power disparity,
maybe in the relationship when they're

02:03:35.885 --> 02:03:37.185
presenting, no, everything is equal.

02:03:37.185 --> 02:03:38.835
It's a good, supportive relationship.

02:03:38.855 --> 02:03:39.895
Hear that a lot?

02:03:40.255 --> 02:03:42.615
And of course, it's not always that
there's something bad under the

02:03:42.615 --> 02:03:44.372
surface, but a lot of times there is.

02:03:44.372 --> 02:03:47.885
And even something mildly bad is
not okay and can cause problems.

02:03:48.145 --> 02:03:49.805
And sometimes there's something very bad.

02:03:49.805 --> 02:03:51.835
So look for the nonobvious.

02:03:51.835 --> 02:03:56.935
And then the second is that give and
take is a very good sign of health.

02:03:57.545 --> 02:03:58.590
Is a very good sign of health.

02:03:58.645 --> 02:04:02.105
So if a person can kind of see that
there's give and take, and it might

02:04:02.105 --> 02:04:03.155
be about something as simple as.

02:04:03.155 --> 02:04:04.445
Like, who chooses where we go to dinner?

02:04:04.994 --> 02:04:07.135
Okay, sometimes one, sometimes the other.

02:04:07.135 --> 02:04:08.675
It depends who has a stronger feeling.

02:04:08.735 --> 02:04:11.595
Or it may be that person always
decides where we go to dinner.

02:04:11.745 --> 02:04:14.785
But the other person's okay with it,
and that person always decides what

02:04:14.815 --> 02:04:16.215
movie they want, the other person.

02:04:16.805 --> 02:04:22.515
So the idea that there's give and
take, and then in periods of time where

02:04:22.845 --> 02:04:26.385
one person may be in a more difficult
place, one person has a significant

02:04:26.385 --> 02:04:30.555
loss or an injury or an illness, then
you see that that shifts a little bit.

02:04:30.575 --> 02:04:32.745
One person is giving more, right?

02:04:32.955 --> 02:04:36.515
But then, ultimately the idea
is that's from the outside.

02:04:36.735 --> 02:04:40.974
If one person is giving more to the
other, there's a generosity of spirit in

02:04:40.985 --> 02:04:46.065
both the giving and the accepting that
leads them to then be stronger together.

02:04:46.315 --> 02:04:49.524
So even when people say, well,
they're imbalances in some way.

02:04:49.525 --> 02:04:53.075
If you just look at what's going on day
to day, but in a healthy relationship

02:04:53.075 --> 02:04:58.165
with high generative drive, the periods
of imbalance strengthen the relationship.

02:04:58.454 --> 02:05:01.265
You see this with friends, where
two p eople have a pretty equal

02:05:01.265 --> 02:05:04.575
friendship, and t hen one person
has something difficult happen and

02:05:04.665 --> 02:05:06.024
the other person is there for them.

02:05:06.194 --> 02:05:07.895
What's true on the other side of that?

02:05:07.895 --> 02:05:09.015
They're better friends.

02:05:09.785 --> 02:05:13.065
And it's also why we often want to
be interconnected when we're healthy.

02:05:13.345 --> 02:05:15.085
What if something bad
happens to both of them?

02:05:15.725 --> 02:05:17.795
It's good to be interconnected,
and friends a nd family

02:05:17.795 --> 02:05:19.555
can be supportive to us.

02:05:20.565 --> 02:05:24.484
The idea that give and take is
healthy, I think, is very central.

02:05:24.755 --> 02:05:28.425
So just looking for evidence, it's often,
if I don't understand what's going on

02:05:28.425 --> 02:05:31.605
in the relationship, maybe it's early
in t he therapy or it's just been kind

02:05:31.605 --> 02:05:33.245
o f opaque, or I can't figure it out.

02:05:33.555 --> 02:05:35.415
I'm looking for, is there a give and take?

02:05:35.415 --> 02:05:38.634
Because then I'm going to think, okay,
more likely than not, things are healthy.

02:05:39.195 --> 02:05:42.455
If I see an imbalance, whether the
person knows it or not, I'm thinking,

02:05:42.475 --> 02:05:44.095
more likely than not, it's unhealthy.

02:05:44.345 --> 02:05:49.635
And then they're just clues along the
pathway of my efforts to understand.

02:05:51.225 --> 02:05:53.965
Andrew Huberman: The non-obvious
piece is really intriguing,

02:05:54.005 --> 02:05:55.134
as is the give and take.

02:05:55.134 --> 02:05:58.694
And I really appreciate that you brought
up the give and take, because that's a

02:05:58.694 --> 02:06:03.635
very concrete place that we can all look
and ask ourselves, even if people aren't

02:06:03.755 --> 02:06:07.915
in romantic relationships, like, what is
the give and take in a given friendship?

02:06:08.664 --> 02:06:11.055
And as you mentioned earlier,
it doesn't have to be scripted.

02:06:11.055 --> 02:06:11.764
One for one.

02:06:11.765 --> 02:06:12.524
One for one.

02:06:13.324 --> 02:06:15.515
Maybe it balances out over
time, or maybe it doesn't.

02:06:15.555 --> 02:06:22.225
I mean, I've had friendships that have
lasted many decades, even where I can

02:06:22.225 --> 02:06:26.065
honestly say I'm always the person
to reach out to the other person but

02:06:26.085 --> 02:06:31.235
when they connect, they connect with
such a depth of attention that I

02:06:31.244 --> 02:06:33.045
don't feel any deprivation whatsoever.

02:06:33.095 --> 02:06:34.814
In fact, I don't think
I've ever considered that.

02:06:34.815 --> 02:06:37.325
I'm the person that always
reaches out until today, and so

02:06:37.325 --> 02:06:38.545
it doesn't bother me whatsoever.

02:06:38.545 --> 02:06:41.995
I feel infinitely rewarded
in the relationship.

02:06:42.175 --> 02:06:44.645
Like, it's generative, it
feels generative, right?

02:06:44.685 --> 02:06:47.535
Paul Conti: It doesn't have to be
equal, however one wants to define

02:06:47.565 --> 02:06:49.795
equal, but it has to be mutual.

02:06:49.795 --> 02:06:52.465
You're feeling goodness
from the relationship.

02:06:52.755 --> 02:06:53.775
So is the other person.

02:06:54.104 --> 02:06:54.604
Okay.

02:06:55.335 --> 02:06:58.545
That's coming from that
high generative place.

02:06:58.805 --> 02:07:01.345
And when we really, let's say
we push that concept forward

02:07:01.555 --> 02:07:02.895
to where we want to be living.

02:07:02.925 --> 02:07:07.485
Not some pie in the sky, but
agency and gratitude as verbs.

02:07:07.485 --> 02:07:12.194
The generative drive is very strongly
expressed, and the other drives are

02:07:12.204 --> 02:07:13.715
subserving the generative drive.

02:07:14.045 --> 02:07:18.175
Then we get to the place where we
really see it is true that it is

02:07:18.175 --> 02:07:20.285
better to give than to receive.

02:07:21.135 --> 02:07:25.054
The happiest people I see are
the people who are giving.

02:07:25.645 --> 02:07:30.174
Now, of course, it feels great to
receive, but it feels better to

02:07:30.185 --> 02:07:33.455
give because there's a goodness
in the self, in the giving.

02:07:33.455 --> 02:07:37.839
And I remember seeing this as a child and
being too young to understand it, b ut I

02:07:37.839 --> 02:07:39.975
was a little kid, I liked getting things.

02:07:39.975 --> 02:07:42.805
And as a little kid, I liked getting
things more than I liked giving.

02:07:42.805 --> 02:07:44.125
Like, that's okay when
you're a little kid.

02:07:44.525 --> 02:07:48.865
But what I did observe is that my maternal
grandmother, who is very sweet and loving

02:07:49.275 --> 02:07:52.395
and caring, she loved giving, right?

02:07:52.395 --> 02:07:53.255
You give her presents.

02:07:53.255 --> 02:07:53.725
And we did.

02:07:53.725 --> 02:07:55.685
And she really liked getting presents.

02:07:55.965 --> 02:07:56.764
She loved giving.

02:07:57.494 --> 02:07:58.785
There was an excess of goodness.

02:07:58.785 --> 02:08:01.735
And then as I got older and I learned
more, like who she had been in the

02:08:01.735 --> 02:08:03.935
community and how she had been to people.

02:08:04.174 --> 02:08:09.235
And I could see, and now, through
the lens that there was such goodness

02:08:09.325 --> 02:08:13.055
in her, I think she's, for me, she's
the shining model of goodness that I

02:08:13.055 --> 02:08:15.525
internalize as I aspire to be better.

02:08:15.695 --> 02:08:18.195
So then I think I want to be like that.

02:08:18.195 --> 02:08:22.874
I am more generative as I try and
think more about giving than receiving.

02:08:22.985 --> 02:08:26.015
I'm not trying to say I'm some
noble person or I'm being ascetic.

02:08:26.265 --> 02:08:29.275
I'm thinking that's a good
way for u s all to feel right.

02:08:29.335 --> 02:08:36.154
Or someone, a very, very successful person
that I consult to that is in my life,

02:08:36.595 --> 02:08:42.825
who talked about how he always makes
inside of himself the best understanding

02:08:42.825 --> 02:08:45.955
of what's going on between him and
another person, whether it's a very big

02:08:46.215 --> 02:08:50.375
financial deal or it's about power or
it's personal and then gives a little bit.

02:08:50.905 --> 02:08:52.215
Always, where have we ended up?

02:08:52.835 --> 02:08:54.355
Let me give a little bit, right.

02:08:54.615 --> 02:08:57.294
And there's a person who's very,
very s uccessful, very, very happy.

02:08:58.045 --> 02:09:04.395
But I would argue the goodness in
him is why he's successful and happy.

02:09:04.415 --> 02:09:06.645
It's not that he's successful
and therefore he's happy.

02:09:06.665 --> 02:09:07.135
Right.

02:09:07.135 --> 02:09:10.455
It's what's inside of him that
fosters both of those good things.

02:09:10.455 --> 02:09:12.885
And he could be just as happy
without the big success.

02:09:12.885 --> 02:09:13.594
Maybe he wouldn't.

02:09:13.824 --> 02:09:15.834
Maybe he wasn't minded to do that.

02:09:16.085 --> 02:09:17.535
And he grows a nice garden.

02:09:17.684 --> 02:09:18.875
He could be equally happy.

02:09:18.875 --> 02:09:24.065
But the point is the goodness
in self, to be able to do that,

02:09:24.595 --> 02:09:26.135
to feel good about doing that.

02:09:26.175 --> 02:09:30.015
I feel better about giving that
than I would have receiving that.

02:09:30.034 --> 02:09:31.675
That's pretty stark, right?

02:09:31.675 --> 02:09:34.135
It's something given to the other
that I would have gotten right now,

02:09:34.395 --> 02:09:38.185
and we're in maybe a negotiation to
give it feels better than to have it.

02:09:39.105 --> 02:09:40.865
Andrew Huberman: I'm sure there
are many people thinking about

02:09:41.335 --> 02:09:46.425
individuals who are highly successful,
who are not givers, who are takers.

02:09:47.095 --> 02:09:52.735
I do think those examples of takers, as
I'm calling them, grab a lot of attention.

02:09:53.165 --> 02:09:57.115
But I know, at least within science
and the other domains of life I've

02:09:57.115 --> 02:10:02.925
been in, that there are far more
successful people who are also

02:10:02.935 --> 02:10:05.795
givers, even to a great extent.

02:10:05.845 --> 02:10:08.055
And of course, that doesn't mean
that they're giving to the point

02:10:08.055 --> 02:10:11.705
of an inability to give further
or to take care of themselves.

02:10:12.034 --> 02:10:14.925
The giving is part of taking
care of themselves, but it's

02:10:14.955 --> 02:10:17.115
part of this generative cycle.

02:10:17.365 --> 02:10:18.605
It's not one thing.

02:10:18.605 --> 02:10:19.745
It's not a tit for tat.

02:10:20.435 --> 02:10:23.744
It's part of something that makes them
feel good, makes others feel good.

02:10:24.864 --> 02:10:26.084
It's sort of anti transactional.

02:10:27.385 --> 02:10:31.225
And as we're talking about the self
and interrelations between selves

02:10:31.265 --> 02:10:35.325
in these different relationship
contexts today, this word

02:10:35.335 --> 02:10:37.085
transactional keeps coming to mind.

02:10:37.925 --> 02:10:43.820
And what I'm so aware of as you're
describing what healthy selves and

02:10:43.885 --> 02:10:48.064
healthy relationships look like, is
that it runs counter to pretty much

02:10:48.104 --> 02:10:52.095
everything that I've heard and that we
hear in the world about relationships

02:10:52.205 --> 02:10:56.045
and about the relationship to self,
meaning it's not transactional.

02:10:56.695 --> 02:10:58.545
It's really about a cycle.

02:10:58.845 --> 02:11:03.785
And we're using this word generative over
and over in its specific context today.

02:11:03.785 --> 02:11:08.645
So I don't want to rob that word for
a different purpose, but I'm imagining

02:11:08.645 --> 02:11:12.905
a sort of upward spiral in my mind,
or perhaps something that's really

02:11:12.905 --> 02:11:16.845
like a circle of life that just keeps
growing bigger and bigger and bigger.

02:11:18.534 --> 02:11:20.484
Maybe we could talk a little
bit about this notion of

02:11:20.775 --> 02:11:22.625
relationships being transactional.

02:11:22.885 --> 02:11:27.645
I mean, it's such a loaded word, but
I have a close friend who's married

02:11:27.645 --> 02:11:34.175
with more than a few children who
told me the other day, I realized

02:11:34.225 --> 02:11:36.374
that it's all kind of transactional.

02:11:36.395 --> 02:11:40.035
Like they're extracting from me and
I'm extracting from them, and it's

02:11:40.035 --> 02:11:41.345
benevolent because it's all good.

02:11:41.345 --> 02:11:44.945
And I thought, wow,
this is really dreadful.

02:11:45.655 --> 02:11:47.925
No one wants to think that
the closest relationships in

02:11:47.925 --> 02:11:49.935
their lives are transactional.

02:11:50.475 --> 02:11:51.925
And he was coming to that conclusion.

02:11:51.925 --> 02:11:54.995
I disagreed with him, and I don't know
where that all sits for him right now.

02:11:54.995 --> 02:11:58.944
But maybe we could talk about the
transactional versus non-transactional

02:11:59.524 --> 02:12:03.745
aspects of relationship,
because we all want things.

02:12:04.175 --> 02:12:05.285
That's perfectly healthy.

02:12:05.285 --> 02:12:10.385
I believe we all experience
disappointment and pleasure and relief,

02:12:10.385 --> 02:12:13.625
and sometimes major disappointment,
pleasure, relief, et cetera.

02:12:13.625 --> 02:12:17.654
But what is the role of
transaction in relationships?

02:12:18.104 --> 02:12:21.525
And how does psychiatry, how do you,
and how should we think about that?

02:12:22.595 --> 02:12:28.125
Paul Conti: Well, we often get confused
because there are transactions in every

02:12:28.215 --> 02:12:33.065
relationship, but that does not mean
that every relationship is transactional.

02:12:33.815 --> 02:12:37.125
So if we think about what
transactions are, we can think of

02:12:37.125 --> 02:12:39.185
in kind of the stereotypical way.

02:12:39.245 --> 02:12:41.875
So it can also be like,
here's the transaction.

02:12:41.915 --> 02:12:43.985
I'll do the dishes, and you
wash the clothes, right?

02:12:43.985 --> 02:12:47.015
Andrew Huberman: Or, for instance,
I'll make the money, and the other

02:12:47.015 --> 02:12:48.535
person will raise the children.

02:12:48.545 --> 02:12:49.045
Paul Conti: Right.

02:12:49.695 --> 02:12:51.505
So there are transactions.

02:12:52.034 --> 02:12:56.455
But that does not mean that the be
all and end all of it are sort of

02:12:56.525 --> 02:12:59.145
hard hearted, calculated transactions.

02:12:59.165 --> 02:13:03.855
And transactions also occur in less
obvious but equally important ways.

02:13:04.425 --> 02:13:09.977
So another way that transactions occur is
right now I'm putting something out there.

02:13:09.977 --> 02:13:11.329
Because I'm saying something, right?

02:13:11.604 --> 02:13:14.215
And then at a point, I stop
and I'm waiting for you to

02:13:14.215 --> 02:13:15.225
put something out there.

02:13:15.225 --> 02:13:18.065
And then I take in what you said,
and we're doing something that

02:13:18.075 --> 02:13:20.065
in that sense is transactional.

02:13:20.244 --> 02:13:21.615
I'm waiting for you to give me something.

02:13:21.615 --> 02:13:24.655
I take it in, I process it, I
give something back out to you.

02:13:25.045 --> 02:13:29.035
But that's not the be
all and end all of it.

02:13:29.045 --> 02:13:33.525
So there are transactions, whether it's
who's going to wash the dishes, who's

02:13:33.525 --> 02:13:36.901
going to do the clothes, or it's what d
o I put out there that you take in, a nd

02:13:36.901 --> 02:13:39.045
what do you put out there that I take in?

02:13:39.345 --> 02:13:44.024
That there is something greater than
something beyond the transactional.

02:13:44.164 --> 02:13:48.844
And there is some controversy to
this, a thought that there really is

02:13:48.874 --> 02:13:50.595
a historical thought in the field.

02:13:50.855 --> 02:13:55.545
That there are just aggressive
and pleasure drives in us, and

02:13:55.545 --> 02:13:57.605
that everything is transactional.

02:13:57.914 --> 02:14:01.385
Now, again, we don't really
have a way of disproving that.

02:14:01.385 --> 02:14:04.005
I mean, there's not going to be
some equation that disproves that.

02:14:04.694 --> 02:14:08.785
But I think it's entirely
disproven by human experience.

02:14:08.815 --> 02:14:15.125
And there's so many, we could talk
about an infinite number of resources

02:14:15.155 --> 02:14:18.535
to consider this, but just imagine
the writings of Viktor Frankl and

02:14:18.935 --> 02:14:23.605
the writings and the theories around
human interactions and psychological

02:14:23.624 --> 02:14:25.125
theories that have come of it.

02:14:25.355 --> 02:14:27.707
To think that everything is just
transactional, it's a denial of the

02:14:27.707 --> 02:14:33.505
humanness in all of us that I think he
just brought to the fore so strongly.

02:14:33.505 --> 02:14:37.614
But again, there could be nearly
infinite resources throughout

02:14:37.675 --> 02:14:40.015
human history that say, hey,
we're not entirely transactional.

02:14:40.774 --> 02:14:47.165
It's not just aggression and pleasure,
but there's something more going on here.

02:14:47.175 --> 02:14:50.135
There's learning that feels
good for the sake of learning.

02:14:50.145 --> 02:14:52.724
There's kindness that feels
good for the sake of kindness.

02:14:52.985 --> 02:14:56.374
There's giving that feels good,
because giving feels good.

02:14:56.784 --> 02:14:58.134
This is going on in us.

02:14:58.135 --> 02:15:02.085
It's going on when we're, like, loving
children, for example, or loving animals.

02:15:02.305 --> 02:15:06.615
There's something inside of us
that's not just transactional.

02:15:06.785 --> 02:15:11.945
And that's why I think what we're
talking about is, if it's truth,

02:15:11.954 --> 02:15:13.725
it should all hang together, right?

02:15:13.805 --> 02:15:14.915
It must all hang together.

02:15:14.915 --> 02:15:21.655
And this is why there's an us over top of
each individual that's in a relationship.

02:15:22.145 --> 02:15:24.635
This is why you can know
everything about me.

02:15:24.745 --> 02:15:27.615
You can know everything about you, and
you can know nothing about our friendship.

02:15:28.075 --> 02:15:29.485
Absolutely nothing, right?

02:15:29.585 --> 02:15:31.184
Because it's something different.

02:15:31.735 --> 02:15:35.414
And if it were all just transactional,
there would be nothing different, right?

02:15:35.674 --> 02:15:40.425
And I think our experience as human
beings, I know that to be true,

02:15:40.425 --> 02:15:44.484
because I just know that I don't
always feel selfish about things.

02:15:45.015 --> 02:15:47.165
Like, maybe sometimes I do,
and I do something nice because

02:15:47.165 --> 02:15:48.035
it'll make me look good.

02:15:48.035 --> 02:15:49.324
Like, okay, we're all human, right?

02:15:49.715 --> 02:15:54.305
But I know that there's good
feeling from others towards me.

02:15:54.355 --> 02:15:56.635
At times, that's just
about the good feeling.

02:15:56.645 --> 02:15:59.485
I know that there's that
from me towards others.

02:15:59.485 --> 02:16:03.735
So that tells us, yes, there is
something other than just the I.

02:16:03.914 --> 02:16:08.545
There is the we of dyads, right, of
relationships, no matter whether they're

02:16:08.835 --> 02:16:11.035
work, family, friendship, relationship.

02:16:11.225 --> 02:16:15.115
And then there are the levels
beyond that that are larger "we"s.

02:16:15.485 --> 02:16:17.235
The we of groups.

02:16:19.225 --> 02:16:22.355
Andrew Huberman: So if I understand
correctly, it can be the case that

02:16:22.624 --> 02:16:26.464
one person makes the majority or all
of the income for a family, the other

02:16:26.465 --> 02:16:29.665
person raises the children, takes
care of the majority of the home.

02:16:30.085 --> 02:16:35.575
And of course, there is a transaction
there, a set of transactions, but that

02:16:35.695 --> 02:16:38.965
it's in service to something larger
that really isn't transactional.

02:16:39.374 --> 02:16:44.055
I'm just stealing your words here,
but if I were to expand on that,

02:16:44.515 --> 02:16:48.535
just to make sure I understand that
the non-transactional thing that

02:16:48.535 --> 02:16:53.425
emerges from that is generative,
because it's a family, right?

02:16:53.425 --> 02:16:57.885
It's a family that everyone can extract
growth and pleasure and meaning from.

02:16:58.545 --> 02:17:02.845
And just because the roles are divided
as such, it doesn't necessarily mean

02:17:02.845 --> 02:17:07.115
that the relationship is defined
as transactional in the sense

02:17:07.115 --> 02:17:09.045
that it's less than it could be.

02:17:09.335 --> 02:17:09.835
Paul Conti: Right.

02:17:11.035 --> 02:17:13.094
If you think about what's
really being transacted.

02:17:13.115 --> 02:17:16.504
So, say one person is out in the
world and is making an income.

02:17:16.805 --> 02:17:20.015
The other person is, say, taking care of
the family and taking care of the home.

02:17:20.315 --> 02:17:21.184
What's being transacted?

02:17:21.534 --> 02:17:25.495
Well, the person who's making the
money is sharing the money, right?

02:17:25.715 --> 02:17:28.585
In that sense, transacting some
of the money to the other person.

02:17:28.585 --> 02:17:29.805
Like, you get to have some of it, too.

02:17:29.985 --> 02:17:31.295
You get to have some
of the benefit from it.

02:17:31.555 --> 02:17:34.954
And the person who is at home,
who's taking care of the family

02:17:34.954 --> 02:17:37.815
in the home, saying, you get to
have some of that too, right?

02:17:37.954 --> 02:17:40.565
You get to come home, the
children are taken care of, right?

02:17:40.575 --> 02:17:41.444
The home is taken care of.

02:17:41.444 --> 02:17:44.525
So both are transacting
something to the other.

02:17:44.795 --> 02:17:46.405
I mean, that's the truth of it.

02:17:46.825 --> 02:17:49.598
If you look at what's really going
on there, one person's getting the

02:17:49.598 --> 02:17:51.954
benefit of money they didn't earn,
the other person's getting benefits.

02:17:51.954 --> 02:17:54.215
They have childcare they
didn't do or pay for.

02:17:54.924 --> 02:17:57.725
They're not paying for childcare,
they're sharing resources.

02:17:57.735 --> 02:18:00.205
So, yes, that's true.

02:18:00.205 --> 02:18:01.725
But is that it?

02:18:02.735 --> 02:18:03.885
Are these two robots?

02:18:04.605 --> 02:18:07.485
One is the money making robot, and
the other is the childcare robot.

02:18:07.665 --> 02:18:09.125
That's not what's going on.

02:18:10.005 --> 02:18:13.035
The way we want to envision that,
i f we have two healthy people with

02:18:13.035 --> 02:18:17.214
g enerative drives is they love one
another, they're creating something better

02:18:17.214 --> 02:18:18.735
than they could create on their own.

02:18:18.745 --> 02:18:23.045
In fact, they've created children,
they've created a home together, and

02:18:23.045 --> 02:18:24.935
they're nurturing that family together.

02:18:25.215 --> 02:18:26.875
That is generative.

02:18:26.915 --> 02:18:29.804
And because it's generative,
it can also be flexible.

02:18:30.045 --> 02:18:33.424
So let's say the person who's
at home says, I need to do some

02:18:33.425 --> 02:18:35.154
things outside of the house, right?

02:18:35.344 --> 02:18:38.885
Or let's say the person who's out working
thinks, oh, my God, I feel overwhelmed.

02:18:38.885 --> 02:18:41.295
And I like to switch to a better
job, but that job's halftime.

02:18:41.495 --> 02:18:43.865
I could be half in the house, right?

02:18:44.335 --> 02:18:47.165
This is where people can come
together in the same way.

02:18:47.165 --> 02:18:51.594
We talked about the two and an eight on
the sex drive or sexuality scale, where

02:18:51.595 --> 02:18:54.104
people then can find compromises, right?

02:18:54.104 --> 02:18:57.435
And if one really wants to stay out of
the home all the time, but the person in

02:18:57.435 --> 02:19:01.435
the home wants to leave the home, well,
they find a way that that works, right?

02:19:01.435 --> 02:19:04.695
Because their care for one another,
the generative spirit that is

02:19:04.695 --> 02:19:09.425
in them individually and as a
couple, lets them nurture that.

02:19:09.425 --> 02:19:11.865
And one isn't interested
in oppressing the other.

02:19:11.865 --> 02:19:12.684
I'm not going to leave my job.

02:19:12.695 --> 02:19:14.805
You stay at home or I'm staying at home.

02:19:14.805 --> 02:19:15.514
You stay out there.

02:19:15.514 --> 02:19:17.115
It's like, okay, let's think about things.

02:19:17.335 --> 02:19:18.595
Let's communicate, right?

02:19:18.605 --> 02:19:23.354
That's how the generative drive not
only makes that awesome, like the

02:19:23.374 --> 02:19:25.705
five isn't the compromised position
between the eight and the two.

02:19:25.705 --> 02:19:27.674
The five is awesome right here.

02:19:27.685 --> 02:19:32.235
The transactional aspects are awesome
because of the generative aspects of

02:19:32.275 --> 02:19:36.965
what comes of it far more than either
of those people could make on their own.

02:19:36.965 --> 02:19:39.214
I mean, one would say it's not so obvious.

02:19:39.275 --> 02:19:42.655
Maybe if one makes X amount of
dollars, each could make half X.

02:19:43.055 --> 02:19:44.075
That's not what we're talking about.

02:19:44.085 --> 02:19:46.364
Neither of them can create
that family on their own.

02:19:48.645 --> 02:19:50.635
Andrew Huberman: It seems that so
much of what we're talking about

02:19:50.635 --> 02:19:56.145
relies on the hallmarks that we always
hear make up good relationships of

02:19:56.145 --> 02:19:58.535
all kinds, romantic and otherwise.

02:19:59.085 --> 02:20:05.424
Things like communication, listening,
generosity, all the stuff that we all know

02:20:05.424 --> 02:20:08.835
that we should bring to relationship and
hopefully are getting from relationships.

02:20:08.835 --> 02:20:11.885
And that if we're not, that
we should probably request

02:20:11.895 --> 02:20:14.975
from others in polite ways.

02:20:15.385 --> 02:20:15.885
Paul Conti: Right.

02:20:16.824 --> 02:20:17.895
I have one word for that.

02:20:18.795 --> 02:20:19.435
Kindergarten.

02:20:20.435 --> 02:20:21.395
Right?
Think about that.

02:20:21.645 --> 02:20:23.374
We learned that in kindergarten.

02:20:23.374 --> 02:20:24.955
What you're describing is so simple.

02:20:25.865 --> 02:20:29.765
That's why it's so high up on the,
this is what the geyser is pushing up.

02:20:29.765 --> 02:20:34.085
It's high up from the two pillars,
because what you're talking about is

02:20:34.085 --> 02:20:38.105
simple generosity of spirit, giving
rather than taking, being kind to

02:20:38.105 --> 02:20:41.594
others, letting things go, feeling good
about yourself, even if you fall down.

02:20:42.034 --> 02:20:43.695
We learn this in kindergarten.

02:20:43.695 --> 02:20:48.045
So we must, somewhere inside of us,
really value it, but then we let it go.

02:20:48.055 --> 02:20:49.795
We make things overly complex.

02:20:50.045 --> 02:20:56.054
And this idea that in many ways
we should go back to kindergarten

02:20:56.545 --> 02:20:58.205
because there's a purity there.

02:20:58.665 --> 02:21:02.325
Children at an age where they can
learn, and then we bring a nice kind

02:21:02.325 --> 02:21:05.495
of learning to them and a nurturing
to them and we can bring it to

02:21:05.495 --> 02:21:10.125
ourselves, too, so that we simplify
that which has become overly complex.

02:21:10.704 --> 02:21:13.235
We simplify because
trauma makes complexity.

02:21:13.235 --> 02:21:17.044
Having to just make one's way in
the world is complicated, right?

02:21:17.045 --> 02:21:21.195
So things get more and more complicated
and we lose the simple roots of

02:21:21.195 --> 02:21:26.404
goodness or the goodness of simplicity,
and we can come back to that.

02:21:26.405 --> 02:21:30.395
So I couldn't resist when you said,
oh, these simple, these basic things.

02:21:31.335 --> 02:21:34.374
That's the point of it, because
that's where agency and gratitude

02:21:34.894 --> 02:21:37.075
and the generative drive, that's
where they live in the simplicity.

02:21:37.765 --> 02:21:39.545
Andrew Huberman: I'm glad
you mentioned kindergarten.

02:21:39.565 --> 02:21:44.345
It brought me back to images of
kindergarten where, yeah, as far

02:21:44.345 --> 02:21:46.975
as I can remember now, there were
all the critical components of

02:21:46.975 --> 02:21:50.314
a great generative environment,
adults who really cared about us.

02:21:50.324 --> 02:21:54.485
Fortunately, there were snack time
with oranges, so nourishing food,

02:21:54.614 --> 02:21:56.545
there was nap time in the afternoon.

02:21:56.545 --> 02:21:57.535
Like, these things are all things--

02:21:57.545 --> 02:21:59.175
Paul Conti: You got
some exercise, explore.

02:21:59.175 --> 02:22:01.175
Andrew Huberman: You could take a
guinea pig home on the weekends.

02:22:01.595 --> 02:22:02.585
They had this guinea pig.

02:22:02.624 --> 02:22:05.835
I think there were several guinea
pigs, but they kept trying to convince

02:22:05.835 --> 02:22:10.405
us it was the same guinea pig because
they would allow one family to take

02:22:10.495 --> 02:22:11.865
home the guinea pig each weekend.

02:22:11.865 --> 02:22:13.945
And sometimes I think it
didn't work out quite so well.

02:22:13.945 --> 02:22:16.885
But anyway, the guinea pig
was a pervasive feature.

02:22:16.885 --> 02:22:18.315
So you learned to take care of things.

02:22:18.315 --> 02:22:21.784
And in all seriousness, now that I
step back from it, there's nothing

02:22:21.795 --> 02:22:25.945
more generative, it seems, than a
kindergarten classroom environment,

02:22:25.985 --> 02:22:28.384
or we should say a healthy
kindergarten classroom environment.

02:22:28.454 --> 02:22:30.995
It's all about support of others.

02:22:30.995 --> 02:22:36.445
And if we go to the pillars, the structure
of self and the function of self, surely

02:22:36.445 --> 02:22:37.925
there's a lot going on at home, too.

02:22:37.925 --> 02:22:39.935
But think about what's salient.

02:22:39.935 --> 02:22:46.475
It's learning messages of self
confidence, like instilling those.

02:22:46.475 --> 02:22:49.284
It's behaviors that are based
on strivings and hopefulness.

02:22:50.144 --> 02:22:51.515
It's all the good things.

02:22:51.685 --> 02:22:52.935
Yes, all the good things.

02:22:53.135 --> 02:22:58.465
And so, as I was thinking about
communication and all these good things,

02:22:58.465 --> 02:23:02.425
that clearly kindergarten is a wonderful
template for, in all seriousness

02:23:02.425 --> 02:23:03.945
I think, is an amazing template.

02:23:05.325 --> 02:23:09.715
I'm thinking about what gets in the way,
and of course, trauma can get in the

02:23:09.715 --> 02:23:12.535
way, and we should talk about that more.

02:23:13.055 --> 02:23:17.164
But it seems to me that one of the things
that gets in the way of asking for what

02:23:17.164 --> 02:23:21.494
we want, of hearing requests of us in
the way that is going to bring about the

02:23:21.505 --> 02:23:24.635
most generative goodness, is anxiety.

02:23:25.325 --> 02:23:28.695
It's like you're tired from a
long day and someone mentions,

02:23:28.715 --> 02:23:30.695
we have to take the trash out.

02:23:31.505 --> 02:23:32.385
I was at work all day.

02:23:34.475 --> 02:23:39.114
That kind of thing or earlier you
were talking about the issue of the

02:23:39.114 --> 02:23:41.075
non issue in terms of power dynamics.

02:23:43.545 --> 02:23:46.575
Well, let's just say I've had the
experience before in relationship of

02:23:46.575 --> 02:23:51.875
feeling like if I make a request or I
have a quote unquote complaint about

02:23:51.875 --> 02:23:54.925
something, that the other person is
going to be so upset about the fact

02:23:54.925 --> 02:23:59.555
that they didn't do something well,
that it's going to be three days of

02:23:59.555 --> 02:24:02.185
just diminished happiness for everybody.

02:24:02.215 --> 02:24:05.155
So I just assume, not deal with it, right?

02:24:05.455 --> 02:24:08.155
So when we hear the word anxiety, I
think we often think about the person,

02:24:08.155 --> 02:24:12.725
like quaking about public speaking
or like getting the circuits in

02:24:12.725 --> 02:24:15.724
their brain hijacked that were only
designed for saber toothed tigers.

02:24:15.725 --> 02:24:20.455
But anxiety seems to serve both
a very important functional role

02:24:20.455 --> 02:24:23.345
in modern life that has nothing
to do with physical threat.

02:24:24.095 --> 02:24:28.795
But it also seems to be the feature
that if not kept in check, if we can't

02:24:28.805 --> 02:24:33.255
regulate our own anxiety, I'm realizing
there's no way that we're going to

02:24:33.255 --> 02:24:37.485
be in a position to ask for what we
need and what we want, to hear what's

02:24:37.485 --> 02:24:39.405
needed of us and what others want.

02:24:39.915 --> 02:24:45.235
In other words, anxiety seems like a
major barrier to the generative drive.

02:24:46.624 --> 02:24:50.455
Paul Conti: I think the place to start
is i f you show me a person who has

02:24:50.455 --> 02:24:52.734
no anxiety, I'll show you a mannequin.

02:24:53.934 --> 02:24:55.765
We all have anxiety in us.

02:24:55.795 --> 02:24:57.355
Remember, it's just a word, right?

02:24:57.845 --> 02:24:58.615
What are we getting at?

02:24:58.615 --> 02:25:04.575
We're getting at a sense of tension
or a sense of disquiet inside of

02:25:04.575 --> 02:25:08.755
us that we would like to solve, we
would like to ease, if we could.

02:25:09.534 --> 02:25:14.465
It is not helpful or healthy if
we have very, very low levels of

02:25:14.465 --> 02:25:18.035
that, because then our strivings
are certainly going to suffer.

02:25:18.225 --> 02:25:22.384
There's not a lot of motivation
to go make change in the

02:25:22.385 --> 02:25:23.775
world, a change in our lives.

02:25:23.784 --> 02:25:28.424
So if we have too little of that
tension, that doesn't go well.

02:25:28.424 --> 02:25:33.115
And of course, that maps
to a low assertiveness,

02:25:33.755 --> 02:25:35.905
aggression, proactive drive.

02:25:36.325 --> 02:25:38.885
There are other ways that could
show i tself, but it maps very

02:25:38.885 --> 02:25:40.584
clearly, I think, to that.

02:25:40.855 --> 02:25:44.584
And that's not good for us on
the other end of the spectrum.

02:25:44.585 --> 02:25:47.184
We're much more concerned with the
other end of the spectrum because a

02:25:47.184 --> 02:25:50.975
lot of anxiety feels very, very bad
and it feels very bad right now, right?

02:25:51.355 --> 02:25:56.335
So our high levels of anxiety,
they narrow our cognitive spectrum.

02:25:56.335 --> 02:26:00.574
They narrow our ability to think
about what's going on around

02:26:00.574 --> 02:26:02.145
us, to think about ourselves.

02:26:02.395 --> 02:26:07.135
So the idea with anxiety is
to recognize, hey, we all have

02:26:07.135 --> 02:26:08.995
it, and we can call it anxiety.

02:26:08.995 --> 02:26:09.985
We can call it tension.

02:26:09.985 --> 02:26:12.755
We can call it whatever we
want t o, but we want that to

02:26:12.755 --> 02:26:15.505
be i n a healthy place, right?

02:26:16.155 --> 02:26:21.625
Which comes, of course, back to the self,
that if my levels of anxiety are very

02:26:21.735 --> 02:26:28.255
high and therefore it's causing problems
in my relationship, like maybe I'm

02:26:28.255 --> 02:26:29.965
always asking my partner if we're okay.

02:26:30.135 --> 02:26:30.885
That happens a lot.

02:26:30.915 --> 02:26:31.645
Like, are we okay?

02:26:31.645 --> 02:26:32.175
Are you happy?

02:26:32.704 --> 02:26:34.205
Because there's a lot of anxiety in me.

02:26:34.615 --> 02:26:37.324
The first place to go
is to look at myself.

02:26:37.335 --> 02:26:39.604
So we could say, oh, I
have attachment insecurity.

02:26:40.135 --> 02:26:41.654
That's just stating the obvious, right?

02:26:41.825 --> 02:26:44.225
Andrew Huberman: Or, sorry to
interrupt, but a very common one

02:26:44.915 --> 02:26:47.765
nowadays that I think is new in
the course of human history is

02:26:49.985 --> 02:26:51.405
the tugging the line phenomenon.

02:26:51.405 --> 02:26:54.445
You text somebody, you're thinking
about them, you don't hear back,

02:26:55.085 --> 02:26:57.255
and then tension starts to mount.

02:26:57.324 --> 02:27:01.755
And then depending on the context,
you may either be concerned about

02:27:01.755 --> 02:27:04.455
the person or even suspicious
about what the person is up to.

02:27:04.455 --> 02:27:06.295
I mean, here it's very contextual, right?

02:27:06.565 --> 02:27:09.735
And it depends on the history of both
individuals and that relationship.

02:27:10.115 --> 02:27:13.645
But then the person will reach
back eventually and it either

02:27:13.645 --> 02:27:17.155
will bring relief or relief with
some resent, like, where were you?

02:27:17.165 --> 02:27:17.924
What happened?

02:27:19.005 --> 02:27:20.655
This is very, very common.

02:27:21.675 --> 02:27:25.465
So much so, in fact, that I've
come to learn in talking to

02:27:25.465 --> 02:27:27.054
others, and this is the classic...

02:27:27.225 --> 02:27:30.755
I have a friend who, but really
others who are in the landscape

02:27:30.755 --> 02:27:31.935
of looking for a relationship.

02:27:32.265 --> 02:27:38.675
And there are people I know well who
go through a lot of effort to set an

02:27:38.705 --> 02:27:41.135
intermittent schedule of response.

02:27:41.135 --> 02:27:43.755
Like to not give the other
person the sense that they always

02:27:43.755 --> 02:27:44.845
respond with short latency.

02:27:45.275 --> 02:27:48.914
Because, indeed, sometimes they will be
able to do that and sometimes they won't.

02:27:48.914 --> 02:27:51.764
What they're basically trying to do is
make sure that the person doesn't, they're

02:27:51.765 --> 02:27:54.745
actually trying to take care of the
other person in addition to themselves.

02:27:55.215 --> 02:27:57.975
Because I think we come to expect
a certain latency of response

02:27:57.975 --> 02:27:58.885
with certain individuals.

02:27:58.885 --> 02:28:02.295
And if we don't hear back with that
particular latency of response,

02:28:03.105 --> 02:28:04.755
our own anxiety starts to pick up.

02:28:04.755 --> 02:28:08.525
And it can be quite damaging to a
relationship, in particular to the

02:28:09.124 --> 02:28:11.664
generative drives within us, because
in that time that we're stressed,

02:28:11.735 --> 02:28:14.455
we're not tending to other things,
including things that we could do for the

02:28:14.455 --> 02:28:15.965
relationship that we're so worried about.

02:28:15.965 --> 02:28:19.375
Paul Conti: The famous scene in Swingers,
right, where t he person leaves a message

02:28:19.415 --> 02:28:22.910
and then lives the whole relationship
in his own m ind and breaks up with the

02:28:22.935 --> 02:28:24.665
person and they never actually spoke.

02:28:25.015 --> 02:28:27.455
There's the anxiety run wild.

02:28:27.455 --> 02:28:31.345
And of course, I remember that
was very popular when it came out.

02:28:31.345 --> 02:28:31.585
Why?

02:28:31.585 --> 02:28:35.435
Because it resonated with the
insecurities we feel and now with the

02:28:35.435 --> 02:28:38.415
ability to feel those insecurities
in a much more immediate way.

02:28:38.435 --> 02:28:39.725
That person didn't text me back.

02:28:40.305 --> 02:28:45.525
There's a lot more of that inside o f
us, which really points to if you're able

02:28:45.525 --> 02:28:48.645
to identify that you're anxious, too.

02:28:48.645 --> 02:28:52.335
Anxious for comfort, which, again,
if one looks inside or even listens,

02:28:52.335 --> 02:28:54.905
maybe to what others have said or
reflected, like, there's a lot of

02:28:54.905 --> 02:28:58.205
data, especially introspection, t
o be able to identify that, right?

02:28:58.565 --> 02:29:00.254
The next question is always, why?

02:29:01.485 --> 02:29:02.384
Let's go look at that.

02:29:02.614 --> 02:29:04.645
Maybe that person has been
anxious their whole life.

02:29:05.194 --> 02:29:07.715
Maybe they just have
run tense all the time.

02:29:07.715 --> 02:29:08.035
You know what?

02:29:08.035 --> 02:29:11.325
Sometimes a little bit of medicine
that kind of just pulls that down.

02:29:11.325 --> 02:29:14.180
P erson can take that for 50 years, life
is better, t here's no side effects, s o

02:29:14.305 --> 02:29:16.285
sometimes that might be the case, right?

02:29:16.585 --> 02:29:20.335
Sometimes the person's anxiety kind of
grew throughout childhood, and maybe

02:29:20.335 --> 02:29:22.954
that's because there were difficult
things or traumatic things that happened.

02:29:23.165 --> 02:29:26.735
Maybe there was nothing like that,
but maybe the person was very good at

02:29:26.735 --> 02:29:29.685
engaging in the world and then felt
more and more pressures upon themselves.

02:29:29.685 --> 02:29:32.135
And no one ever did anything wrong,
and they've only been rewarded.

02:29:32.695 --> 02:29:36.025
But regardless, look at
the anxiety in yourself.

02:29:36.025 --> 02:29:37.765
Go back to those pillars, right?

02:29:37.765 --> 02:29:43.335
And one might discover, for example,
maybe the anxiety I'm feeling is

02:29:43.355 --> 02:29:44.855
attributable to the other person.

02:29:44.985 --> 02:29:47.025
Am I feeling anxious
because I'm intimidated?

02:29:47.665 --> 02:29:50.605
Am I feeling anxious because I
know that if I don't behave in a

02:29:50.605 --> 02:29:53.695
certain way, the next time there's
a group meeting, there'll be

02:29:53.704 --> 02:29:56.264
some snarky joke made about me.

02:29:56.265 --> 02:29:58.324
So, anxiety, sometimes it's the self.

02:29:58.674 --> 02:30:00.794
Sometimes it's got a lot
of biological components.

02:30:00.805 --> 02:30:02.645
Sometimes it's got a lot of
psychological components.

02:30:02.645 --> 02:30:03.525
Sometimes both.

02:30:04.045 --> 02:30:05.315
Sometimes it's the environment.

02:30:05.315 --> 02:30:06.435
Sometimes it's the other.

02:30:06.745 --> 02:30:07.455
The other person.

02:30:07.455 --> 02:30:09.435
So look at why.

02:30:09.664 --> 02:30:11.534
Because that's how we learn.

02:30:11.845 --> 02:30:13.295
Like, what is going on inside of me?

02:30:13.295 --> 02:30:14.754
Where is that level of anxiety?

02:30:14.975 --> 02:30:16.494
How does it not feel comfortable?

02:30:16.614 --> 02:30:17.565
What actually is it?

02:30:17.585 --> 02:30:19.755
How is it changing, how I'm behaving?

02:30:19.755 --> 02:30:20.965
How might it be changing?

02:30:21.185 --> 02:30:22.545
How am I responding to it?

02:30:22.825 --> 02:30:23.995
So, what are we doing?

02:30:23.995 --> 02:30:29.924
We're going back to this, look in those
10 cabinets and figure out why, which we

02:30:29.925 --> 02:30:34.474
can do the vast majority of times between
ourselves and the use of others, either

02:30:34.475 --> 02:30:36.014
professionally or non professionally.

02:30:36.335 --> 02:30:40.425
We can go understand the
anxiety or the lack of anxiety.

02:30:40.425 --> 02:30:41.865
Why am I not so motivated?

02:30:42.305 --> 02:30:42.625
Right?

02:30:42.625 --> 02:30:45.205
I mean, that's the case with a lot
of people who feel demoralized.

02:30:45.215 --> 02:30:46.895
They don't feel they can
get anywhere in the world.

02:30:47.445 --> 02:30:50.414
The world's a bad place, and how are
you going to make your way in the world?

02:30:50.414 --> 02:30:55.145
And they start feeling nihilistic, or
they feel like the eight ball is against

02:30:55.145 --> 02:30:56.985
them, even in a generational capacity.

02:30:57.175 --> 02:31:02.515
And now they feel demoralized, and the
tension inside is soothed by things.

02:31:02.525 --> 02:31:05.945
So maybe that's the person who's
overusing the thing that soothes, right.

02:31:06.034 --> 02:31:09.725
So they could go look at, hey, I
was kind of motivated and did well

02:31:09.725 --> 02:31:13.625
in sports or did well in this, was
interested in that, what's going on?

02:31:13.625 --> 02:31:16.595
That's how a person could identify,
for example, being demoralized.

02:31:16.605 --> 02:31:21.015
So that process of inquiry gives
us the information that we can

02:31:21.015 --> 02:31:22.995
use in the service of change.

02:31:23.275 --> 02:31:26.875
And the change, while not achieved
100% of the time, the change for

02:31:26.875 --> 02:31:31.285
the better is predictable if it's
arising from a place of understanding.

02:31:32.245 --> 02:31:35.175
Andrew Huberman: I'm very curious
about frames of mind in relationship.

02:31:35.445 --> 02:31:41.065
In particular, how being in our own
experience, say, anxious because someone

02:31:41.075 --> 02:31:45.654
hasn't responded to us and really paying
attention to the anxiety and drilling into

02:31:45.654 --> 02:31:48.245
it and asking ourselves, why am I anxious?

02:31:48.245 --> 02:31:50.475
And do I deserve to be anxious?

02:31:50.475 --> 02:31:51.905
Is it about me or is it about them?

02:31:52.965 --> 02:31:58.325
Whether or not that's a valid pursuit,
or whether or not focusing on the

02:31:58.335 --> 02:32:02.624
other person and trying to imagine
what's going on in their head, that

02:32:02.624 --> 02:32:05.654
they might be doing this or that
they seem to do something repeatedly.

02:32:06.065 --> 02:32:09.305
This, of course, relates to much more
than just the scenario of waiting for

02:32:09.305 --> 02:32:10.985
a text message and feeling anxious.

02:32:11.465 --> 02:32:16.555
I think so much of how we come into
relationships of all kinds, romantic

02:32:16.645 --> 02:32:22.234
certainly, but family relationships
are in and around the tendency to

02:32:22.235 --> 02:32:26.185
switch back and forth, sometimes
seemingly at random, between our own

02:32:26.185 --> 02:32:27.785
experience and what am I feeling?

02:32:27.785 --> 02:32:29.155
What am I experiencing?

02:32:29.185 --> 02:32:30.545
And then thinking about the other.

02:32:30.545 --> 02:32:32.434
Like, what are they thinking?

02:32:32.435 --> 02:32:33.415
What are they experiencing?

02:32:33.415 --> 02:32:36.865
I mean, this has everything
to do with human dynamics.

02:32:37.815 --> 02:32:42.785
I would think that it's near impossible
for the typical person to just live

02:32:42.785 --> 02:32:47.025
life through their own frame and
lens and never pay attention at all

02:32:47.035 --> 02:32:48.115
to what others might be thinking.

02:32:48.125 --> 02:32:53.684
Even, for instance, the most exploitive,
extractive narcissist sociopath presumably

02:32:53.685 --> 02:32:57.554
is thinking about who in the room
is going to be their target because

02:32:57.555 --> 02:32:59.334
of how that person might be feeling.

02:32:59.565 --> 02:33:03.995
And of course, on the benevolent side,
people who want to do positive, generative

02:33:04.485 --> 02:33:09.525
things in the world are probably thinking
about who to align with, who has common

02:33:09.525 --> 02:33:13.085
goals that they might want to work with
or be with romantically that could help

02:33:13.215 --> 02:33:15.504
them and the other person generate.

02:33:15.935 --> 02:33:18.725
So what is this thing that we do?

02:33:19.805 --> 02:33:24.945
What is it called, and how does it work
to place ourselves in the mind of others?

02:33:25.205 --> 02:33:29.745
And what roles does it serve and what
goals does it undercut when we do this?

02:33:30.615 --> 02:33:33.364
Paul Conti: Well, I think the first
thing to say is that everything

02:33:33.364 --> 02:33:35.235
follows the same simple pattern.

02:33:36.014 --> 02:33:39.945
So I start with thinking about me, right?

02:33:39.965 --> 02:33:42.398
If I'm anxious, why am I anxious?

02:33:42.398 --> 02:33:43.665
What's going on inside of me?

02:33:43.665 --> 02:33:45.115
Then I think about you.

02:33:45.175 --> 02:33:46.275
Are you anxious too?

02:33:46.585 --> 02:33:48.845
Or maybe I'm not anxious,
but I notice that you are.

02:33:49.595 --> 02:33:52.825
So there's the thought about the
I, because I can't think in a

02:33:52.854 --> 02:33:56.425
clear headed way about you unless
I've thought about me, right?

02:33:56.425 --> 02:33:59.410
Because if I'm really, really
anxious, then how am I supposed

02:33:59.605 --> 02:34:02.255
to understand and try and get an
idea of where you're at, right?

02:34:02.735 --> 02:34:03.994
So then where do we arrive?

02:34:04.315 --> 02:34:07.115
We arrive at the magic bridge of the us.

02:34:08.265 --> 02:34:10.214
That's what connects us.

02:34:10.255 --> 02:34:15.035
Whether the us is a friendship, it's a
professional relationship, it's romance.

02:34:15.825 --> 02:34:17.095
There's the bridge of the us.

02:34:17.095 --> 02:34:20.645
Because it's not just
how or when am I anxious.

02:34:20.675 --> 02:34:22.205
How or when are you anxious?

02:34:23.045 --> 02:34:24.565
What are things like when we're together?

02:34:25.485 --> 02:34:25.975
Maybe that.

02:34:25.975 --> 02:34:27.865
Maybe I'm anxious when we're together.

02:34:27.904 --> 02:34:29.565
And what's that tension about?

02:34:29.565 --> 02:34:30.685
Or you're anxious when we're together.

02:34:30.685 --> 02:34:34.235
And maybe that's for reasons we
could talk about and make less.

02:34:34.255 --> 02:34:37.425
Maybe there's some insecurity in one
of us, or maybe the other person is

02:34:37.425 --> 02:34:41.295
behaving in a certain way that's not
making the second person feel good.

02:34:41.775 --> 02:34:46.085
We can then come together and see
how does the us impact the level

02:34:46.255 --> 02:34:50.955
of anxiety, and how do we then take
away from the us being stronger?

02:34:50.955 --> 02:34:53.025
So think about, w e talked
about the trauma bond.

02:34:53.265 --> 02:34:56.295
Trauma bond can be
enacted in a negative way.

02:34:56.295 --> 02:34:57.865
It can be enacted in a positive way.

02:34:58.124 --> 02:35:01.855
And those two people who can go to
the museum together, who can't go

02:35:01.855 --> 02:35:06.655
to the museum separately, they're
living in the magic bridge of the us.

02:35:07.295 --> 02:35:10.335
They're both at the museum, but
neither can go to the museum.

02:35:10.815 --> 02:35:14.255
Neither can go to the museum,
but both can go to the museum.

02:35:14.505 --> 02:35:17.615
And then they take away
stronger selves from that.

02:35:18.244 --> 02:35:20.535
That's a reinforcing experience.

02:35:20.605 --> 02:35:21.244
It's positive.

02:35:21.465 --> 02:35:22.745
It builds the generative drive.

02:35:22.764 --> 02:35:23.805
It builds confidence.

02:35:24.165 --> 02:35:27.125
The person went, they were assertive,
and that was gratified in a good

02:35:27.125 --> 02:35:28.395
way, and they felt pleasure.

02:35:28.925 --> 02:35:33.575
So when we look at the us, it is about
the us in the moment, but it is also

02:35:33.575 --> 02:35:37.615
about how two people are impacting one
another in the rest of their lives.

02:35:37.615 --> 02:35:41.715
And of course, this is more important
the closer the relationship, like this

02:35:41.715 --> 02:35:45.205
is very important, for example, in close
friendships or family relationships.

02:35:45.605 --> 02:35:49.265
And I think this is of extreme
importance in relationships.

02:35:49.695 --> 02:35:53.894
These are the two people who presumably
are the closest to one another on Earth.

02:35:54.164 --> 02:36:01.655
So a shared us that promotes understanding
and bolsters a sense of agency and

02:36:01.655 --> 02:36:06.605
gratitude and bolsters the generative
drive that's great for both people

02:36:06.715 --> 02:36:09.845
when they're not in the us, when
one goes one way and one goes the

02:36:09.845 --> 02:36:12.355
other, because that happens all the
time in relationships, too, right?

02:36:12.355 --> 02:36:16.955
Go to work at different places or
we bolster ourselves outside of

02:36:16.955 --> 02:36:21.965
relationships if we see what the magic
bridge of the relationship can be.

02:36:21.965 --> 02:36:25.095
So, we can look there for
problems like, why am I anxious?

02:36:25.095 --> 02:36:25.824
Why are you anxious?

02:36:25.835 --> 02:36:26.715
Why are we anxious?

02:36:26.895 --> 02:36:28.804
Why are you only anxious
when we're we and not...?

02:36:29.195 --> 02:36:31.534
We can look for all that,
and we should, right?

02:36:31.815 --> 02:36:36.464
But we can also even more
powerfully look for the good there.

02:36:36.905 --> 02:36:40.925
How can our shared bond, whatever
it may be, be better for both of us?

02:36:40.945 --> 02:36:46.385
And what stronger incentive could there
be than to do that in relationships?

02:36:46.555 --> 02:36:49.084
Romantic relationships, the ones that
are closest to us, if they're not

02:36:49.085 --> 02:36:50.224
romantic, they could be friendships.

02:36:50.225 --> 02:36:54.435
Whatever our closest relationships are,
they're the most important vehicles,

02:36:54.465 --> 02:36:57.845
so to speak, to better health and
happiness, to getting to that place

02:36:57.865 --> 02:37:02.115
of peace and contentment and delight.

02:37:02.655 --> 02:37:04.805
The us is very, very powerful.

02:37:04.805 --> 02:37:08.014
In fact, even more powerful
than the I and the you.

02:37:09.775 --> 02:37:14.515
Andrew Huberman: When thinking about
the us and trying to understand why

02:37:14.515 --> 02:37:19.435
somebody that we know and are in
relationship with is behaving the way

02:37:19.435 --> 02:37:23.815
they are or might be feeling or claiming
they feel the way that they are.

02:37:24.055 --> 02:37:28.244
How useful do you think it is for
us to put ourselves in their shoes?

02:37:30.194 --> 02:37:33.425
I can think of all sorts of ways
in which this could be beneficial.

02:37:33.455 --> 02:37:37.395
I can also think of all sorts of ways
in which it would be focusing off the

02:37:37.395 --> 02:37:44.574
self and our own experience in ways that
might be defensive avoidance or denial.

02:37:45.514 --> 02:37:46.354
Yeah, I'll come clean.

02:37:46.354 --> 02:37:49.915
I mean, there have been plenty of times
in relationship where I get fixated on

02:37:50.085 --> 02:37:56.484
why someone is the way they are, behaved
the way they did, and more often than not,

02:37:56.494 --> 02:38:01.654
by taking a step back and thinking about
why my reaction to that is the way it is.

02:38:02.324 --> 02:38:09.095
I don't solve the "problem," but I get a
lot further along in terms of quelling my

02:38:09.095 --> 02:38:11.295
own anxiety and finding a path forward.

02:38:11.485 --> 02:38:11.985
Paul Conti: Right.

02:38:12.935 --> 02:38:17.805
Well, mentalization, which is the
ability to discern feeling states,

02:38:17.855 --> 02:38:20.625
intention states in self and others.

02:38:20.625 --> 02:38:22.435
But now we're looking for others, right?

02:38:22.435 --> 02:38:26.415
So the ability to understand feeling
states, what's going on inside of you?

02:38:26.435 --> 02:38:27.155
Intention.

02:38:27.165 --> 02:38:28.605
What are your intentions?

02:38:28.765 --> 02:38:35.334
That should only be good, because if
we're seeing it through a clear lens,

02:38:35.565 --> 02:38:40.264
meaning a lens that's not biased by
some problem, like a defense mechanism

02:38:40.264 --> 02:38:44.565
of rationalization, for example, or of
projection, if we're seeing clearly,

02:38:44.905 --> 02:38:46.324
we're learning about the other.

02:38:47.224 --> 02:38:49.075
And the learning is never bad.

02:38:49.395 --> 02:38:51.565
Knowledge, truth is good.

02:38:51.935 --> 02:38:55.185
So if we learn about the other by,
in that sense, putting ourselves in

02:38:55.185 --> 02:38:57.744
their shoes, then we gain information.

02:38:57.744 --> 02:39:02.165
But it's the health in us that
is so crucial to what we do

02:39:02.165 --> 02:39:03.555
with that information, right?

02:39:03.745 --> 02:39:08.545
So one person could say, put themselves
in the other person's shoes and

02:39:08.545 --> 02:39:11.345
they can say, okay, that person, I
can see that person's responding.

02:39:11.345 --> 02:39:13.814
And they say, pretty calm,
pretty calm about something.

02:39:14.964 --> 02:39:16.575
And maybe that's just a good thing.

02:39:16.624 --> 02:39:20.295
Maybe there's some contention going
on and that person's maintaining

02:39:20.295 --> 02:39:23.995
their cool and that person is going
to be really helpful in navigating

02:39:23.995 --> 02:39:28.375
an argument or a disagreement to
some really positive endpoint.

02:39:29.374 --> 02:39:34.305
So, if a person sees clearly,
okay, I can see that you're calm.

02:39:34.305 --> 02:39:38.151
And I also see that you're trying to
figure things out and the things that

02:39:38.151 --> 02:39:39.485
you're saying are sort of positive.

02:39:39.485 --> 02:39:42.455
You're disagreeing with me,
but you don't seem to mean ill.

02:39:42.895 --> 02:39:43.955
Then I can see, you know what?

02:39:43.955 --> 02:39:47.925
I think your calm is good, because I'm
getting a little bit upset and you're not.

02:39:48.865 --> 02:39:50.684
And I see that there's
a benevolence in you.

02:39:50.685 --> 02:39:53.905
So maybe you'll guide us to the
p lace that maybe I can't, right?

02:39:54.325 --> 02:39:58.935
But think about if there's
not a clear lens inside of me.

02:39:59.575 --> 02:40:05.705
If I have a defense system that leads
me to externalize, responsibility that

02:40:05.715 --> 02:40:08.775
leads me to project or leads me to do
all sorts of things that are not healthy.

02:40:09.045 --> 02:40:12.815
Now I might think, well, you're calm
because you just don't care about me.

02:40:13.905 --> 02:40:15.965
I mean, so think of the same exact thing.

02:40:16.445 --> 02:40:19.685
Now in the example we're giving the person
in your position is like being really

02:40:19.685 --> 02:40:24.875
benign and benevolent, but it happens
all the time where that is misconstrued.

02:40:25.215 --> 02:40:30.204
So it's not that the insight,
that person is calm, I see that

02:40:30.264 --> 02:40:33.195
they're calm, it's that there's
a deficit in the mentalization.

02:40:33.195 --> 02:40:35.935
The person is not fully
understanding their intention.

02:40:36.835 --> 02:40:39.095
What's coming through then gets distorted.

02:40:39.145 --> 02:40:40.995
So, we think about defense mechanisms.

02:40:41.275 --> 02:40:45.615
They can be clear, they can let light
through in this beautiful way that has

02:40:45.615 --> 02:40:48.004
fidelity, or they can be very distorting.

02:40:48.225 --> 02:40:55.745
So the thought that, if I'm discerning
that you are benevolent and you seem to be

02:40:55.775 --> 02:41:00.514
trying to solve the problem for us, but I
take in and you just don't care about me

02:41:00.515 --> 02:41:02.515
and you're trying to put one over on me.

02:41:03.445 --> 02:41:08.074
If I've assessed it accurately,
but it comes on the other

02:41:08.075 --> 02:41:10.375
end in a way that's changed.

02:41:10.575 --> 02:41:14.275
That's because there's a lack of
clarity , there's distortion inside of me.

02:41:15.005 --> 02:41:15.505
Right?

02:41:15.914 --> 02:41:19.475
So then if I'm really working on
myself, I'm in the best place I

02:41:19.475 --> 02:41:23.304
can be, I'm going to be better
at mentalizing about you and me.

02:41:24.155 --> 02:41:26.945
Even if I say, well, we're having a
disagreement, I know I tend to get

02:41:26.945 --> 02:41:29.995
a little defensive, so I'm going t
o be a little biased to see w hat's

02:41:30.005 --> 02:41:32.275
in you in a kind of negative way.

02:41:32.605 --> 02:41:36.305
So what am I seeing like, okay, you're
pretty calm, maybe you don't care.

02:41:36.405 --> 02:41:38.865
I know I can think that, right?

02:41:38.865 --> 02:41:40.034
But come on, you're trying.

02:41:40.034 --> 02:41:40.875
You want to solve our problem.

02:41:41.395 --> 02:41:44.135
Imagine when that goes on inside
of a person, which sometimes

02:41:44.135 --> 02:41:47.635
goes on consciously or goes on
unconsciously or some mixture.

02:41:48.225 --> 02:41:49.565
It's so powerful.

02:41:49.795 --> 02:41:53.134
The person is aware of their own state,
which helps them to be aware of the

02:41:53.135 --> 02:41:54.755
state of the others, of the other.

02:41:54.784 --> 02:41:57.444
And then the information they're
getting comes through with fidelity.

02:41:57.635 --> 02:42:00.585
And now all of a sudden, instead of
maybe I'm going to blow things up now

02:42:00.585 --> 02:42:03.755
I'm allying with you in solving our
problem because I see clearly about you

02:42:03.755 --> 02:42:06.615
and me and that's going on all the time.

02:42:06.615 --> 02:42:07.795
It might sound, well, it's complicated.

02:42:07.795 --> 02:42:09.795
There's a lot of back and forth, right?

02:42:09.795 --> 02:42:12.695
There are millions of things
going on every split second in our

02:42:12.704 --> 02:42:15.665
unconscious mind that is throwing
all this up to the conscious mind.

02:42:15.665 --> 02:42:19.645
It's doing things rapidly that's
going on all the time in us, whether

02:42:19.645 --> 02:42:21.155
we want to acknowledge it or not.

02:42:21.415 --> 02:42:26.425
And we choose not to acknowledge it at our
own peril because then we're not going to

02:42:26.435 --> 02:42:28.545
those two pillars and their 10 cupboards.

02:42:28.775 --> 02:42:32.355
We're not going to the structure of
self, the function of self, and what

02:42:32.515 --> 02:42:35.314
comprises those two pillars to look for.

02:42:35.465 --> 02:42:36.575
Hey, what's going on?

02:42:36.635 --> 02:42:37.845
Right, let me understand better.

02:42:38.195 --> 02:42:42.935
And even if things are going well, we
can always, as you said not that long

02:42:42.935 --> 02:42:44.525
ago, we can understand ourselves better.

02:42:45.164 --> 02:42:48.865
The stronger I am, the more generative
drive there is in me, the less

02:42:48.875 --> 02:42:53.335
defensiveness, the more the agency and
the gratitude, the more I'm armed for

02:42:53.755 --> 02:42:56.105
whatever difficult thing comes my way.

02:42:56.365 --> 02:42:59.585
So if there's a conflict with
someone, w hether there should be or

02:42:59.585 --> 02:43:01.795
there shouldn't b e, maybe someone's
being really aggressive and I've

02:43:01.875 --> 02:43:04.014
got to kind of say some things
and try and counter that, right?

02:43:04.275 --> 02:43:06.435
I'm going to be able to
discern one from another.

02:43:06.435 --> 02:43:08.344
I'm going to be more effective in both.

02:43:08.815 --> 02:43:13.935
I just set myself up for success,
and if I'm setting me up for success,

02:43:14.205 --> 02:43:17.275
then I'm also setting you up for
success if you're someone I have a

02:43:17.275 --> 02:43:18.945
relationship with, no matter what it is.

02:43:19.215 --> 02:43:24.055
And then ultimately I'm setting the
we right, the magical bridge of us.

02:43:24.265 --> 02:43:26.105
I'm setting that up for success too.

02:43:26.335 --> 02:43:30.024
And that's how you see in these
levels of emergence, that if you

02:43:30.024 --> 02:43:33.095
understand everything about you and
understand everything about me, and

02:43:33.095 --> 02:43:37.035
let's say a person understands we
have a generative drive, you don't

02:43:37.035 --> 02:43:39.645
really know what our relationship is
going to be like, but you think, I bet

02:43:39.645 --> 02:43:40.795
it's going to be a good one, right?

02:43:40.795 --> 02:43:45.265
And then what would we then
contribute, s ay, to a broader culture?

02:43:45.265 --> 02:43:48.145
So a group of people, maybe there's
10 people, a bunch of friends getting

02:43:48.145 --> 02:43:51.975
together, we would contribute goodness
at that next level, which is then

02:43:51.975 --> 02:43:53.535
the culture o f the larger group.

02:43:54.085 --> 02:43:56.275
Andrew Huberman: In hearing your
description of mentalization, this

02:43:56.275 --> 02:44:01.255
ability for all of us to get into
the mind of another and to try

02:44:01.255 --> 02:44:05.075
and imagine motivations and states
that would explain their behavior--

02:44:05.075 --> 02:44:05.608
Paul Conti: And the self, right?
The mind of self and other.

02:44:07.204 --> 02:44:10.425
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, that seems
like a natural reflex that's healthy.

02:44:10.425 --> 02:44:14.464
And it seems to surface most
often as the consequence of

02:44:14.475 --> 02:44:16.335
negative interactions, right?

02:44:16.605 --> 02:44:17.574
Something didn't go right.

02:44:17.574 --> 02:44:19.665
And so we kind of explore
like, was it them?

02:44:19.665 --> 02:44:20.165
Was it me?

02:44:20.374 --> 02:44:22.095
Was it something that happened before?

02:44:22.745 --> 02:44:26.135
What else is going on with this person
as a way to try and arrive at some sort

02:44:26.135 --> 02:44:27.885
of hopefully generative understanding.

02:44:28.215 --> 02:44:33.055
But it seems to me that there's also
great value in mentalizing about others

02:44:33.525 --> 02:44:37.635
under conditions in which things are
going well, so that one can potentially

02:44:37.735 --> 02:44:41.925
make things or encourage things to go
even better in future interactions.

02:44:41.934 --> 02:44:42.434
Paul Conti: Right.

02:44:42.554 --> 02:44:45.515
Well, the first thing I would
say i s, I think the reflex

02:44:45.875 --> 02:44:49.745
is most often not mentalizing.

02:44:50.075 --> 02:44:53.979
That the reflex is most often
not mentalizing, r ight?

02:44:54.105 --> 02:44:58.774
Because the reflexes often come
from a position of not feeling safe.

02:45:00.045 --> 02:45:00.885
There's some conflict.

02:45:00.885 --> 02:45:02.115
I don't feel good.

02:45:02.115 --> 02:45:05.585
If there's some conflict, people, we
often get very defensive very quickly,

02:45:05.795 --> 02:45:09.305
whether we show it on the outside
or we start partitioning inside.

02:45:09.595 --> 02:45:13.694
And the problems come not from
mentalizing, but they come from

02:45:13.695 --> 02:45:16.875
not mentalizing and not being
aware of the difference, because

02:45:16.875 --> 02:45:20.455
then I conclude, I know what's
going on in you and I don't right.

02:45:20.605 --> 02:45:22.325
Because I don't know
what's going on in me.

02:45:22.575 --> 02:45:23.965
Right.
And I think you're being aggressive.

02:45:23.965 --> 02:45:24.465
Really.

02:45:24.815 --> 02:45:26.125
I'm feeling kind of defensive.

02:45:26.125 --> 02:45:28.975
I'm feeling vulnerable, and
then I'm getting aggressive.

02:45:29.255 --> 02:45:31.665
But I can't handle that because
I don't want to be aggressive.

02:45:31.665 --> 02:45:32.384
So you're aggressive.

02:45:32.845 --> 02:45:38.315
So there's such a difference between, say,
coming at a self other conflict from the

02:45:38.315 --> 02:45:42.325
perspective of, say, not mentalizing, but
thinking that you are versus mentalizing.

02:45:43.625 --> 02:45:46.285
There's a difference between, is it me?

02:45:46.595 --> 02:45:47.495
I know it can't be.

02:45:48.235 --> 02:45:48.984
Is it you?

02:45:49.095 --> 02:45:49.785
It must be.

02:45:50.425 --> 02:45:50.735
Right?

02:45:50.735 --> 02:45:52.615
And that's how a lot of that goes.

02:45:52.735 --> 02:45:55.425
And then, of course, what's the
data t hat's gathered, the data

02:45:55.425 --> 02:45:56.915
that's gathered supports that.

02:45:56.915 --> 02:46:00.355
I mean, I think there's wars I
think have happened based upon

02:46:00.355 --> 02:46:03.045
this, let alone conflicts in
friendships and in relationships.

02:46:03.414 --> 02:46:06.054
The key about mentalizing
is that's not what it is.

02:46:06.055 --> 02:46:06.625
Mentalizing.

02:46:06.625 --> 02:46:07.464
It goes like this.

02:46:08.045 --> 02:46:08.685
Is it me?

02:46:09.265 --> 02:46:10.014
Is it you?

02:46:10.165 --> 02:46:10.844
Is it us?

02:46:11.754 --> 02:46:12.665
Let's figure that out.

02:46:13.645 --> 02:46:14.705
Let's figure it out together.

02:46:16.085 --> 02:46:18.365
It's not defensive, it's not aggressive.

02:46:18.365 --> 02:46:19.245
It's not projecting.

02:46:19.245 --> 02:46:21.885
It's really actually seeing.

02:46:22.085 --> 02:46:25.784
And look, if it's me, I want to be
aware of it and say, yeah, like, whoa,

02:46:25.784 --> 02:46:27.060
I got up on the wrong side of the bed.

02:46:27.305 --> 02:46:28.495
Let me just say I'm sorry, right?

02:46:28.764 --> 02:46:31.165
Or if I might say, look, I know
that you're being aggressive and

02:46:31.165 --> 02:46:34.225
you normally wouldn't be, and say,
look, let me just get away from it?

02:46:34.245 --> 02:46:36.615
Let the person I care about
calm down, come back later.

02:46:37.235 --> 02:46:40.345
Or if it's us, is really something
b etween us, then let's sort it out.

02:46:40.815 --> 02:46:46.314
The information that allows the healthy,
the agency and gratitude decisions

02:46:46.505 --> 02:46:47.505
is always there through mentalizing.

02:46:48.675 --> 02:46:51.505
The danger is when we're not
mentalizing, but we think we are.

02:46:52.285 --> 02:46:52.785
Andrew Huberman: Got it.

02:46:54.425 --> 02:46:57.785
In keeping with thinking about
others and what's going on with them.

02:46:58.485 --> 02:47:01.885
Mentalizing, that is, and in thinking
about what's going on with ourselves and

02:47:01.885 --> 02:47:05.415
the exploration of the cupboards under
the pillars of structure of self, function

02:47:05.415 --> 02:47:09.985
of self and our desire for all of that
to geyser up into agency and gratitude.

02:47:11.045 --> 02:47:15.085
One thing that we hear about so
much these days, and generally I

02:47:15.085 --> 02:47:18.544
think it's good that people are
talking about them, are boundaries.

02:47:19.734 --> 02:47:22.045
You've crossed my boundary,
or I'm setting a boundary.

02:47:22.045 --> 02:47:27.095
And I've certainly embedded the
message in my head that in order

02:47:27.095 --> 02:47:30.235
for certain relationships to be
at their most loving in my life,

02:47:30.335 --> 02:47:34.695
sometimes the boundaries require very
little frequency of communication.

02:47:35.435 --> 02:47:38.055
But that doesn't mean the
relationships aren't ongoing.

02:47:39.435 --> 02:47:41.505
So what are your thoughts on boundaries?

02:47:41.534 --> 02:47:45.245
And how should we think about boundaries
for the sake of healthy relationships?

02:47:45.975 --> 02:47:49.255
Paul Conti: Well, healthy
boundaries always start inside.

02:47:49.655 --> 02:47:53.684
And then once we have them squared away
inside, we can project them outward.

02:47:54.155 --> 02:47:56.285
The same as everything works, right?

02:47:56.285 --> 02:47:59.435
It starts with the self, and
then it includes the other.

02:47:59.665 --> 02:48:03.655
So let's say you took an
example of a friend who's just

02:48:04.425 --> 02:48:05.765
a little too presumptuous.

02:48:05.775 --> 02:48:09.594
Like the kind of person you'd rather k
nock on your door, but who just opens the

02:48:09.595 --> 02:48:11.745
door and comes in, that kind of thing.

02:48:12.155 --> 02:48:14.815
And you like the person a lot, and
there's a lot of resonance, and you

02:48:14.815 --> 02:48:16.295
don't want to lose the friendship.

02:48:16.555 --> 02:48:18.855
Let's imagine what could happen here.

02:48:18.905 --> 02:48:21.675
It's not the only thing that can
happen, but a thought experiment, right?

02:48:22.105 --> 02:48:24.949
So the person may then question
themselves, like, is there

02:48:25.025 --> 02:48:26.105
something wrong with me?

02:48:26.735 --> 02:48:29.365
Because I don't really like this
person coming in my front door.

02:48:29.725 --> 02:48:30.425
Is that just me?

02:48:30.425 --> 02:48:31.724
Am I being weird about that?

02:48:31.725 --> 02:48:34.705
Or the person can kind of stop
and think about that, and they can

02:48:34.705 --> 02:48:38.645
take stock of self, and they can
arrive at an answer for themselves.

02:48:38.645 --> 02:48:41.885
Like, look, that person may
conclude that, you know what?

02:48:42.075 --> 02:48:44.235
People I'm as close to as this
person come in my front door.

02:48:44.235 --> 02:48:44.585
You know what?

02:48:44.585 --> 02:48:45.424
That's okay, right?

02:48:45.624 --> 02:48:46.021
You know what?

02:48:46.021 --> 02:48:47.205
I don't need to set a boundary there.

02:48:47.935 --> 02:48:49.085
That's possible, right?

02:48:49.485 --> 02:48:54.225
More often, what the person concludes
is, no, I don't really want that.

02:48:54.485 --> 02:48:56.961
I do actually want this
person to knock on the door.

02:48:56.961 --> 02:48:57.755
And that's okay.

02:48:58.505 --> 02:48:59.805
There's not something wrong with me.

02:49:00.105 --> 02:49:01.515
It's not that I'm being a jerk.

02:49:01.515 --> 02:49:03.245
It's not that I'm being
a bad friend, right?

02:49:03.445 --> 02:49:04.425
Because what are they saying?

02:49:04.445 --> 02:49:06.565
You might say, oh, they're preparing
for what the other person could

02:49:06.565 --> 02:49:10.934
say, but no, they're countering what
they're testing out to themselves.

02:49:10.934 --> 02:49:12.255
Does that mean I'm a bad friend?

02:49:12.285 --> 02:49:13.135
No, right?

02:49:13.855 --> 02:49:14.405
That kind of thing.

02:49:14.485 --> 02:49:17.555
So they know, and then they
understand, and they have the

02:49:17.615 --> 02:49:19.095
right to set the boundary, right?

02:49:19.095 --> 02:49:20.215
Like, okay, it's my house.

02:49:20.215 --> 02:49:22.785
I try to be generous, but I still
don't want people coming in the door.

02:49:22.785 --> 02:49:27.255
Like, you come to some conclusion
that setting the boundary is okay and

02:49:27.255 --> 02:49:29.265
you're squared away with it inside.

02:49:29.825 --> 02:49:33.135
Then you communicate the boundary
outward, and you decide, how

02:49:33.135 --> 02:49:34.435
do I want to communicate that?

02:49:34.635 --> 02:49:37.495
Like saying, hey, man, don't
come in the front door anymore.

02:49:38.005 --> 02:49:41.645
That's not so good right now, the
friendship is really on the rocks, right?

02:49:41.945 --> 02:49:46.785
But to say to someone something like,
yeah, look, I care about you, and I trust

02:49:46.785 --> 02:49:50.285
you, and I know you feel the same way
about me, but it just makes me nervous

02:49:50.645 --> 02:49:51.935
when people just come in the door.

02:49:52.725 --> 02:49:53.345
Do you mind?

02:49:53.395 --> 02:49:56.445
Is it okay if you just
please knock on the door now?

02:49:56.445 --> 02:49:57.644
Think about how that's been done.

02:49:58.095 --> 02:50:05.144
It's so accurate to what's going on inside
the person, to how the person wants to

02:50:05.145 --> 02:50:10.415
communicate with the other, then you have
the highest likelihood of effectiveness.

02:50:10.505 --> 02:50:15.845
And the person also, in that sense, has
sort of the clean conscience, so to speak,

02:50:15.845 --> 02:50:18.275
that lets them take in information back.

02:50:18.565 --> 02:50:22.255
So let's say the other person hopefully
has a high generative drive and

02:50:22.274 --> 02:50:25.915
all the good things we're talking
about could say, oh, I'm sorry.

02:50:25.915 --> 02:50:28.425
I didn't mean to make
you feel uncomfortable.

02:50:28.425 --> 02:50:31.995
I kind of just automatically do that,
or I always do that at my brother's

02:50:31.995 --> 02:50:33.465
house when it's close by or whatever.

02:50:33.465 --> 02:50:37.315
The person is saying, no worries,
okay, everything is fine.

02:50:37.345 --> 02:50:37.715
Right?

02:50:37.715 --> 02:50:39.525
But let's say that
person does get mad now.

02:50:40.514 --> 02:50:43.154
You know, like, whoa, what's
going on in this person?

02:50:43.154 --> 02:50:46.375
Because what you're doing is you're seeing
signs of unhealth in the other person.

02:50:46.765 --> 02:50:49.695
And it may be that, whoa, that's
not so healthy with the person.

02:50:50.055 --> 02:50:52.675
But I like other aspects of the
friendship, and that seems like

02:50:52.675 --> 02:50:53.345
kind of a strange idiosyncrasy.

02:50:54.495 --> 02:50:56.025
Then you might decide, you know what?

02:50:56.545 --> 02:50:57.564
I want to keep this friendship.

02:50:57.565 --> 02:51:03.355
So I'll start locking the door, and the
person has to knock, or you might think,

02:51:03.655 --> 02:51:08.315
okay, am I seeing something here that's
about lack of consideration, right?

02:51:08.315 --> 02:51:09.415
That's about selfishness.

02:51:09.425 --> 02:51:10.855
That ultimately may be about envy.

02:51:11.005 --> 02:51:12.985
I'm going to walk into your
house and ruin your privacy.

02:51:13.624 --> 02:51:15.204
I'm going to walk into your
house and make you feel anxious.

02:51:15.204 --> 02:51:16.565
I feel entitled to do that.

02:51:16.745 --> 02:51:18.995
The kind of thing that's really
coming through the lens of envy.

02:51:19.285 --> 02:51:22.975
So then you start thinking about
that, and maybe not always.

02:51:22.975 --> 02:51:28.465
Of course, sometimes you find a
friend who can do the right thing.

02:51:28.485 --> 02:51:31.115
Sometimes you find a friend who can't, and
you can still make the friendship work.

02:51:31.295 --> 02:51:35.295
Sometimes you find a person who's not
a friend, and you see, like, oh, that's

02:51:35.295 --> 02:51:37.325
how e verything is with this person.

02:51:37.325 --> 02:51:38.635
You realize, like, there's no us.

02:51:39.475 --> 02:51:40.995
There's no us, right.

02:51:40.995 --> 02:51:45.625
And that's how people can leave
relationships that ultimately are

02:51:45.625 --> 02:51:47.045
exploitive in one way or another.

02:51:47.045 --> 02:51:51.715
Whether it's a friendship, it's a romance,
if a person really understands, there's

02:51:51.715 --> 02:51:53.855
the me, the you, the magical bridge of us.

02:51:53.885 --> 02:51:56.275
And then, wait, there's
no magical bridge of us.

02:51:57.445 --> 02:52:00.605
I feel that there's a magical bridge
of us, but the other person doesn't.

02:52:00.875 --> 02:52:05.325
There's immense power of
understanding and then of appropriate

02:52:05.325 --> 02:52:06.895
self care and self protection.

02:52:08.645 --> 02:52:10.065
Andrew Huberman: Over and over again.

02:52:10.624 --> 02:52:13.555
Not just during today's episode,
but in the previous two episodes.

02:52:13.875 --> 02:52:19.675
It seems that getting squared away
with oneself, or at least one's own

02:52:19.675 --> 02:52:24.464
internal processes to some degree,
gaining some insight as to where the

02:52:24.475 --> 02:52:28.305
negative or even positive emotion
within us is arising when we're in

02:52:28.305 --> 02:52:30.275
relationship to anyone or anything.

02:52:30.614 --> 02:52:32.455
It just seems so key.

02:52:32.515 --> 02:52:33.735
It's like the foundation of it.

02:52:33.985 --> 02:52:42.494
You said getting to the maximum amount of
agency and gratitude is the goal, really.

02:52:42.555 --> 02:52:47.345
And that's done by cycling back
through the cupboards that reside under

02:52:47.345 --> 02:52:48.644
structure of self and function of self.

02:52:48.835 --> 02:52:50.655
And asking questions about oneself.

02:52:51.385 --> 02:52:56.615
And really, unless we are trained
psychiatrists like you, none of

02:52:56.615 --> 02:52:59.435
us really should be doing that
for the other person, it seems.

02:52:59.445 --> 02:53:04.085
This is really each and all of
our own responsibility to do for

02:53:04.085 --> 02:53:08.355
ourselves, but that it serves
others in so many positive ways.

02:53:08.665 --> 02:53:09.165
Paul Conti: Right.

02:53:09.325 --> 02:53:09.725
Right.

02:53:09.725 --> 02:53:13.645
So mentalization greases the
progress of all things good.

02:53:14.325 --> 02:53:18.425
Because it is about actual understanding,
feeling states, intentions.

02:53:18.895 --> 02:53:24.164
But I have to first mentalize about
me before I mentalize about you.

02:53:24.204 --> 02:53:27.785
A way of putting this would be, if I'm
thinking about you before I'm thinking

02:53:27.785 --> 02:53:29.485
about me, I'm on a fool's errand.

02:53:30.635 --> 02:53:32.295
You have to start with the self.

02:53:32.595 --> 02:53:35.425
And to try and attain clarity of self.

02:53:35.635 --> 02:53:37.954
Or to realize that you can't.

02:53:39.275 --> 02:53:41.375
It's a very important nuance.

02:53:41.594 --> 02:53:44.535
If I know that, say there's trauma
in me about a certain thing.

02:53:44.535 --> 02:53:49.085
And I know that I respond in ways
where sometimes my emotions get high.

02:53:49.095 --> 02:53:53.765
And I can't quite think through it
all, which can happen to me with

02:53:54.365 --> 02:53:58.175
references or something that's
encompassing a certain kind of trauma.

02:53:58.524 --> 02:54:00.275
I want to be aware of that, right?

02:54:00.275 --> 02:54:04.785
So this idea of not only being aware
of what you know about yourself, but

02:54:04.795 --> 02:54:07.345
being aware that there are things
you don't know about yourself.

02:54:07.625 --> 02:54:12.234
So if I know enough to know that
a certain kind of interaction

02:54:12.244 --> 02:54:13.624
about a certain kind of trauma.

02:54:13.935 --> 02:54:19.085
Really sets me to a place where I'm
not able to mentalize like I normally

02:54:19.085 --> 02:54:21.365
would be, I'm sort of flying blind.

02:54:21.875 --> 02:54:25.235
Then, good for me to know that, because
I don't know what that's doing to me.

02:54:25.605 --> 02:54:27.715
So it's the last time to make conclusions.

02:54:27.725 --> 02:54:28.965
So let's say I'm in that state.

02:54:29.005 --> 02:54:33.374
Let's say I'm in that state because of
something that just happened, and now

02:54:33.374 --> 02:54:34.824
we have some sort of conflict, right?

02:54:34.824 --> 02:54:37.125
Then for me to realize, like,
well, what's going on in me?

02:54:37.175 --> 02:54:42.765
I'm bringing to this a lack of clarity
because I'm really activated, right.

02:54:42.765 --> 02:54:46.005
In that sense, the anxiety, the
tension in me has been raised.

02:54:46.075 --> 02:54:50.074
The negative emotion, that
reflexive, negative affect, feeling

02:54:50.074 --> 02:54:52.085
emotion has been raised in me.

02:54:52.085 --> 02:54:55.495
And then I realize, like, I don't know
how it's going to go if we interact

02:54:55.514 --> 02:54:57.914
about it now because I can't rely on me.

02:54:58.285 --> 02:55:00.015
So, like, hey, can we
talk about this later?

02:55:00.255 --> 02:55:02.785
The same way if you recognize
in the other that person who's

02:55:02.785 --> 02:55:06.984
usually rational is all worked up,
recognize that in the other, right?

02:55:06.984 --> 02:55:09.574
A nd maybe that person doesn't realize
that they still want to interact.

02:55:09.614 --> 02:55:12.194
And you say, let's revisit this.

02:55:12.194 --> 02:55:14.935
We're both kind of calm,
cool and collected, right?

02:55:15.255 --> 02:55:19.835
So mentalization greases the wheels of
all progress, but like anything else

02:55:19.835 --> 02:55:22.925
has to be deployed in a way that works.

02:55:22.925 --> 02:55:27.405
And the way that works is I start with
me, then I go to you, and then I go to us.

02:55:28.575 --> 02:55:32.289
Andrew Huberman: I'm fully on
board the start with me, then to

02:55:32.385 --> 02:55:35.465
the other, and then to us model.

02:55:35.575 --> 02:55:36.775
It makes total sense.

02:55:36.775 --> 02:55:43.635
In fact, so much so that I'm starting to
build an image of my mind, where first

02:55:43.645 --> 02:55:49.214
is kind of a rule, which is it starts
with self understanding, thoughtful,

02:55:49.224 --> 02:55:52.695
structured inquiry along the lines of
the map that was laid out in episodes

02:55:52.695 --> 02:55:56.895
one and two of this series and that we've
been alluding to numerous times today.

02:55:56.925 --> 02:55:57.425
Paul Conti: Yes.

02:55:57.815 --> 02:56:02.055
Andrew Huberman: And then there's a
second line or rule that I've got written

02:56:02.055 --> 02:56:06.575
on this imaginary piece of paper in my
mind, where anytime I default to thinking

02:56:06.575 --> 02:56:12.245
about another without first going to
my own map and exploring what's going

02:56:12.245 --> 02:56:16.745
on with me, that my own map starts to
become blurry, like I'm losing access to

02:56:16.745 --> 02:56:18.494
it and it potentially could disappear.

02:56:18.525 --> 02:56:21.534
I'm just creating this in my own
mind as a way to create a little

02:56:21.534 --> 02:56:22.795
bit of, I think, healthy anxiety.

02:56:23.285 --> 02:56:24.005
Paul Conti: I like it.

02:56:24.005 --> 02:56:26.925
Andrew Huberman: To really go
there first, paints the picture.

02:56:26.975 --> 02:56:27.475
Paul Conti: Yes.

02:56:27.975 --> 02:56:34.845
Andrew Huberman: And the purpose in having
such an image in my mind, that access

02:56:34.845 --> 02:56:38.775
to the self and understanding of self is
potentially drifting away, is because I

02:56:38.775 --> 02:56:43.105
think, for me, there's a third line in
this rule set that I'm imagining, which

02:56:43.105 --> 02:56:49.135
is that the less understanding we have
of our own map and internal process,

02:56:49.485 --> 02:56:56.214
the more likely I am, or we all are,
to just latch on to an unhealthy map.

02:56:56.675 --> 02:56:58.035
Another unhealthy map.

02:56:58.104 --> 02:56:58.575
Paul Conti: Yes.

02:56:58.575 --> 02:57:01.615
I have to even follow the directions
o f our own unhealthy map.

02:57:02.195 --> 02:57:04.915
Which leads us to someone else's
unhealthy map, and then we

02:57:05.125 --> 02:57:06.525
latch onto it, like you said.

02:57:06.535 --> 02:57:07.535
Yeah, absolutely.

02:57:07.545 --> 02:57:07.915
Yes.

02:57:07.915 --> 02:57:08.335
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, that's right.

02:57:08.335 --> 02:57:11.295
Because it's still active, even
if it's blurry or if it's obscured

02:57:11.295 --> 02:57:13.184
from my awareness and drifting away.

02:57:13.184 --> 02:57:17.065
Paul Conti: So if I'm trying to guide
myself with my broken compass, I run

02:57:17.065 --> 02:57:18.825
into someone else with a broken compass.

02:57:19.115 --> 02:57:22.045
Now we're both wandering, so be aware.

02:57:22.045 --> 02:57:25.495
If the compass isn't working the way
we want it to, can we go look at that?

02:57:26.265 --> 02:57:30.014
Can we make the map healthy, make
the map accurate, make the map

02:57:30.015 --> 02:57:31.294
guide us towards what we want?

02:57:31.305 --> 02:57:34.895
Because then we will find other people
who've worked on their own maps that way.

02:57:35.895 --> 02:57:36.615
Andrew Huberman: Absolutely.

02:57:37.175 --> 02:57:41.625
And so often I hear about, and
frankly, I've experienced this

02:57:41.875 --> 02:57:46.074
feeling like, oh, it's just a matter
of finding somebody who is healthy.

02:57:46.454 --> 02:57:46.725
Right?

02:57:46.725 --> 02:57:49.074
And then things will be
much easier and much better.

02:57:49.105 --> 02:57:50.955
And surely that has to be the case.

02:57:50.994 --> 02:57:56.195
And surely there have to be instances
where people have an interaction

02:57:56.195 --> 02:57:58.595
with somebody that leads to a
relationship with somebody and the

02:57:58.595 --> 02:57:59.805
other person is much healthier.

02:57:59.805 --> 02:58:03.945
And whatever trauma we come to the
relationship with is best supported

02:58:03.945 --> 02:58:06.225
or better supported than it would be
if we were with somebody exploitative

02:58:06.994 --> 02:58:11.445
or who was truly damaged in some
way, who basically had a map that was

02:58:11.445 --> 02:58:14.505
really, I don't want to say screwed
up, but that they had not explored.

02:58:14.515 --> 02:58:15.335
Paul Conti: They need a lot of work.

02:58:15.365 --> 02:58:18.665
Andrew Huberman: They need a lot of
work, but I don't see that very often.

02:58:18.685 --> 02:58:22.795
I definitely see people who, at least
from my outside read, seem to have healthy

02:58:22.805 --> 02:58:27.794
maps or are doing regular exploration
of their maps and therefore healthy.

02:58:27.864 --> 02:58:28.245
Who knows?

02:58:28.245 --> 02:58:32.275
I don't know what they do with
their time in every domain of life.

02:58:32.595 --> 02:58:38.855
But once again, we come back to this
importance of understanding what's

02:58:38.855 --> 02:58:44.344
in those cupboards, what's in those
pillars, as a not just important but

02:58:44.345 --> 02:58:50.235
critical step in understanding and
building ourselves in positive ways.

02:58:50.235 --> 02:58:54.225
And as I said once or twice before in this
series, but I'm going to say it again,

02:58:55.195 --> 02:59:00.465
and I'm sure again and again before we
conclude this series, is that what's

02:59:00.465 --> 02:59:06.164
so attractive about this map is that
it sets a very clear and simple set of

02:59:06.165 --> 02:59:08.595
ideals, not necessarily simple to attain.

02:59:08.595 --> 02:59:13.105
And as you've said, it takes time,
but agency, concept and gratitude,

02:59:13.485 --> 02:59:18.805
empowerment, humility, leading up
to peace, contentment and delight,

02:59:18.815 --> 02:59:24.704
all as action states, not as passive
states to just bask in and disappear.

02:59:25.165 --> 02:59:27.285
And this notion of the generative drive.

02:59:27.285 --> 02:59:31.405
And by now, in this episode, I'm sure
people are well on board the understanding

02:59:31.405 --> 02:59:34.624
that the generative drive is not just
about going out and doing things.

02:59:34.965 --> 02:59:39.654
It's about doing things in service to
and in a way that supports learning,

02:59:39.654 --> 02:59:43.815
knowing, creating, not just of
others and in the world, but inside.

02:59:43.815 --> 02:59:44.315
Paul Conti: Yes.

02:59:45.685 --> 02:59:51.685
I love the map imagery because you can
almost see the map changing, right?

02:59:51.685 --> 02:59:54.555
As the person, I imagine the person
is busying away in the cupboards.

02:59:54.565 --> 02:59:56.535
Oh, there's a lot to
work on in this cupboard.

02:59:56.535 --> 03:00:00.085
And they're busying away and they're doing
the work, and we can see the map changing.

03:00:00.415 --> 03:00:04.325
That path that looked like a really
good path actually goes through a swamp.

03:00:04.865 --> 03:00:05.918
We didn't see the swamp on the map.

03:00:05.995 --> 03:00:07.105
Now it appears on the map.

03:00:07.365 --> 03:00:09.885
That other path that looked
like it's a little circuitous.

03:00:10.195 --> 03:00:10.994
That's a good path.

03:00:11.335 --> 03:00:12.565
It might be a harder path.

03:00:12.574 --> 03:00:15.295
It is a little circuitous, but
look where it leads, right?

03:00:15.635 --> 03:00:20.244
The map becomes clear as we
do the work on ourselves.

03:00:20.745 --> 03:00:21.105
Andrew Huberman: Yes.

03:00:21.105 --> 03:00:24.765
And also the understanding that you've
laid out for us here really helps

03:00:24.765 --> 03:00:29.255
avoid a lot of the common pitfalls that
are associated with sticky language.

03:00:29.675 --> 03:00:30.885
And sticky for good reason.

03:00:30.885 --> 03:00:33.975
I mean, what's stickier and more
interesting for people that are interested

03:00:33.975 --> 03:00:39.015
in themselves, in relationship than
things like boundaries or labels like

03:00:39.025 --> 03:00:40.845
anxious, attached, or secure, attached.

03:00:40.845 --> 03:00:43.795
And I'm not being disparaging of
those labels, but I'm realizing

03:00:43.795 --> 03:00:45.315
those are just labels, right?

03:00:45.645 --> 03:00:52.555
They don't define action items and
specific lines of inquiry to get us back

03:00:52.555 --> 03:00:55.015
into our self understanding over and over.

03:00:55.905 --> 03:00:57.455
Not as a full time job, right?

03:00:57.455 --> 03:01:01.365
We all have to live our lives, but
as a way actually to be more leaned

03:01:01.365 --> 03:01:02.795
into life in the outside world.

03:01:03.175 --> 03:01:08.045
Paul Conti: Those labels define people no
better than the numerical diagnoses in the

03:01:08.045 --> 03:01:11.354
psychiatric taxonomy book that we glorify.

03:01:12.105 --> 03:01:13.925
Labels are not understanding.

03:01:13.935 --> 03:01:15.405
Numbers are not understanding.

03:01:15.655 --> 03:01:16.355
They can help.

03:01:16.425 --> 03:01:17.945
Taxonomies are good.

03:01:17.995 --> 03:01:22.285
Sometimes labels let us categorize
things, but labels are not a

03:01:22.285 --> 03:01:23.825
substitute for understanding.

03:01:24.065 --> 03:01:26.215
Numbers are not a substitute
for understanding.

03:01:26.315 --> 03:01:31.635
If we look at ourselves, we get
real understanding, and that's

03:01:31.635 --> 03:01:32.525
what makes the difference.

03:01:32.525 --> 03:01:36.445
That's what bolsters agency,
gratitude, all those good things.

03:01:36.445 --> 03:01:40.035
Clear mentalization, greasing
the wheels of progress more, the

03:01:40.485 --> 03:01:41.735
generative drive getting stronger.

03:01:42.005 --> 03:01:45.135
We really can find goodness, and often do.

03:01:45.135 --> 03:01:48.174
And that's why I think everything
we're t alking about is very hopeful.

03:01:48.184 --> 03:01:50.195
It acknowledges there's complexity.

03:01:50.195 --> 03:01:51.075
There are pitfalls.

03:01:51.245 --> 03:01:55.415
There are all sorts of things to it that
we need to be aware of and to beware of.

03:01:55.725 --> 03:01:58.994
But that doesn't mean that ultimately
it isn't positive that we're not

03:01:58.994 --> 03:02:03.445
speaking to, hey, whatever it is that's
ailing you, that thing can be better.

03:02:05.034 --> 03:02:07.565
Andrew Huberman: Well, I'm so grateful
that you're sharing your knowledge and

03:02:07.565 --> 03:02:12.755
experience around all of this with us
and that you've laid out such a clear

03:02:13.195 --> 03:02:19.055
and logical and deep and tractable,
really actionable understanding

03:02:19.055 --> 03:02:20.925
of all this that we can engage in.

03:02:20.925 --> 03:02:22.864
It's tremendously powerful.

03:02:22.874 --> 03:02:23.305
Paul Conti: Thank you.

03:02:23.305 --> 03:02:24.235
I so appreciate it.

